/01/us/north-dakota-abortion-law-is-blocked.html', 'snippet': 'A state judge on Wednesday temporarily blocked enforcement of a law requiring doctors who perform abortions to have admitting privileges at nearby hospitals.', 'lead_paragraph': 'A state judge on Wednesday temporarily blocked enforcement of a law requiring doctors who perform abortions to have admitting privileges at nearby hospitals. The law is similar to measures promoted by anti-abortion groups and blocked by courts in other states, including Mississippi and Alabama. It would have forced North Dakota’s only abortion center, the Red River Clinic in Fargo, to close because it uses out-of-state doctors who have been refused admitting privileges at local hospitals. Medical experts say such requirements do not improve patient safety since hospitals will accept and treat emergency patients regardless. The North Dakota lawsuit that resulted in Wednesday’s temporary injunction was brought on behalf of the clinic by the Center for Reproductive Rights, based in New York.', 'print_section': 'A', 'print_page': '13', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Abortion Law Is Blocked in North Dakota', 'kicker': 'National Briefing | Plains', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'North Dakota: Abortion Law Is Blocked', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'North Dakota', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Eckholm, Erik', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'ABORTION', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Center for Reproductive Rights', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Suits and Litigation (Civil)', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2013-08-01T03:54:28+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'National', 'section_name': 'U.S.', 'byline': {'original': 'By Erik Eckholm', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Erik', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Eckholm', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Brief', '_id': 'nyt://article/ebb00454-be0c-5b94-9b06-ebb880109ce4', 'word_count': 121, 'uri': 'nyt://article/ebb00454-be0c-5b94-9b06-ebb880109ce4'}, {'abstract': 'The Dodd-Frank law and other financial reforms represent regulatory progress but failed to go far enough in reining in the biggest banks, an economist writes.', 'web_url': 'https://economix.blogs.nytimes.com/2013/08/01/sadly-too-big-to-fail-is-not-over/', 'snippet': 'The Dodd-Frank law and other financial reforms represent regulatory progress but failed to go far enough in reining in the biggest banks, an economist writes.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Simon Johnson, former chief economist of the International Monetary Fund, is the Ronald A. Kurtz Professor of Entrepreneurship at the M.I.T. Sloan School of Management and co-author of “White House Burning: The Founding Fathers, Our National Debt, and Why It Matters to You.”', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Sadly, Too Big to Fail Is Not Over', 'kicker': 'Economix', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Banking and Financial Institutions', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Dodd-Frank Wall Street Reform and Consumer Protection Act (2010)', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Regulation and Deregulation of Industry', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Subprime Mortgage Crisis', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Dodd, Christopher J', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Frank, Barney', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Lew, Jacob J', 'rank': 7, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Federal Reserve System', 'rank': 8, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2013-08-01T04:01:39+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Business Day', 'subsection_name': 'Economy', 'byline': {'original': 'By Simon Johnson', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Simon', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Johnson', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/ad74fba4-f061-5f1a-955f-e39409f64fae', 'word_count': 1690, 'uri': 'nyt://article/ad74fba4-f061-5f1a-955f-e39409f64fae'}, {'abstract': 'I recently found myself at DeathClock.com, “the Internet’s friendly reminder that life is slipping away … second by second.” Then, not allowing myself to wallow one grain of sand longer, I decided to quit my day job.', 'web_url': 'https://well.blogs.nytimes.com/2013/08/01/the-tick-tock-of-the-death-clock/', 'snippet': 'I recently found myself at DeathClock.com, “the Internet’s friendly reminder that life is slipping away … second by second.” Then, not allowing myself to wallow one grain of sand longer, I decided to quit my day job.', 'lead_paragraph': 'With every new silver hair sprouting from my scalp, I can’t help but think of the shortening arc ahead of me. Now in my mid-50s, for the first time I’m no longer looking up, over and beyond. Rather, my trajectory points downward at the approaching horizon. In this frame of mind, I recently found myself at DeathClock.com, the “Internet’s friendly” — if not scientific — “reminder that life is slipping away … second by second.” After I completed the short questionnaire, the Death Clock’s algorithm quickly did the math, concluding: “Your personal day of death is Wednesday, April 23, 2031.”', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/08/01/health/01well_petrow2/01well_petrow2-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 467, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'image