iginal': '', 'person': [], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Slideshow', '_id': 'nyt://slideshow/baade8b6-8e30-5f83-8e72-90887d1e36db', 'word_count': 0, 'uri': 'nyt://slideshow/baade8b6-8e30-5f83-8e72-90887d1e36db'}, {'abstract': 'The menu name-checks Sichuan and Manchuria, but the food plays up their differences rather than seeking common ground.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2013/08/07/dining/reviews/hungry-city-spice-symphony-in-midtown-east.html', 'snippet': 'The menu name-checks Sichuan and Manchuria, but the food plays up their differences rather than seeking common ground.', 'lead_paragraph': 'The scarlet sauce on a hillock of crispy, half-broken noodles is, ostensibly, Chinese sweet-and-sour. It is easy to pinpoint the familiar notes of soy, sugar and vinegar, but how to explain the steady, rippling bass line of chile heat? Squeeze of lime, whiff of cilantro, streaks of sour mango powder and black, sulfur-tinged salt: the balance tips toward tanginess and betrays the dish’s true roots, in India.', 'print_section': 'D', 'print_page': '6', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/08/07/dining/07HUNGRY_SPAN/07HUNGRY_SPAN-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 400, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2013/08/07/dining/07HUNGRY_SPAN/07HUNGRY_SPAN-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 400}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/08/07/dining/07HUNGRY_SPAN/07HUNGRY_SPAN-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 683, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/08/07/dining/07HUNGRY_SPAN/07HUNGRY_SPAN-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 1365, 'width': 2048, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/08/07/dining/07HUNGRY_SPAN/07HUNGRY_SPAN-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2013/08/07/dining/07HUNGRY_SPAN/07HUNGRY_SPAN-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/08/07/dining/07HUNGRY_SPAN/07HUNGRY_SPAN-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'The Marriage of Indian and Chinese Cuisines', 'kicker': 'Hungry City', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Culinary Hyphenation', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Restaurants', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Midtown Area (Manhattan, NY)', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Spice Symphony (Manhattan, NY, Restaurant)', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2013-08-01T18:02:03+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Dining', 'section_name': 'Food', 'byline': {'original': 'By Ligaya Mishan', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Ligaya', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Mishan', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Review', '_id': 'nyt://article/42a64cf5-c7b9-53af-839b-1a0ead7cc868', 'word_count': 663, 'uri': 'nyt://article/42a64cf5-c7b9-53af-839b-1a0ead7cc868'}, {'abstract': 'A reader wonders if The Times benefits in any way when specific brands show up in articles.', 'web_url': 'https://publiceditor.blogs.nytimes.com/2013/08/01/perfectly-reasonable-question-no-4-on-mentioning-brand-names/', 'snippet': 'A reader wonders if The Times benefits in any way when specific brands show up in articles.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Dan Goldberg, who identifies himself as the user of an Android phone, thinks The Times is giving Apple some free publicity, and he doesn’t like it.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Perfectly Reasonable Question No. 4: On Mentioning Brand Names', 'kicker': 'The Public Editor&#039;s Journal', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'New York Times', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2013-08-01T18:02:49+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Opinion', 'byline': {'original': 'By Margaret Sullivan', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Margaret', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Sullivan', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/9eb1c931-b3b6-5e8f-9770-eb544053044a', 'word_count': 379, 'uri': 'nyt://article/9eb1c931-b3b6-5e8f-9770-eb544053044a'}, {'abstract': 'Rating agencies are in a funny market. To stay in business, they must appear credible to investors. But so long as that criterion is met, the agency that grades the easiest will make the most money.', 'web_url': 'https://economix.blogs.nytimes.com/2013/08/01/pick-your-own-judge/', 'snippet': 'Rating agencies are in a funny market. To stay in business, they must appear credible to investors. But so long as that criterion is met, the agency that grades the easiest will make the most money.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Nathaniel Popper’s excellent article on rating agencies today — demonstrating that Standard & Poor’s seems to have improved its market share in the rating of commercial real estate securitizations by lowering its standards — points to what a strange market the ratings agencies are in.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Pick Your Own Judge', 'kicker': 'Economix', 'content_kic