with captive chimpanzees, an enterprise that has helped save millions of human lives, is being dismantled. The National Institutes of Health, citing a reduced need for chimps in research as well as their “likeness to humans,” recently decreed that all but 50 of the 451 chimps that are federally owned or supported will be retired and moved to sanctuaries. ', 'print_section': 'A', 'print_page': '21', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/08/02/opinion/0802OPEDbyers/0802OPEDbyers-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 537, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2013/08/02/opinion/0802OPEDbyers/0802OPEDbyers-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 537}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/08/02/opinion/0802OPEDbyers/0802OPEDbyers-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 768, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/08/02/opinion/0802OPEDbyers/0802OPEDbyers-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 1536, 'width': 2048, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/08/02/opinion/0802OPEDbyers/0802OPEDbyers-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2013/08/02/opinion/0802OPEDbyers/0802OPEDbyers-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/08/02/opinion/0802OPEDbyers/0802OPEDbyers-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Apes Need Vaccines, Too', 'kicker': 'Op-Ed Contributor', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Apes Need Vaccines, Too', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Monkeys and Apes', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'VandeBerg, John L', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Cruelty to Animals', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Vaccination and Immunization', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Research', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'National Institutes of Health', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2013-08-02T00:57:16+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'OpEd', 'section_name': 'Opinion', 'byline': {'original': 'By John L. VandeBerg', 'person': [{'firstname': 'John', 'middlename': 'L.', 'lastname': 'VandeBerg', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Op-Ed', '_id': 'nyt://article/5f306f33-157d-595d-bae4-71caf99da2bc', 'word_count': 821, 'uri': 'nyt://article/5f306f33-157d-595d-bae4-71caf99da2bc'}, {'abstract': 'Republican lawmakers have a bold plan for summer recess: staging fake meetings with prescreened, softball questions.', 'web_url': 'https://opinionator.blogs.nytimes.com/2013/08/01/saboteurs-in-the-potato-salad/', 'snippet': 'Republican lawmakers have a bold plan for summer recess: staging fake meetings with prescreened, softball questions.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Just now, a cell of several hundred people has been dispatched into the American summer, to picnics, town halls, radio stations, hospitals and Little League playing fields, with a mission to derail the economic recovery and drum up support for sabotaging federal law. They’re not terrorists, nor are they agents of a foreign government. This is your United States Congress, the Republican House, on recess for the next five weeks.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Saboteurs in the Potato Salad', 'kicker': 'Timothy Egan', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'United States Politics and Government', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Republican Party', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2013-08-02T01:00:01+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Opinion', 'byline': {'original': 'By Timothy Egan', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Timothy', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Egan', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/90db58a3-2a4c-55f3-83b8-f7ce74520d11', 'word_count': 865, 'uri': 'nyt://article/90db58a3-2a4c-55f3-83b8-f7ce74520d11'}, {'abstract': 'The United States must move now on substantive steps to curb climate change, at home and internationally.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2013/08/02/opinion/a-republican-case-for-climate-action.html', 'snippet': 'The United States must move now on substantive steps to curb climate change, at home and internationally.', 'lead_paragraph': 'EACH of us took turns over the past 43 years running the Environmental Protection Agency. We served Republican presidents, but we have a message that transcends political affiliation: the United States must move now on substantive steps to curb climate change, at home and internationally. ', 'print_section': 'A', 'print_page': '21', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'A Republican Case for Climate Action', 'kicker': 'Op-Ed Contributors', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'A Republican Case for Climate Action', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Ruckelshaus, William D', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', '