ate': '2013-08-02T01:07:37+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Weekend', 'section_name': 'Movies', 'byline': {'original': 'By Nicolas Rapold', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Nicolas', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Rapold', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Review', '_id': 'nyt://article/d4bad10d-8534-5225-aad7-32b6795ac393', 'word_count': 281, 'uri': 'nyt://article/d4bad10d-8534-5225-aad7-32b6795ac393'}, {'abstract': 'President Obama’s corporate tax reform proposal would solve some problems and create others.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2013/08/02/opinion/jobs-and-taxes.html', 'snippet': 'President Obama’s corporate tax reform proposal would solve some problems and create others.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Job creation and tax reform are two major challenges that have gone unmet as Congressional Republicans have serially obstructed the Obama administration’s agenda.', 'print_section': 'A', 'print_page': '20', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Jobs and Taxes', 'kicker': 'Editorial', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Jobs and Taxes', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Corporate Taxes', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Labor and Jobs', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Republican Party', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Obama, Barack', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'United States Politics and Government', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'United States Economy', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Editorials', 'rank': 7, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2013-08-02T01:08:48+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Editorial', 'section_name': 'Opinion', 'byline': {'original': 'By The Editorial Board', 'person': [], 'organization': 'The Editorial Board'}, 'type_of_material': 'Editorial', '_id': 'nyt://article/5a8293d1-302a-5c83-aeb5-ef85e7a47cab', 'word_count': 563, 'uri': 'nyt://article/5a8293d1-302a-5c83-aeb5-ef85e7a47cab'}, {'abstract': 'The Fair Sentencing Act has had many benefits, including reducing the disparity in the way courts punished crack cocaine offenses versus powder.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2013/08/02/opinion/sentencing-reform-starts-to-pay-off.html', 'snippet': 'The Fair Sentencing Act has had many benefits, including reducing the disparity in the way courts punished crack cocaine offenses versus powder.', 'lead_paragraph': 'In 2010, Congress passed the Fair Sentencing Act, which reduced the vast disparity in the way the federal courts punish crack versus powder cocaine offenses. Instead of treating 100 grams of cocaine the same as 1 gram of crack for sentencing purposes, the law cut the ratio to 18 to 1. Initially, the law applied only to future offenders, but, a year later, the United States Sentencing Commission voted to apply it retroactively. Republicans raged, charging that crime would go up and that prisoners would overwhelm the courts with frivolous demands for sentence reductions. Senator Charles Grassley of Iowa said the commission was pursuing “a liberal agenda at all costs.” ', 'print_section': 'A', 'print_page': '20', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Sentencing Reform Starts to Pay Off', 'kicker': 'Editorial', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Sentencing Reform Starts to Pay Off', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Cocaine and Crack Cocaine', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Mandatory Sentencing', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Sentences (Criminal)', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Drug Abuse and Traffic', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Prisons and Prisoners', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Editorials', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2013-08-02T01:08:48+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Editorial', 'section_name': 'Opinion', 'byline': {'original': 'By The Editorial Board', 'person': [], 'organization': 'The Editorial Board'}, 'type_of_material': 'Editorial', '_id': 'nyt://article/f7118ecc-43d6-5213-b637-1aa295106037', 'word_count': 375, 'uri': 'nyt://article/f7118ecc-43d6-5213-b637-1aa295106037'}, {'abstract': 'Connecticut officials have repeatedly postponed the report of the shooting at Newtown, while critical questions remain unanswered.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2013/08/02/opinion/the-slow-motion-inquiry-into-sandy-hook.html', 'snippet': 'Connecticut officials have repeatedly postponed the report of the shooting at Newtown, while critical questions remain unanswered.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Connecticut officials continue to keep the public in the dark about the state’s official investigation into the shooting massacre at Sandy Hook Elementary School in December, even as state and local police have been discussing it at forums across the nation, sometimes in graphic detail. Though the fullest possible report was promised after the tragedy, its release has been repeatedly postponed, with authorities advising patience while the public grows increasingly puzzled. ', 'print_section': 'A', 'print_page': '20', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'The Slow Motion Inquiry Into Sandy Hook', 'kicker': 'Editorial', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'The Slow Motion Inquiry Into Sandy Hook', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Newtown, Conn, Shooting (2012)', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Connecticut', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Editorials', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Malloy, Dannel P', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2013-08-02T01:08:50+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Editorial', 'section_name': 'Opinion', 'byline': {'original': 'By The Editorial Board', 'person': [], 'organization': 'The Editorial Board'}, 'type_of_material': 'Editorial', '_id': 'nyt://article/062fc9c5-f389-559a-bd6a-5f6bda5d7a1e', 'word_count': 285, 'uri': 'nyt://article/062fc9c5-f389-559a-bd6a-5f6bda5d7a1e'}, {'abstract': 'Environmental regulators must adopt strong rules to reduce the risk of accidents and spills.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2013/08/02/opinion/mr-obama-moves-on-chemical-plant-safety.html', 'snippet': 'Environmental regulators must adopt strong rules to reduce the risk of accidents and spills.', 'lead_paragraph': 'President Obama took an important step toward making America’s chemical plants safer on Thursday by directing federal agencies to update regulations, coordinate policies and share information with one another and state and local governments. If followed up by strong rules, his order could go a long way toward protecting millions of Americans who live near chemical plants from disasters like the explosion at a Texas fertilizer plant in April that killed 15 people. Across the country, more than 2,500 facilities produce, use or store toxic chemicals that put more than 10,000 people at risk.', 'print_section': 'A', 'print_page': '20', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Mr. Obama Moves on Chemical Plant Safety', 'kicker': 'Editorial', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Mr. Obama Moves on Chemical Plant Safety', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Chemicals', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Obama, Barack', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Environmental Protection Agency', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Factories and Manufacturing', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Regulation and Deregulation of Industry', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Editorials', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2013-08-02T01:09:19+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Editorial', 'section_name': 'Opinion', 'byline': {'original': 'By The Editorial Board', 'person': [], 'organization': 'The Editorial Board'}, 'type_of_material': 'Editorial', '_id': 'nyt://article/1251ff07-1877-5c60-984b-4508d332bf6d', 'word_count': 351, 'uri': 'nyt://article/1251ff07-1877-5c60-984b-4508d332bf6d'}, {'abstract': 'The dispute over Edward J. Snowden is less a singular sore point between the United States and Russia than a symptom of a relationship that has soured across the board.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2013/08/02/us/politics/obamas-fall-moscow-trip-is-even-more-in-doubt.html', 'snippet': 'The dispute over Edward J. Snowden is less a singular sore point between the United States and Russia than a symptom of a relationship that has soured across the board.', 'lead_paragraph': 'WASHINGTON — President Obama is even less likely to go through with a visit to Moscow this fall after Russia’s decision on Thursday to grant Edward J. Snowden temporary asylum. For Mr. Obama, though, the Snowden affair is only one of myriad reasons to beg off the scheduled meeting with President Vladimir V. Putin. ', 'print_section': 'A', 'print_page': '3', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/08/02/world/relations/relations-articleLarge-v3.jpg', 'height': 400, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2013/08/02/world/relations/relations-articleLarge-v3.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 400}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/08/02/world/relations/relations-jumbo-v3.jpg', 'height': 683,