r', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/b7fb4486-224a-5d4b-8222-3dd00c0a6228', 'word_count': 211, 'uri': 'nyt://article/b7fb4486-224a-5d4b-8222-3dd00c0a6228'}, {'abstract': 'This number of “involuntary part-timers” in the federal government jumped in July, to 199,000, nearly four times the number in recent years.', 'web_url': 'https://economix.blogs.nytimes.com/2013/08/02/skyrocketing-numbers-of-federal-workers-on-forced-leave/', 'snippet': 'This number of “involuntary part-timers” in the federal government jumped in July, to 199,000, nearly four times the number in recent years.', 'lead_paragraph': 'The latest jobs report showed that federal workers didn’t lose jobs in July, leading to some suggestions that the sequester hasn’t affected government employment. But the overall federal government jobs total doesn’t capture furloughs — that is, forced unpaid leave — because people who are being furloughed for say, one day a week, are technically still counted as being employed.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Skyrocketing Numbers of Federal Workers on Forced Leave', 'kicker': 'Economix', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Government Employees', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Labor and Jobs', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Layoffs and Job Reductions', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Working Hours', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2013-08-02T16:04:32+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Business Day', 'subsection_name': 'Economy', 'byline': {'original': 'By Catherine Rampell', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Catherine', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Rampell', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'repo