'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/78fe121f-956a-5029-ac0c-a01865c55eaf', 'word_count': 344, 'uri': 'nyt://article/78fe121f-956a-5029-ac0c-a01865c55eaf'}, {'abstract': 'A paddle as a literary relic? Yes, the one used by the writer John Graves, who died on Wednesday. It’s from a trip he made famous in “Goodbye to a River.”', 'web_url': 'https://artsbeat.blogs.nytimes.com/2013/08/02/the-best-little-literary-paddle-in-texas/', 'snippet': 'A paddle as a literary relic? Yes, the one used by the writer John Graves, who died on Wednesday. It’s from a trip he made famous in “Goodbye to a River.”', 'lead_paragraph': 'John Graves, an author revered in Texas but relatively unknown beyond the borders of the state, died on Wednesday at 92. The New York Times obituary discusses his best-known work, “Goodbye to a River,” published in 1960. That book tells the story of a three-week trip down the Brazos River, from just below Possum Kingdom Lake to a spot near the town of Glen Rose, near where Mr. Graves built his home, which he called Hard Scrabble. ', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/08/02/arts/02artsbeat-graves/02artsbeat-graves-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 402, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2013/08/02/arts/02artsbeat-graves/02artsbeat-graves-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 402}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/08/02/arts/02artsbeat-graves/02artsbeat-graves-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 686, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/08/02/arts/02artsbeat-graves/02artsbeat-graves-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 1259, 'wi