ual-treatment-to-same-sex-spouses-visa-applications.html', 'snippet': 'The secretary of state said the change, which takes effect immediately, is the result of a review after the Supreme Court’s decision on the Defense of Marriage Act.', 'lead_paragraph': 'LONDON — The United States will treat the visa applications of same-sex married couples in the same manner as opposite-sex spouses, Secretary of State John Kerry said Friday, announcing a policy change set in motion in June, after the Supreme Court struck down a law against same-sex marriage.', 'print_section': 'A', 'print_page': '8', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'New U.S. Policy Gives Equal Treatment to Same-Sex Spouses’ Visa Applications', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'New U.S. Policy Gives Equal Treatment to Same-Sex Spouses’ Visa Applications', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Same-Sex Marriage, Civil Unions and Domestic Partnerships', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Gordon, Michael R', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Visas', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'State Department', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Kerry, John', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Immigration and Emigration', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2013-08-02T16:34:18+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Foreign', 'section_name': 'World', 'byline': {'original': 'By Michael R. Gordon', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Michael', 'middlename': 'R.', 'lastname': 'Gordon', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/d9c95d1b-be39-53b9-b0c8-0410f4e50433', 'word_count': 438, 'uri': 'nyt://article/d9c95d1b-be39-53b9-b0c8-0410f4e50433'}, {'abstract': 'The unique texture of classic gazpacho comes from bread, but in these five varieties, you won’t miss it.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2013/08/05/health/gazpachos-minus-the-bread.html', 'snippet': 'The unique texture of classic gazpacho comes from bread, but in these five varieties, you won’t miss it.', 'lead_paragraph': 'For years I insisted that gazpacho can’t be gazpacho if it doesn’t have bread as one of its ingredients. After all, bread is what gives classic gazpacho its unique texture. But this week I decided to forget about the bread and focus in on the combination of tomatoes, vinegar, olive oil, onion and garlic that is really behind the signature flavor of gazpacho. The new Spanish chefs have been reinterpreting this lively, low-calorie soup for over a decade now, so I felt free to experiment, too.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/08/03/health/05recipehealth/05recipehealth-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 397, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2013/08/03/health/05recipehealth/05recipehealth-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 397}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/08/03/health/05recipehealth/05recipehealth-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 678, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/08/03/health/05recipehealth/05recipehealth-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 1355, 'width': 2048, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/08/03/health/05recipehealth/05recipehealth-thumbStandard-v2.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2013/08/03/health/05recipehealth/05recipehealth-thumbStandard-v2.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/08/03/health/05recipehealth/05recipehealth-thumbLarge-v2.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Gazpachos Minus the Bread', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Medicine and Health', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Diet and Nutrition', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Recipes', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2013-08-02T16:39:20+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Science', 'section_name': 'Health', 'byline': {'original': 'By Martha Rose Shulman', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Martha', 'middlename': 'Rose', 'lastname': 'Shulman', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'recipe', '_id': 'nyt://article/33a94ae0-a140-5ff7-8fee-ba86ce100498', 'word_count': 595, 'uri': 'nyt://article/33a94ae0-a140-5ff7-8fee-ba86ce100498'}, {'abstract': 'Tips for Microsoft Outlook and using Java on Firefox.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2013/08/02/technology/personaltech/forwarding-multiple-messages-in-outlook.html', 'snippet': 'Tips for Microsoft Outlook and using Java on Firefox.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Q. Instead of forwarding messages one by one in Outlook 2010, is there a way to send a bunch of separate e-mail messages as one big message?', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Forwarding Multiple Messages in Outlook', 'kicker': 'Q&A', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Computers and the Internet', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Computer Security', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'E-Mail', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2013-08-02T16:43:44+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Business', 'section_name': 'Technology', 'subsection_name': 'Personal Tech', 'byline': {'original': 'By J. D. Biersdorfer', 'person': [{'firstname': 'J.', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': '', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Question', '_id': 'nyt://article/39f9793a-2e8e-5e35-bd38-db01a450dc2c', 'word_count': 448, 'uri': 'nyt://article/39f9793a-2e8e-5e35-bd38-db01a450dc2c'}, {'abstract': 'Ulan Bator, the capital, is booming, offering tourists a crowded and charismatic entry point to the world’s least densely populated country.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2013/08/04/travel/in-mongolia-the-skyline-by-the-steppes.html', 'snippet': 'Ulan Bator, the capital, is booming, offering tourists a crowded and charismatic entry point to the world’s least densely populated country.', 'lead_paragraph': 'In September the hills around Ulan Bator, the rough and roiling capital of Mongolia, are flecked with wildflowers. Below them, districts of gers, or yurts, encircle the city. The circular canvas structures, insulated with felt and supported by poles, have housed central Asian nomads since before the time of Genghis Khan — before there even was a Mongolia. Now, half of the nation’s population lives permanently in or near the capital. Look beyond the gers, to where the offices and condominiums rise, and you’ll see why: Ulan Bator is booming. Construction cranes spike the sky. Traffic — Hummers and Lexuses, jacked-up jalopies and crammed city buses — churns. It’s impossible to ignore the economic surge that copper, gold and coal mining have brought to the city in recent years.', 'print_section': 'TR', 'pri