tract': 'Some notable people at The Times share their favorite reading experience of the year.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2013/08/04/books/review/so-far-so-good.html', 'snippet': 'Some notable people at The Times share their favorite reading experience of the year.', 'lead_paragraph': 'With the year more than half over and sweltering weather encouraging us to stay inside and read, I asked some notable people at The Times for their favorite book (so far) of 2013. Unsurprisingly, some busy respondents are still catching up on 2012. Mark Thompson, C.E.O. of The New York Times Company, cited Robert Macfarlane’s perambulating travel book “The Old Ways.” Thompson praised it for “rooting the immediate felt experience of walking through a landscape in the deep context of culture and history. I’m an inveterate walker myself — now, thanks to Macfarlane, I know why I do it.”', 'print_section': 'BR', 'print_page': '4', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/08/04/books/04openbook/04openbook-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 213, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2013/08/04/books/04openbook/04openbook-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 213}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/08/04/books/04openbook/04openbook-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 364, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/08/04/books/04openbook/04openbook-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 373, 'width': 1049, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/08/04/books/04openbook/04openbook-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2013/08/04/books/04openbook/04openbook-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/08/04/books/04openbook/04openbook-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'So Far, So Good', 'kicker': 'Open Book', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'So Far, So Good', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Books and Literature', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2013-08-02T17:08:07+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'BookReview', 'section_name': 'Books', 'subsection_name': 'Book Review', 'byline': {'original': 'By John Williams', 'person': [{'firstname': 'John', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Williams', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/2a628c33-46e4-5411-a8ae-a135755176da', 'word_count': 393, 'uri': 'nyt://article/2a628c33-46e4-5411-a8ae-a135755176da'}, {'abstract': 'A 20-year search for a beloved children’s story.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2013/08/04/books/review/one-book-in.html', 'snippet': 'A 20-year search for a beloved children’s story.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Last year in the pages of the Book Review, Drew Gilpin Faust described her elation at finding her favorite childhood book in a rare-book store in London, after she had searched for it for decades. In the old days, devotion to such a quest would often fail, filed in a card catalog under “Unrequited Love.” But just as the Internet reunites old lovers, so too can it reunite readers with other fugitive objects of love, like the books of childhood, usually with happier outcomes. The Internet’s shared secrets, though, may also keep some grails tantalizingly out of reach, even as they are in sight.', 'print_section': 'BR', 'print_page': '26', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/08/04/books/review/0804-bks-Eicher/0804-bks-Eicher-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 604, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2013/08/04/books/review/0804-bks-Eicher/0804-bks-Eicher-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 604}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/08/04/books/review/0804-bks-Eicher/0804-bks-Eicher-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 1024, 'width': 1019, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/08/04/books/review/0804-bks-Eicher/0804-bks-Eicher-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 1197, 'width': 1190, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/08/04/books/review/0804-bks-Eicher/0804-bks-Eicher-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2013/08/04/books/review/0804-bks-Eicher/0804-bks-Eicher-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/08/04/books/review/0804-bks-Eicher/0804-bks-Eicher-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'One Book In . . .', 'kicker': 'Essay', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'One Book In . . .', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Books and Literature', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2013-08-02T17:08:32+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'BookReview', 'section_name': 'Books', 'subsection_name': 'Book Review', 'byline': {'original': 'By Terry Eicher', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Terry', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Eicher', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/559a7605-8d0d-538c-9773-ec01964e9f5d', 'word_count': 1263, 'uri': 'nyt://article/559a7605-8d0d-538c-9773-ec01964e9f5d'}, {'abstract': 'Culling overcrowded shelves is never easy.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2013/08/04/books/review/one-book-out.html', 'snippet': 'Culling overcrowded shelves is never easy.', 'lead_paragraph': 'I’ve decided that books are my enemy, though they used to be my great love. They are taking over. They crowd my dining room, they double up in the bedroom, they make the attic floor sag. We even have a library in the bathroom: shelves and shelves of books where a normal person might have a vanity table or piles of towels. ', 'print_section': 'BR', 'print_page': '27', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/08/04/books/review/0804-bks-WILENTZ/0804-bks-WILENTZ-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 210, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2013/08/04/books/review/0804-bks-WILENTZ/0804-bks-WILENTZ-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 210}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/08/04/books/review/0804-bks-WILENTZ/0804-bks-WILENTZ-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 359, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url':