tion list, makes clear that her off-screen life has been anything but prim.', 'lead_paragraph': 'COME ON GET HAPPY: The singer and actress Shirley Jones is best known for playing young innocents (Laurey in “Oklahoma!”), reserved prigs (Marian the librarian in “The Music Man”) and wholesome matriarchs (Shirley Partridge in “The Partridge Family”).', 'print_section': 'BR', 'print_page': '22', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/08/04/books/review/04shirley-jones/04shirley-jones-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 450, 'width': 454, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/08/04/books/review/04shirley-jones/04shirley-jones-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 450, 'width': 454, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/08/04/books/review/04shirley-jones/04list-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2013/08/04/books/review/04shirley-jones/04list-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/08/04/books/review/04shirley-jones/04list-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Inside the List', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'TBR: Inside the List', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Books and Literature', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2013-08-02T17:09:54+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'BookReview', 'section_name': 'Books', 'subsection_name': 'Book Review', 'byline': {'original': 'By Gregory Cowles', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Gregory', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Cowles', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/d7ef984f-177f-5b71-a39a-f2a0d51532a9', 'word_count': 605, 'uri': 'nyt://article/d7ef984f-177f-5b71-a39a-f2a0d51532a9'}, {'abstract': 'Paperback books of particular interest.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2013/08/04/books/review/paperback-row.html', 'snippet': 'Paperback books of particular interest.', 'lead_paragraph': 'THE CASUAL VACANCY,  by J. K. Rowling. (Back Bay/Little, Brown, $18.) Rowling’s first post-“Harry Potter” novel, a dissection of middle-class life in an outwardly idyllic English town, displays her keen powers of observation. After the sudden death of a parish councilor, a vicious political battle erupts over plans to redraw municipal boundaries and possibly shunt a nearby housing project to a different district.', 'print_section': 'BR', 'print_page': '24', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Paperback Row', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Paperback Row', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Books and Literature', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2013-08-02T17:10:20+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'BookReview', 'section_name': 'Books', 'subsection_name': 'Book Review', 'byline': {'original': 'By Ihsan Taylor', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Ihsan', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Taylor', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'List', '_id': 'nyt://article/ea1f08e8-fdd6-5d34-9d2e-2252a742b5e7', 'word_count': 496, 'uri': 'nyt://article/ea1f08e8-fdd6-5d34-9d2e-2252a742b5e7'}, {'abstract': 'Recently reviewed books of particular interest.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2013/08/04/books/review/editors-choice.html', 'snippet': 'Recently reviewed books of particular interest.', 'lead_paragraph': 'THOSE ANGRY DAYS: Roosevelt, Lindbergh, and America’s Fight Over World War II, 1939-1941,  by Lynne Olson. (Random House, $30.) The savage political dispute between Roosevelt and the isolationist movement, presented in spellbinding detail.', 'print_section': 'BR', 'print_page': '22', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Editors’ Choice', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Editors’ Choice | Recent Books of Particular Interest', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Books and Literature', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2013-08-02T17:10:27+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'BookReview', 'section_name': 'Books', 'subsection_name': 'Book Review', 'byline': {'original': '', 'person': [], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'List', '_id': 'nyt://article/b0ce4756-c957-59bc-90ef-fc49ac38635c', 'word_count': 269, 'uri': 'nyt://article/b0ce4756-c957-59bc-90ef-fc49ac38635c'}, {'abstract': 'Some of the decline in the unemployment rate may be attributable to baby boomers’ reaching the end of their working years. But the picture remains weak for those in their prime.', 'web_url': 'https://economix.blogs.nytimes.com/2013/08/02/the-age-equation-in-employment-numbers/', 'snippet': 'Some of the decline in the unemployment rate may be attributable to baby boomers’ reaching the end of their working years. But the picture remains weak for those in their prime.', 'lead_paragraph': 'The rosiest take on the jobs report is that the aging of the country’s population has made the employment statistics look worse than they really are.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'The Age Equation in Employment Numbers', 'kicker': 'Economix', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Age, Chronological', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Baby Boomers', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Labor and Jobs', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Unemployment', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'United States Economy', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2013-08-02T17:10:33+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Business Day', 'subsection_name': 'Economy', 'byline': {'original': 'By David Leonhardt', 'person': [{'firstname': 'David', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Leonhardt', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/abbf716a-7a9c-59b9-a35b-d4812855774c', 'word_count': 402, 'uri': 'nyt://article/abbf716a-7a9c-59b9-a35b-d4812855774c'}, {'abstract': 'Louis Begley’s novel unfolds in the midst of high-WASP society.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2013/08/04/books/review/louis-begleys-memories-of-a-marriage.html', 'snippet': 'Louis Begley’s novel unfolds in the midst of high-WASP society.', 'lead_paragraph': 'In his engrossing novel “Memories of a Marriage,” Louis Begley gives us a chance to see into two different, often obscured worlds. One is the most private recesses of another couple’s marriage. The other is high-WASP society — though most people don’t usually even know where that particular unmarked door is, let alone get a chance to have it quietly shut in their faces.', 'print_section': 'BR', 'print_page': '7', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/08/04/books/review/0804-bks-WOLITZER/0804-bks-WOLITZER-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 485, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'artic