: [{'firstname': 'Meg', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Wolitzer', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Review', '_id': 'nyt://article/e77d66a9-fffb-519a-a8a4-5802ed33236d', 'word_count': 951, 'uri': 'nyt://article/e77d66a9-fffb-519a-a8a4-5802ed33236d'}, {'abstract': 'Michael Paterniti seeks out a ruined cheesemaker in a remote Spanish village.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2013/08/04/books/review/michael-paternitis-telling-room.html', 'snippet': 'Michael Paterniti seeks out a ruined cheesemaker in a remote Spanish village.', 'lead_paragraph': 'You know those floridly descriptive little signs you find pinned into wedges of aging fromage at your local upmarket cheese shop? Those \xadindex-card vignettes that tell you Cheese A is made from the milk of black-\xadsnouted Iberian sheep that merrily feed on yarrow and camomile, and Cheese B is dense, ornery and challenging, not unlike fusion-era Miles Davis?', 'print_section': 'BR', 'print_page': '10', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/08/04/books/review/04kamp/04kamp-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 600, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2013/08/04/books/review/04kamp/04kamp-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 600}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/08/04/books/review/04kamp/04kamp-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 1024, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/08/04/books/review/04kamp/04kamp-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 1410, 'width': 1410, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/08/04/books/review/04kamp/04kamp-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2013/08/04/books/review/04kamp/04kamp-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/08/04/books/review/04kamp/04kamp-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Every Cheese Has a Story', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Every Cheese Has a Story', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Paterniti, Michael', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Books and Literature', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Cheese', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2013-08-02T17:12:22+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'BookReview', 'section_name': 'Books', 'subsection_name': 'Book Review', 'byline': {'original': 'By David Kamp', 'person': [{'firstname': 'David', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Kamp', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Review', '_id': 'nyt://article/6e45afa7-a9f8-5d42-8bd1-faf563d8d8c1', 'word_count': 1209, 'uri': 'nyt://article/6e45afa7-a9f8-5d42-8bd1-faf563d8d8c1'}, {'abstract': 'Mathew Brady grew his business by making portraits of soldiers heading for the Civil War.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2013/08/04/books/review/mathew-brady-by-robert-wilson.html', 'snippet': 'Mathew Brady grew his business by making portraits of soldiers heading for the Civil War.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Death was early American photography’s killer app. Since the first pictures required long exposures, it was convenient to have a subject that held still. There was a psychological angle as well. A 19th-\xadcentury photographer reported that when he visited a town in upstate New York, all the residents welcomed him except the blacksmith, who at first reviled him as a swindler. But then the blacksmith’s son drowned — and the blacksmith came begging for an image of the boy. ', 'print_section': 'BR', 'print_page': '11', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/08/04/books/review/04SUBCRAIN1/04SUBCRAIN1-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 725, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2013/08/04/books/review/04SUBCRAIN1/04SUBCRAIN1-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 725}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/08/04/books/review/04SUBCRAIN1/04SUBCRAIN1-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 1024, 'width': 848, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/08/04/books/review/04SUBCRAIN1/04SUBCRAIN1-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 1692, 'width': 1400, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/08/04/books/review/04SUBCRAIN1/04SUBCRAIN1-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2013/08/04/books/review/04SUBCRAIN1/04SUBCRAIN1-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/08/04/books/review/04SUBCRAIN1/04SUBCRAIN1-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'How Soon It May Be Too Late', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'How Soon It May Be Too Late', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Crain, Caleb', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Brady, Mathew', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Books and Literature', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Photography', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Civil War (US) (1861-65)', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Wilson, Robert', 'rank': 7, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2013-08-02T17:12:40+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'BookReview', 'section_name': 'Books', 'subsection_name': 'Book Review', 'byline': {'original': 'By Caleb Crain', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Caleb', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Crain', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Review', '_id': 'nyt://article/67a544af-4b6f-5226-b08d-b8ca5c0251ee', 'word_count': 967, 'uri': 'nyt://article/67a544af-4b6f-5226-b08d-b8ca5c0251ee'}, {'abstract': 'Philip Bobbitt examines “The Prince” and its author.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2013/08/04/books/review/philip-bobbitts-garments-of-court-and-palace.html', 'snippet': 'Philip Bobbitt examines “The Prince” and its author.', 'lead_paragraph': 'One expects a book by Philip Bobbitt to be over 900 pages (“The Shield of Achilles,” 2002) or just under 700 pages (“Terror and Consent,” 2008). Then how can he diet himself down to a mere 200 or so pages of text on Machiavelli? Bobbitt is a great systematizer in the Toynbee mold — “Shield” gave us six different state systems since 1500 (princely, kingly, territorial, imperial, national, \xadmarket). ', 'print_section': 'BR', 'print_page': '12', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/08/04/books/review/04SUBWILLS/04SUBWILLS-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 626, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2013/08/04/books/review/04SUBWILLS/04SUBWILLS-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 626}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/08/04/books/review/04SUBWILLS/04SUBWILLS-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 1024, 'width': 982, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/08/04/books/review/04SUBWILLS/04SUBWILLS-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 2048, 'width': 1964, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/08/04/books/review/04SUBWILLS/04SUBWILLS-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2013/08/04/books/review/04SUBWILLS/04SUBWILLS-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/08/04/books/review/04SUBWILLS/04SUBWILLS-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'New Statesman', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'New Statesman', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Bobbitt, Philip C', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Machiavelli, Niccolo', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Books and Literature', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2013-08-02T17:12:50+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'BookReview', 'section_name': 'Books', 'subsection_name': 'Book Review', 'byline': {'original': 'By Garry Wills', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Garry', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Wills', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Review', '_id': 'nyt://article/ed0f1e70-273f-521b-97a3-ee0ef9f90a9d', 'word_count': 1279, 'uri': 'nyt://article/ed0f1e70-273f-521b-97a3-ee0ef9f90a9d'}, {'abstract': 'In Ron Carlson’s novel, the members of a 1960s garage band reunite after 30 years.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2013/08/04/books/review/ron-carlsons-return-to-oakpine.html', 'snippet': 'In Ron Carlson’s novel, the members of a 1960s garage band reunite after 30 years.', 'lead_paragraph': 'The boys from Life on Earth — the garage band that rocked Oakpine High on homecoming night, 1969 — are back. They scattered in a hurry all those years ago, on an afternoon in June, after a run of “great” performances (admittedly, standards were low: they knew fewer than 10 songs). Thirty years later they’ve reassembled in Oakpine, Wyo., the fictional setting of Ron Carlson’s fifth novel, “Return to Oakpine.” Their hometown is sweetly, stubbornly sunk for all time in the “Leave It to Beaver” 1950s. Even in ’69 there was no hint of peace songs or race riots or long hair on the men. In ’99 the town seems to know little of manufactured drugs, the tech boom or Y2K. During high school football home games, Oakpine is “as deserted as a town in a movie about the end of the world, not a noise except for the train every two hours.”', 'print_section': 'BR', 'print_page': '13', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/08/04/books/review/0804-bks-Unferth/0804-bks-Unferth-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 685, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2013/08/04/books/review/0804-bks-Unferth/0804-bks-Unferth-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 685}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/08/04/books/review/0804-bks-Unferth/0804-bks-Unferth-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 754, 'width': 660, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/08/04/books/review/0804-bks-Unferth/0804-bks-Unferth-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 754, 'width': 660, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/08/04/books/review/0804-bks-Unferth/0804-bks-Unferth-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2013/08/04/books/review/0804-bks-Unferth/0804-bks-Unferth-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/08/04/books/review/0804-bks-Unferth/0804-bks-Unferth-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Glory Days', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Glory Days', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Carlson, Ron', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Books and Literature', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2013-08-02T17:15:02+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'BookReview', 'section_name': 'Books', 'subsection_name': 'Book Review', 'byline': {'original': 'By Deb Olin Unferth', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Deb', 'middlename': 'Olin', 'lastname': 'Unferth', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Review', '_id': 'nyt://article/a7557722-4b9b-5245-88ec-03a6a9b55410', 'word_count': 613, 'uri': 'nyt://article/a7557722-4b9b-5245-88ec-03a6a9b55410'}, {'abstract': 'A story collection charts the overlooked career of James Purdy.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2013/08/04/books/review/the-complete-short-stories-of-james-purdy.html', 'snippet': 'A story collection charts the overlooked career of James Purdy.', 'lead_paragraph': 'James Purdy, who died in 2009, was so often declared an under\xadappreciated American genius on the basis of his first few novels that it is easy to forget about the later decades of his writing, which were often appreciated not at all. While the early stuff continued to attract admirers (including Jonathan Franzen, who called Purdy’s 1967 novel “Eustace Chisholm and the Works” “so good that almost any novel you read immediately after it will seem at least a little bit posturing, or dishonest, or self-\xadadmiring”), he drew diminishing interest both from American publishers, who took on his later books only after they were published overseas, and from reviewers, who often ignored them entirely.', 'print_section': 'BR', 'print_page': '15', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/08/04/books/review/0804-LELAND/0804-LELAND-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 805, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2013/08/04/books/review/0804-LELAND/0804-LELAND-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 805}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/08/04/books/review/0804-LELAND/0804-LELAND-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 1024, 'width': 764, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/