', 'snippet': 'Two Chinese dissidents explore the endurance of the human spirit in the face of state-sanctioned cruelty.', 'lead_paragraph': 'In exploring the darker aspects of Chinese life, Ma Jian and Liao Yiwu speak mostly to the outside world. The work of both writers has been banned in China, and neither lives in his native land. Ma Jian is a longtime resident of London. Liao crossed the border into Vietnam in 2011 and fled to Berlin, where he remains today.', 'print_section': 'BR', 'print_page': '14', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/08/04/books/review/04PARKER1/04PARKER1-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 880, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2013/08/04/books/review/04PARKER1/04PARKER1-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 880}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/08/04/books/review/04PARKER1/04PARKER1-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 1024, 'width': 699, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/08/04/books/review/04PARKER1/04PARKER1-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 2048, 'width': 1397, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/08/04/books/review/04PARKER1/04PARKER1-thumbStandard-v2.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2013/08/04/books/review/04PARKER1/04PARKER1-thumbStandard-v2.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/08/04/books/review/04PARKER1/04PARKER1-thumbLarge-v2.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Signs of Protest', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Signs of Protest', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Parker, Emily', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Ma Jian', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Books and Literature', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Liao Yiwu', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Huang, Wenguang', 'rank': 7, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Poetry and Poets', 'rank': 8, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'China', 'rank': 9, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Drew, Flora', 'rank': 10, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2013-08-02T17:15:30+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'BookReview', 'section_name': 'Books', 'subsection_name': 'Book Review', 'byline': {'original': 'By Emily Parker', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Emily', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Parker', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Review', '_id': 'nyt://article/8e004e8b-c23e-54d4-8490-1174b4b0d56f', 'word_count': 1175, 'uri': 'nyt://article/8e004e8b-c23e-54d4-8490-1174b4b0d56f'}, {'abstract': 'The history of empires as a record of political-economic blunders.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2013/08/04/books/review/balance-by-glenn-hubbard-and-tim-kane.html', 'snippet': 'The history of empires as a record of political-economic blunders.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Of all the proponents of human agency against the constraints of geography, culture and historical experience, economists are the most incisive. They obey scientific rigor rather than mere civilizational tendencies. And while the laws of economics yield a determinism all their own, adherence to such laws and the limits they impose, Glenn Hubbard and Tim Kane suggest, will allow any state or people to avoid tragic outcomes, regardless of geographical or other limitations. ', 'print_section': 'BR', 'print_page': '17', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/08/04/books/review/0804-bks-KAPLAN/0804-bks-KAPLAN-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 586, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2013/08/04/books/review/0804-bks-KAPLAN/0804-bks-KAPLAN-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 586}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/08/04/books/review/0804-bks-KAPLAN/0804-bks-KAPLAN-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 1000, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/08/04/books/review/0804-bks-KAPLAN/0804-bks-KAPLAN-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 2000, 'width': 2048, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/08/04/books/review/0804-bks-KAPLAN/0804-bks-KAPLAN-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2013/08/04/books/review/0804-bks-KAPLAN/0804-bks-KAPLAN-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/08/04/books/review/0804-bks-KAPLAN/0804-bks-KAPLAN-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Rise and Fall', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Rise and Fall', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Kane, Timothy J (1968- )', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Hubbard, R Glenn', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Books and Literature', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'United States Economy', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2013-08-02T17:16:42+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'BookReview', 'section_name': 'Books', 'subsection_name': 'Book Review', 'byline': {'original': 'By Robert D. Kaplan', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Robert', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': '', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Review', '_id': 'nyt://article/fb5e5d97-7f69-5616-973f-36f99d899c85', 'word_count': 1306, 'uri': 'nyt://article/fb5e5d97-7f69-5616-973f-36f99d899c85'}, {'abstract': 'Adam Fitzgerald’s debut collection encompasses everything from John Locke to “Buffy the Vampire Slayer.”', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2013/08/04/books/review/adam-fitzgeralds-late-parade.html', 'snippet': 'Adam Fitzgerald’s debut collection encompasses everything from John Locke to “Buffy the Vampire Slayer.”', 'lead_paragraph': 'My heart aches, and a drowsy numbness pains my sense as the poet finishes his reading, but before I can reach for the hemlock, the older gentleman next to me says, “Is there a book that’ll help me understand this kind of poetry?” The poet had gone on for close to an hour, reading poems that were without charm, music, meaning — without people, really, since nothing in them seemed to come from or speak to our common humanity. I have taught and written about poetry for decades; if I was stunned, what chance had the older gentleman, a retired cleric who is a regular at this weekly series, a well-read and earnest listener who wanted so much to like poems that didn’t care at all for him? There’s no book that’ll help you, I told him. It’s not your fault. It’s the poems’ fault. And the poet’s. The \xadpoems are bad, and he shouldn’t have read them.', 'print_section': 'BR', 'print_page': '16', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/08/04/books/review/0804-KIRBY/0804-KIRBY-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 749, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2013/08/04/books/review/0804-KIRBY/0804-KIRBY-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 749}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/08/04/books/review/0804-KIRBY/0804-KIRBY-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 749, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/08/04/books/review/0804-KIRBY/0804-KIRBY-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 749, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/08/04/books/review/0804-KIRBY/0804-KIRBY-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2013/08/04/books/review/0804-KIRBY/0804-KIRBY-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/08/04/books/review/0804-KIRBY/0804-KIRBY-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Lush Life', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Lush Life', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Books and Literature', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'valu