6, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Political Advertising', 'rank': 7, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2013-08-02T18:27:33+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Metro', 'section_name': 'New York', 'byline': {'original': 'By Javier C. Hernández', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Javier', 'middlename': 'C.', 'lastname': 'Hernández', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/4b50e71b-0654-53cb-9838-c5944cffe817', 'word_count': 521, 'uri': 'nyt://article/4b50e71b-0654-53cb-9838-c5944cffe817'}, {'abstract': 'It’s the rare director who can transform the familiar into something brand new. Nicolas Roeg succeeded handsomely with “The Man Who Fell to Earth.”', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2013/08/04/movies/the-man-who-fell-into-movie-acting.html', 'snippet': 'It’s the rare director who can transform the familiar into something brand new. Nicolas Roeg succeeded handsomely with “The Man Who Fell to Earth.”', 'lead_paragraph': 'It’s the rare director who can transform the familiar into something that feels as if it were being seen for the first time. The director Nicolas Roeg succeeded handsomely with “The Man Who Fell to Earth” (1976), part of the retrospective “Watch That Man: David Bowie, Movie Star” at the Walter Reade Theater. In adapting Walter Tevis’s 1963 novel about an extraterrestrial on a secret mission who uses alien technology to become a tycoon on Earth, Mr. Roeg takes Japanese Kabuki, New Mexico landscapes, corporate soullessness and America’s television-saturated culture and has us perceive them as an interstellar out-of-towner might. ', 'print_section': 'AR', 'print_page': '2', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'The Man Who Fell Into Movie Acting', 'kicker': 'Film', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'The Man Who Fell Into Movie Acting', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Movies', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Roeg, Nicolas', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2013-08-02T18:28:16+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Arts&Leisure', 'section_name': 'Movies', 'byline': {'original': 'By Andy Webster', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Andy', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Webster', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/bba30103-2a40-53f2-be81-417f7f7cd8f1', 'word_count': 177, 'uri': 'nyt://article/bba30103-2a40-53f2-be81-417f7f7cd8f1'}, {'abstract': 'Seeking out raw beats from the world’s street-level sound systems is the knack and mission of Diplo, the producer and disc jockey at the core of Major Lazer', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2013/08/04/arts/music/a-world-of-sounds-at-a-dj-marathon.html', 'snippet': 'Seeking out raw beats from the world’s street-level sound systems is the knack and mission of Diplo, the producer and disc jockey at the core of Major Lazer', 'lead_paragraph': 'Seeking out raw beats from the world’s street-level sound systems is the knack and mission of Diplo, the producer and disc jockey at the core of Major Lazer. For more than a decade, Diplo has culled, adapted and often anthologized an international assortment of beats, from Jamaican dancehall to Brazilian baile funk to Angolan kuduro. Is it exposure, exploitation, networking, diluting, sensationalizing, mongrelizing, trend-surfing, cosmopolitanism? All of the above; it’s D.J. remix culture. Mad Decent is Diplo’s label, and he’s applying the brand to dance-party marathons in 13 cities headlined by Major Lazer, which is rooted in dancehall but hurls all kinds of bass-heavy beats. ', 'print_section': 'AR', 'print_page': '2', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'A World of Sounds at a D.J. Marathon', 'kicker': 'The Week Ahead | 