: 'Zoning', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Stringer, Scott M', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Midtown Area (Manhattan, NY)', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2013-08-02T18:52:25+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Opinion', 'byline': {'original': 'By Eleanor Randolph', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Eleanor', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Randolph', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/888d3710-4feb-5198-b9eb-d13b6eab8fc4', 'word_count': 327, 'uri': 'nyt://article/888d3710-4feb-5198-b9eb-d13b6eab8fc4'}, {'abstract': 'A spate of excellent movies about aging may reflect filmmakers’ dawning recognition of the way this global demographic shift affects all our lives.', 'web_url': 'https://newoldage.blogs.nytimes.com/2013/08/02/still-mine-adds-to-movies-on-aging/', 'snippet': 'A spate of excellent movies about aging may reflect filmmakers’ dawning recognition of the way this global demographic shift affects all our lives.', 'lead_paragraph': '“She has her good days and her bad days, that’s all,” Craig Morrison, who is in his late 80s, testily tells his son. It’s an intensely self-sufficient man’s warning to back off, a we-don’t-need-anyone’s-help declaration that will sound familiar to many readers here.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': '‘Still Mine’ Adds to Movies on Aging', 'kicker': 'The New Old Age', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': "Alzheimer's Disease", 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Dementia', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Elderly', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Movies', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2013-08-02T18:55:44+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Health', 'byline': {'original': 'By Paula Span', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Paula', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Span', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/db2c2f3f-d3a3-5ba9-bb9b-ddbc15153e31', 'word_count': 628, 'uri': 'nyt://article/db2c2f3f-d3a3-5ba9-bb9b-ddbc15153e31'}, {'abstract': 'The composer David Lang based “the whisper opera,” coming to Lincoln Center, on insights and confessions he distilled from the Web.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2013/08/04/arts/music/david-langs-whisper-opera-mines-truths-from-the-web.html', 'snippet': 'The composer David Lang based “the whisper opera,” coming to Lincoln Center, on insights and confessions he distilled from the Web.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Opera and technology have long had an uneasy relationship. The one has always required the other — from the Baroque spectacle of 17th-century operas, with their deus-ex-machina gimmickry, to the stagecraft required to mount any contemporary production of Wagner’s “Ring” cycle. ', 'print_section': 'AR', 'print_page': '8', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/08/04/arts/04OPERA1_SPAN/04OPERA1_SPAN-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 400, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2013/08/04/arts/04OPERA1_SPAN/04OPERA1_SPAN-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 400}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/08/04/arts/04OPERA1_SPAN/04OPERA1_SPAN-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 683, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/08/04/arts/04OPERA1_SP