umbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/08/04/education/edlife/04onlinecheat/04onlinecheat-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 1365, 'width': 2048, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/08/04/education/edlife/04onlinecheat/04onlinecheat-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2013/08/04/education/edlife/04onlinecheat/04onlinecheat-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/08/04/education/edlife/04onlinecheat/04onlinecheat-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'To Catch a Cheat', 'kicker': 'Blackboard | Academic Integrity', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'To Catch a Cheat', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Colleges and Universities', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Cheating', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'E-Learning', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2013-08-02T19:45:04+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'EdLife', 'section_name': 'Education', 'subsection_name': 'Education Life', 'byline': {'original': '', 'person': [], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/2b398ddf-758e-583e-808f-99d92204a6b8', 'word_count': 300, 'uri': 'nyt://article/2b398ddf-758e-583e-808f-99d92204a6b8'}, {'abstract': 'A handful of companies have taken the essay mill to the next level.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2013/08/04/education/edlife/paying-for-a-pass-in-an-online-class.html', 'snippet': 'A handful of companies have taken the essay mill to the next level.', 'lead_paragraph': 'You don’t even have to log in. A handful of companies have taken the essay mill to the next level. NoNeedtoStudy.com, WeTakeYourClass.com and BoostMyGrades.com, among others, will take an entire online course for you. ', 'print_page': '4', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Skipping Class (All of Them)', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Skipping Class (All of Them)', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Colleges and Universities', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Cheating', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2013-08-02T19:45:04+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'EdLife', 'section_name': 'Education', 'subsection_name': 'Education Life', 'byline': {'original': '', 'person': [], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/a58e86a0-ab99-5bb2-8c3e-1b4e13b4dc82', 'word_count': 61, 'uri': 'nyt://article/a58e86a0-ab99-5bb2-8c3e-1b4e13b4dc82'}, {'abstract': 'What and where to study, based on graduates’ earnings.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2013/08/04/education/edlife/calculating-the-net-worth-of-a-college-degree.html', 'snippet': 'What and where to study, based on graduates’ earnings.', 'lead_paragraph': 'FOR 18-year-old consumers, financial decisions escalate exponentially. Tall latte or a grande splurge? Lucky Brand or True Religion? State U. or N.Y.U.? Statistics or psych or maybe social work?', 'print_section': 'ED', 'print_page': '6', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Following the Money', 'kicker': 'Strategy | R.O.I.', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Following the Money', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Colleges and Universities', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Labor and Jobs', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Wages and Salaries', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2013-08-02T19:45:05+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'EdLife', 'section_name': 'Education', 'subsection_name': 'Education Life', 'byline': {'original': 'By Samantha Stainburn', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Samantha', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Stainburn', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/e54cdc2b-f546-5f75-b1cd-8ecfb3381838', 'word_count': 1316, 'uri': 'nyt://article/e54cdc2b-f546-5f75-b1cd-8ecfb3381838'}, {'abstract': 'The SAT is being overhauled — again — and the ACT is going digital. The test makers reveal what the exams might look like.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2013/08/04/education/edlife/what-the-new-sat-and-digital-act-might-look-like.html', 'snippet': 'The SAT is being overhauled — again — and the ACT is going digital. The test makers reveal what the exams might look like.', 'lead_paragraph': 'SAY farewell to vocabulary flashcards with arcane words like “compendious,” “membranous,” “mendacious,” “pugnacious,” “depreciatory,” “redolent,” “treacly” and “jettison.” In the new SAT, to be unveiled in 2015, David Coleman, president of the College Board, wants to get rid of obscure words that are . . . just SAT words, and replace them with more common words like “synthesis,” “distill” and “transform,” used in context as they will be in college and in life.', 'print_section': 'ED', 'print_page': '16', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/08/04/education/0804TIME4/0804TIME4-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 532, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2013/08/04/education/0804TIME4/0804TIME4-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xla