type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'EdLife', 'section_name': 'Education', 'subsection_name': 'Education Life', 'byline': {'original': 'By Tamar Lewin', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Tamar', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Lewin', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/f4d98b5c-4317-559e-9c99-941e6bc4b1c2', 'word_count': 1512, 'uri': 'nyt://article/f4d98b5c-4317-559e-9c99-941e6bc4b1c2'}, {'abstract': 'Art and music schools top the government’s list of highest net prices.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2013/08/04/education/edlife/a-lofty-net-price-for-art-and-music-schools.html', 'snippet': 'Art and music schools top the government’s list of highest net prices.', 'lead_paragraph': 'SINCE 2011, the Department of Education has tried to shame colleges over costs. This summer’s data release shows that 7 of the 10 most expensive private nonprofit colleges (in terms of net price, or total cost of attendance minus average grant aid) are art and music conservatories. In defense: tiny endowments, small studio classes and expensive glassblowing equipment and audio mixers. On the other hand: these are watercolorists and musicians.', 'print_section': 'ED', 'print_page': '4', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/08/04/education/0804BBOARD2/0804BBOARD2-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 649, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2013/08/04/education/0804BBOARD2/0804BBOARD2-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 649}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/08/04/education/0804BBOARD2/0804BBOARD2-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 1024, 'width': 947, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/08/04/education/0804BBOARD2/0804BBOARD2-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 1106, 'width': 1022, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/08/04/education/0804BBOARD2/0804BBOARD2-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2013/08/04/education/0804BBOARD2/0804BBOARD2-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/08/04/education/0804BBOARD2/0804BBOARD2-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'The Costliest Colleges', 'kicker': 'Blackboard  | Art Smarts', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'The Costliest Colleges', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Colleges and Universities', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2013-08-02T19:45:09+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'EdLife', 'section_name': 'Education', 'subsection_name': 'Education Life', 'byline': {'original': '', 'person': [], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/abd224be-2f0f-5191-a045-7a54e5d7790a', 'word_count': 115, 'uri': 'nyt://article/abd224be-2f0f-5191-a045-7a54e5d7790a'}, {'abstract': 'A student who met with almost every single faculty member.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2013/08/04/education/edlife/desk-side-chats-with-200-professors.html', 'snippet': 'A student who met with almost every single faculty member.', 'lead_paragraph': 'FRESHMAN year at Hamilton College, in Clinton, N.Y., Bret Turner asked a music professor, “Why is music important?” Enthused by the passionate response, he decided to speak with every teacher on campus about his or her discipline. By the time he graduated, in May, he’d chatted with 200 of 223 faculty members, a half-hour to an hour each.', 'print_section': 'ED', 'print_page': '4', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/08/04/education/0804BRET/0804BRET-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 662, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2013/08/04/education/0804BRET/0804BRET-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 662}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/08/04/education/0804BRET/0804BRET-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 1024, 'width': 928, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/08/04/education/0804BRET/0804BRET-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 1415, 'width': 1282, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/08/04/education/0804BRET/0804BRET-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2013/08/04/education/0804BRET/0804BRET-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/08/04/education/0804BRET/0804BRET-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Talking With Teachers', 'kicker': 'Blackboard | Faculty Engagement', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Talking With Teachers, All 200 of Them', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Colleges and Universities', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Hamilton College', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2013-08-02T19:45:14+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'EdLife', 'section_name': 'Education', 'subsection_name': 'Education Life', 'byline': {'original': '', 'person': [], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/74afa4df-c282-569d-a425-ae2a16f04f42', 'word_count': 184, 'uri': 'nyt://article/74afa4df-c282-569d-a425-ae2a16f04f42'}, {'abstract': 'Questions for the co-author of “Paying for the Party.”', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2013/08/04/education/edlife/elizabeth-a-armstrong-on-her-book-paying-for-the-party.html', 'snippet': 'Questions for the co-author of “Paying for the Party.”', 'lead_paragraph': 'IN the fall of 2004, 53 young women arriving for their first year at a public research university found that one room on their dorm floor had a sign saying, “This room is occupied by sociology researchers studying college student social life.”', 'print_section': 'ED', 'print_page': '8', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/08/04/education/edlife/04talking-head/04talking-head-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 900, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2013/08/04/education/edlife/04talking-head/04talking-head-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 900}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/08/04/education/edlife/04talking-head/04talking-head-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 1024, 'width': 683, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/08/04/education/edlife/04talking-head/04talking-head-superJumbo.jpg', 'h