s', 'value': 'Equifax Inc', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Suits and Litigation (Civil)', 'rank': 7, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2013-08-02T19:51:58+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Business', 'section_name': 'Your Money', 'subsection_name': 'Credit Scores', 'byline': {'original': 'By Tara Siegel Bernard', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Tara', 'middlename': 'Siegel', 'lastname': 'Bernard', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/4e154ef0-f7d1-5179-aef4-847674019e88', 'word_count': 1551, 'uri': 'nyt://article/4e154ef0-f7d1-5179-aef4-847674019e88'}, {'abstract': 'Miriam Linna, a book and music collector who resells her finds, has been spending her Sundays salvaging stock damaged by Hurricane Sandy.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2013/08/04/nyregion/a-seventh-day-but-not-a-day-of-rest.html', 'snippet': 'Miriam Linna, a book and music collector who resells her finds, has been spending her Sundays salvaging stock damaged by Hurricane Sandy.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Stuff doesn’t stop for Sundays or holidays, and when you talk about Miriam Linna and her husband, Billy Miller, you’re talking about stuff. She collects paperbacks, with an emphasis on pulp fiction; they both collect records, the scruffier the better. Then they recycle the gems through their businesses. From their apartment in a converted school in Prospect Heights, Brooklyn, she runs Kicks Books (recent titles include “Gone Man Squared” by the English beat poet Royston Ellis) and both run the resolutely lo-fi Norton Records. Since 1984, they have also made joyful noises with their garage rock band, the A-Bones, with Ms. Linna, 57, on drums, and Mr. Miller singing. Since Hurricane Sandy flooded their warehouse in Red Hook, they have been working to salvage records and books, including on Sundays. Roaming among their collections is their mixed-breed dog, Queenie.', 'print_section': 'MB', 'print_page': '2', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/08/04/nyregion/04ROUTINE_SPAN/0804ROUTINE-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 400, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2013/08/04/nyregion/04ROUTINE_SPAN/0804ROUTINE-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 400}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/08/04/nyregion/04ROUTINE_SPAN/0804ROUTINE-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 683, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/08/04/nyregion/04ROUTINE_SPAN/0804ROUTINE-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 1365, 'width': 2048, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/08/04/nyregion/04ROUTINE_SPAN/0804ROUTINE-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2013/08/04/nyregion/04ROUTINE_SPAN/0804ROUTINE-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/08/04/nyregion/04ROUTINE_SPAN/0804ROUTINE-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'A Seventh Day, but Not a Day of Rest', 'kicker': 'Sunday Routine', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'A Seventh Day, but Not a Day of Rest', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Books and Literature', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Prospect Heights (Brooklyn, NY)', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Linna, Miriam', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2013-08-02T19:52:07+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Metro', 'section_name': 'New York', 'byline': {'original': 'By John Leland', 'person': [{'firstname': 'John', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Leland', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/db0f68b3-e65f-517f-9d96-4b3d1eeffc03', 'word_count': 749, 'uri': 'nyt://article/db0f68b3-e65f-517f-9d96-4b3d1eeffc03'}, {'abstract': 'The Natural History Museum of Los Angeles County has transformed its approach to evolution, culture, history and humanity.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2013/08/03/arts/design/the-remaking-of-a-los-angeles-museum.html', 'snippet': 'The Natural History Museum of Los Angeles County has transformed its approach to evolution, culture, history and humanity.', 'lead_paragraph': 'LOS ANGELES — The traditional natural history museum is powerful and familiar, but it is also strange. It is weighty, ponderous, pieced together from relics of lost worlds. It evolved in the 19th century, displaying the geological cataclysms that molded the earth’s surface, the creatures that clambered into habitats and the indigenous cultures that were once considered closer to nature. No other museum genre has changed so glacially. ', 'print_section': 'C', 'print_page': '1', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/08/03/arts/03NATURALjp1/03NATURALjp1-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 384, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2013/08/03/arts/03NATURALjp1/03NATURALjp1-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 384}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type'