 Moriarity (Jonny Weston, above right, with Mr. Butler), a legend-in-the-making with a dream and a battered board, the way of the waves — specifically, the monster crushers in Northern California on a break known as Mavericks. Curtis Hanson and Michael Apted shared the directing duties on this film, based on a true story that begins in 1987 and is lent authenticity by the real-life surfers Greg Long, Peter Mel and Zach Wormhoudt. “When the movie works, its buoyancy can be infectious and persuasive, particularly in the surfing scenes and in the mesmerizing shots of the heaving, churning ocean and waves,” Manohla Dargis wrote in The New York York Times. “The movie captures the hypnotic quality of this watery landscape, with its steel blue undulations and the Freudian oceanic feeling it summons. It invites you to gaze into this watery abyss, to ponder its mysteries as well as those of the swimmer whose image brackets the movie and evokes John F. Kennedy’s observation: ‘We are tied to the ocean. And when we go back to the sea, whether it is to sail or to watch, we are going back from whence we came.’\xa0” ', 'print_section': 'C', 'print_page': '7', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'What’s On Saturday', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'What’s On Saturday', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [], 'pub_date': '2013-08-03T05:00:07+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Culture', 'section_name': 'Arts', 'subsection_name': 'Television', 'byline': {'original': 'By Kathryn Shattuck', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Kathryn', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Shattuck', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Schedule', '_id': 'nyt://article/ab4333e8-dabf-5703-97e8-de3cca23cfcf', 'word_count': 765, 'uri': 'nyt://article/ab4333e8-dabf-5703-97e8-de3cca23cfcf'}, {'abstract': 'C.C. Sabathia, who has been one of