paragraph': 'WASHINGTON — LESS than 24 hours after Pfc. Bradley Manning was convicted last week of handing off 250,000 State Department cables and defense documents to WikiLeaks, The Guardian published on its Web site the latest classified material from the leaker of the moment, Edward J. Snowden. That installment included the National Security Agency’s playbook for XKeyscore, a powerful surveillance program enabling the agency’s analysts to monitor and trace Internet searches around the globe. ', 'print_section': 'SR', 'print_page': '4', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/08/04/sunday-review/04CYBER/04CYBER-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 600, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2013/08/04/sunday-review/04CYBER/04CYBER-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 600}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/08/04/sunday-review/04CYBER/04CYBER-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 1024, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/08/04/sunday-review/04CYBER/04CYBER-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 1800, 'width': 1800, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/08/04/sunday-review/04CYBER/04CYBER-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2013/08/04/sunday-review/04CYBER/04CYBER-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/08/04/sunday-review/04CYBER/04CYBER-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'A Washington Riddle: What Is ‘Top Secret’?', 'kicker': 'News Analysis', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'A Washington Riddle: What Is ‘Top Secret’?', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Classified Information and State Secrets', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'United States Defense and Military Forces', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Cyberwarfare', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'United States Politics and Government', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Espionage and Intelligence Services', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Drones (Pilotless Planes)', 'rank': 7, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Computers and the Internet', 'rank': 8, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Snowden, Edward J', 'rank': 9, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Manning, Bradley E', 'rank': 10, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Moynihan, Daniel Patrick', 'rank': 11, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Obama, Barack', 'rank': 12, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2013-08-03T18:30:29+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'OpEd', 'section_name': 'Sunday Review', 'byline': {'original': 'By David E. Sanger', 'person': [{'firstname': 'David', 'middlename': 'E.', 'lastname': 'Sanger', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News Analysis', '_id': 'nyt://article/ed3fb25a-4fe1-550e-9d94-1743e56b1447', 'word_count': 984, 'uri': 'nyt://article/ed3fb25a-4fe1-550e-9d94-1743e56b1447'}, {'abstract': 'A national economic, social and cultural whirlwind transformed the life chances of the children and grandchildren of those I graduated from Port Clinton High School with in 1959.', 'web_url': 'https://opinionator.blogs.nytimes.com/2013/08/03/crumbling-american-dreams/', 'snippet': 'A national economic, social and cultural whirlwind transformed the life chances of the children and grandchildren of those I graduated from Port Clinton High School with in 1959.', 'lead_paragraph': 'My hometown — Port Clinton, Ohio, population 6,050 — was in the 1950s a passable embodiment of the American dream, a place that offered decent opportunity for the children of bankers and factory workers alike.', 'print_section': 'SR', 'print_page': '9', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Crumbling American Dreams', 'kicker': 'The Great Divide', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Crumbling American Dreams', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Factories and Manufacturing', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Income Inequality', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Labor and Jobs', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Poverty', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Port Clinton (Ohio)', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2013-08-03T18:30:56+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Opinion', 'byline': {'original': 'By Robert D. Putnam', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Robert', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': '', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/5e7a65bc-12b4-5005-bf18-7ad439593731', 'word_count': 1589, 'uri': 'nyt://article/5e7a65bc-12b4-5005-bf18-7ad439593731'}, {'abstract': 'Indigo Triplett started out as a Marine and now runs her own consulting firm, Careers in Transition, in Tucker, Ga.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2013/08/04/jobs/the-dna-of-helping-people.html', 'snippet': 'Indigo Triplett started out as a Marine and now runs her own consulting firm, Careers in Transition, in Tucker, Ga.', 'lead_paragraph': 'I GREW up in a suburb of Chicago and attended a predominantly white high school. We were at the tail end of the civil rights movement but I never experienced any unrest. However, my parents were not as lucky. When I was in elementary school, my mother came home early one day from her job at a manufacturing company. She said the Ku Klux Klan was meeting in the company cafeteria at 4 p.m. and that there was actually a meeting notice on a bulletin board.', 'print_section': 'BU', 'print_page': '7', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/08/04/business/04-BOSS/04-BOSS-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 778, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2013/08/04/business/04-BOSS/04-BOSS-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 778}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/08/04/business/04-BOSS/04-BOSS-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 1024, 'width': 790, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/08/04/business/04-BOSS/04-BOSS-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 2048, 'width': 1580, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/08/04/business/04-BOSS/04-BOSS-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2013/08/04/business/04-BOSS/04-BOSS-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/08/04/business/04-BOSS/04-BOSS-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'The DNA of Helping People', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'The DNA of Helping People', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Executives and Management (Theory)', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Careers and Professions', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Careers In Transition Inc', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Triplett, Indigo', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2013-08-03T18:31:10+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'SundayBusiness', 'section_name': 'Job Market', 'byline': {'original': 'By Indigo Triplett', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Indigo', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Triplett', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/ca06e28a-2e16-50e0-9184-b5dfbae82b92', 'word_count': 613, 'uri': 'nyt://article/ca06e28a-2e16-50e0-9184-b5dfbae82b92'}, {'abstract': 'Hiram Gonzalez, who was laid off from his reinsurance job at 59, got a new, full-time position after working as a consultant.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2013/08/04/jobs/older-unemployed-and-landing-the-job.html', 'snippet': 'Hiram Gonzalez, who was laid off from his reinsurance job at 59, got a new, full-time position after working as a consultant.', 'lead_paragraph': 'I WAS laid off four times in my career before I turned 50. But when it happened again two years ago, I had just turned 59. Getting laid off at that age is a different ballgame. I knew I could bring value to a company, but in moments of frustration during the job hunt, I wondered how long it would take to find another job, and there was always the chance I wouldn’t be able to find one. ', 'print_section': 'BU', 'print_page': '7', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/08/04/business/04-PRE/04-PRE-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 466, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2013/08/04/business/04-PRE/04-PRE-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 466}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/08/04/business/04-PRE/04-PRE-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 796, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/08/04/business/04-PRE/04-PRE-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 1592, 'width': 2048, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/08/04/business/04-PRE/04-PRE-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2013/08/04/business/04-PRE/04-PRE-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/08/04/business/04-PRE/04-PRE-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Older, Unemployed, and Landing the Job', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Older, Unemployed, And Landing The Job', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Hiring and Promotion', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Layoffs and Job Reductions', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Scor SE', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Aurigen Capital Ltd', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2013-08-03T18:35:49+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'SundayBusiness', 'section_name': 'Job Market', 'byline': {'original': 'By Hiram Gonzalez', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Hiram', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Gonzalez', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/11682468-627d-549b-91f9-3bfbf75d289a', 'word_count': 884, 'uri': 'nyt://article/11682468-627d-549b-91f9-3bfbf75d289a'}, {'abstract': 'State and local governments should make prudent financial decisions without gutting retirement benefits.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2013/08/04/opinion/sunday/public-pensions-after-detroit.html', 'snippet': 'State and local governments should make prudent financial decisions without gutting retirement benefits.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Detroit’s bankruptcy and the problems facing its pension funds offer two important lessons to other communities. One is that state and local governments need to do a much better job managing retirement funds. The other is that they should not pre-emptively reduce hard-earned benefits at the first sign of trouble.', 'print_section': 'SR', 'print_page': '10', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Public Pensions After Detroit', 'kicker': 'Editorial', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Public Pensions After Detroit', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Pensions and Retirement Plans', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'New Brunswick (Canada)', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Detroit (Mich)', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Stockton (Calif)', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Illinois', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Connecticut', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'States (US)', 'rank': 7, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Local Government', 'rank': 8, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Editorials', 'rank': 9, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Government Employees', 'rank': 10, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2013-08-03T18:46:28+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Editorial', 'section_name': 'Opinion', 'subsection_name': 'Sunday Review', 'byline': {'original': 'By The Editorial Board', 'person': [], 'organization': 'The Editorial Board'}, 'type_of_material': 'Editorial', '_id': 'nyt://article/37860cc8-dd03-5bec-8a29-48ef99de7bff', 'word_count': 513, 'uri': 'nyt://article/37860cc8-dd03-5bec-8a29-48ef99de7bff'}, {'abstract': 'The House leadership made an embarrassing retreat from an austere transportation and housing bill after realizing they couldn’t get enough votes to pass it.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2013/08/04/opinion/sunday/republican-no-shows-in-the-budget-wars.html', 'snippet': 'The House leadership made an embarrassing retreat from an austere transportation and housing bill after realizing they couldn’t get enough votes to pass it.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Amid the morass of Washington’s endless budget fights there was a moment of sweet revelation last week: House Republicans choked outright when they failed to muster support for the draconian dreams of Paul Ryan, the Wisconsin legislator and fabled budget hawk who last year ran for vice president on a program of unforgiving fiscal austerity that his colleagues suddenly found too politically risky. ', 'print_section': 'SR', 'print_page': '10', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Republican No-Shows in the Budget Wars', 'kicker': 'Editorial', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Republican No-Shows in the Budget Wars', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Federal Budget (US)', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'House of Representatives', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Republican Party', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Ryan, Paul D Jr', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Editorials', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2013-08-03T18:46:32+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Editorial', 'section_name': 'Opinion', 'subsection_name': 'Sunday Review', 'byline': {'original': 'By The Editorial Board', 'person': [], 'organization': 'The Editorial Board'}, 'type_of_material': 'Editorial', '_id': 'nyt://article/2c0c850a-bc01-55d5-a36d-5610ee864fd7', 'word_count': 378, 'uri': 'nyt://article/2c0c850a-bc01-55d5-a36d-5610ee864fd7'}, {'abstract': 'The Federal Reserve has spent heavily to buy bonds to reduce long-term rates, but rates are actually higher than when it started.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2013/08/04/business/what-the-nation-got-for-800-billion.html', 'snippet': 'The Federal Reserve has spent heavily to buy bonds to reduce long-term rates, but rates are actually higher than when it started.', 'lead_paragraph': 'SINCE last September, when the Federal Reserve announced its third round of quantitative easing, known as QE3, the central bank has spent $800 billion buying bonds. That’s a stupendous sum, more than the budget for the Department of Defense and more than twice what the federal government spends on welfare programs, including food and nutrition assistance, unemployment benefits and disability payments. ', 'print_section': 'BU', 'print_page': '6', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'What the Nation Got for $800 Billion', 'kicker': 'Data Points', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'What the Nation Got For $800 Billion', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Federal Reserve System', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Quantitative Easing', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Interest Rates', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Mann, Catherine L', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'United States Economy', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'United States', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2013-08-03T18:51:09+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'SundayBusiness', 'section_name': 'Business Day', 'byline': {'original': 'By Anna Bernasek', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Anna', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Bernasek', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/b2515dde-8628-58e7-9e84-a65cc90469aa', 'word_count': 345, 'uri': 'nyt://article/b2515dde-8628-58e7-9e84-a65cc90469aa'}, {'abstract': 'Great architects build structures that can make us feel enclosed, liberated or suspended. Great writers, in devising their literary structures, do exactly the same.', 'web_url': 'https://opinionator.blogs.nytimes.com/2013/08/03/writers-as-architects/', 'snippet': 'Great architects build structures that can make us feel enclosed, liberated or suspended. Great writers, in devising their literary structures, do exactly the same.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Great architects build structures that can make us feel enclosed, liberated or suspended. They lead us through space, make us slow down, speed up or stop to contemplate. Great writers, in devising their literary structures, do exactly the same. ', 'print_section': 'SR', 'print_page': '12', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Writers as Architects', 'kicker': 'Draft', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Writers as Architects', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'ARCHITECTURE', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Books and Literature', 'rank': 2