ial': 'Video', '_id': 'nyt://video/5ed9d1a5-264b-57fc-b549-7fc468dfeb7e', 'word_count': 0, 'uri': 'nyt://video/5ed9d1a5-264b-57fc-b549-7fc468dfeb7e'}, {'abstract': 'To abandon your native tongue and adopt another is to dismantle yourself, piece by piece, and then to put yourself together again in a different form.', 'web_url': 'https://opinionator.blogs.nytimes.com/2013/08/04/born-again-in-a-second-language/', 'snippet': 'To abandon your native tongue and adopt another is to dismantle yourself, piece by piece, and then to put yourself together again in a different form.', 'lead_paragraph': 'In her exploration of the Catholic religion, “Letter to a Priest,” written the year before her death in 1943, Simone Weil noticed at some point that “for any man a change of religion is as dangerous a thing as a change of language is for a writer. It may turn out a success, but it can also have disastrous consequences.” The Romanian philosopher Emil Cioran, who was one such writer, talks of the change of language as a catastrophic event in any author’s biography. And rightly so.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Born Again in a Second Language', 'kicker': 'The Stone', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Books and Literature', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Language and Languages', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Philosophy', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Writing and Writers', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Beckett, Samuel', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Conrad, Joseph', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Nabokov, Vladimir', 'rank': 7, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2013-08-04T21:15:06+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Opinion', 'byline': {'original': 'By Costica Bradatan'