his word has appeared in four New York Times articles in the past year.', 'web_url': 'https://learning.blogs.nytimes.com/2013/08/05/word-of-the-day-philatelist/', 'snippet': 'This word has appeared in four New York Times articles in the past year.', 'lead_paragraph': 'philatelist •\\fə-ˈla-tə-list\\• noun', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Word of the Day | philatelist', 'kicker': 'The Learning Network', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [], 'pub_date': '2013-08-05T04:02:18+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Education', 'byline': {'original': 'By The Learning Network', 'person': [], 'organization': 'The Learning Network'}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/abc95c36-889e-550d-b093-4187e2ca8401', 'word_count': 229, 'uri': 'nyt://article/abc95c36-889e-550d-b093-4187e2ca8401'}, {'abstract': 'After 12 years, American Express is stepping back as the presenting sponsor of the festival in Lower Manhattan.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2013/08/05/business/media/att-not-american-express-will-be-chief-sponsor-of-tribeca-film-festival.html', 'snippet': 'After 12 years, American Express is stepping back as the presenting sponsor of the festival in Lower Manhattan.', 'lead_paragraph': 'LOS ANGELES — Come April 16, the stars will strut, paparazzi will pop, and fans will file into packed theaters, turning Lower Manhattan into a temporary film capital during the 13th annual Tribeca Film Festival. But they will not be having all that fun under the ubiquitous, blue-and-white corporate logo of American Express.', 'print_section': 'B', 'print_page': '6', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'AT&T, Not American Express, Will Be Chief Sponsor of Tribeca Film Festival', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'AT&T, Not American Express, Will Be Chief Sponsor of Tribeca Film Festival', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'AT&T Inc', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'AMERICAN EXPRESS COMPANY', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'ADVERTISING AND MARKETING', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2013-08-05T04:11:04+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Business', 'section_name': 'Business Day', 'subsection_name': 'Media', 'byline': {'original': 'By Michael Cieply', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Michael', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Cieply', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/01237857-878d-532f-a608-9d2f58123dfd', 'word_count': 459, 'uri': 'nyt://article/01237857-878d-532f-a608-9d2f58123dfd'}, {'abstract': 'Television highlights.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2013/08/05/arts/television/whats-on-monday.html', 'snippet': 'Television highlights.', 'lead_paragraph': '10:30 P.M. (Showtime 2) 2 DAYS IN NEW YORK (2012) Julie Delpy directed and stars in this tale about 48 hours in the life of Marion, a high-strung photographer; her partner, Mingus (Chris Rock, above left, with Ms. Delpy, right, Talen Ruth Riley and Owen Shipman), a journalist and radio host; and their two children from previous relationships, as they all deal with the arrival of Marion’s Parisian family members, who proceed to drive the couple crazy. Marion’s father (played by Ms. Delpy’s father, Albert) is detained at the airport for trying to enter the United States with cheese and sausage taped to his body; her sister (Alexia Landeau) and her sister’s boyfriend (Alex Nahon) smoke weed in an elevator and have noisy sex in the bathroom, possibly involving Mingus’s electric toothbrush. “The driving crazy part is hilarious and persuasive, partly because Ms. Delpy and her cast depart from the usual caricatures of Frenchness while still engaging in broad, silly satire,” A. O. Scott wrote in The New York Times. And although Marion and Mingus “never quite seem like a credible, comfortable couple,” he added, the film’s best parts “are those that cast an affectionate, unsentimental eye on the city, which looks lived-in, real and nonmythical, the way that Paris often looks in French movies and New York rarely does in Hollywood confections.”', 'print_section': 'C', 'print_page': '7', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'What’s on Monday', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'What’s On Monday', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [], 'pub_date': '2013-08-05T04:15:05+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Culture', 'section_name': 'Arts', 'subsection_name': 'Television', 'byline': {'original': 'By Kathryn Shattuck', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Kathryn', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Shattuck', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Schedule', '_id': 'nyt://article/8aca4e60-8c6d-5c36-aebc-697d2a2bec9a', 'word_count': 757, 'uri': 'nyt://article/8aca4e60-8c6d-5c36-aebc-697d2a2bec9a'}, {'abstract': 'Quotation of the Day for Monday, August 5', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2013/08/05/pageoneplus/quotation-of-the-day-for-monday-august-5.html', 'snippet': 'Quotation of the Day for Monday, August 5', 'lead_paragraph': '“It’s a shame and embarrassing that in a city of 4 million people we are down to one woman.”', 'print_section': 'A', 'print_page': '2', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Quotation of the Day for Monday, August 5', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Quotation of the Day', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [], 'pub_date': '2013-08-05T04:21:21+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Summary', 'section_name': 'Corrections', 'byline': {'origina