 States.', 'lead_paragraph': 'LONDON – HSBC, Britain’s largest bank, said on Monday that earnings rose 10 percent in the first half of the year, thanks in part to lower charges for bad loans, especially in the United States.', 'print_section': 'B', 'print_page': '6', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'HSBC’s First-Half Profit Rose 10%', 'kicker': 'DealBook', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Higher Profit', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Company Reports', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'HSBC Holdings PLC.', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'London (England)', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2013-08-05T10:12:32+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Business Day', 'byline': {'original': 'By Julia Werdigier', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Julia', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Werdigier', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/30a4e7d2-3ba0-5bc4-a01b-c7b3dfe7f1fa', 'word_count': 464, 'uri': 'nyt://article/30a4e7d2-3ba0-5bc4-a01b-c7b3dfe7f1fa'}, {'abstract': 'Doubled cuts, not cut in half.', 'web_url': 'https://krugman.blogs.nytimes.com/2013/08/05/food-stamps-a-correction/', 'snippet': 'Doubled cuts, not cut in half.', 'lead_paragraph': 'I assume we’ll put up a formal correction later, but I somehow confused x and 1/x in today’s column. House Republicans proposed doubling cuts to food stamps, not cutting them in half. Nothing important changes in my argument, but I did make a mistake. And it’s both Times policy and mine to acknowledge errors when you make them.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Food Stamps: A Correction', 'kicker': 'Paul Krugman', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [], 'pub_date': '2013-08-05T10:15:34+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Opinion', 'byline': {'original': 'By Paul Krugman', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Paul', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Krugman', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/906192e1-2a5a-565c-8ba8-6c7f01ba3ce2', 'word_count': 58, 'uri': 'nyt://article/906192e1-2a5a-565c-8ba8-6c7f01ba3ce2'}, {'abstract': 'Cutting out the middleman is problematic.', 'web_url': 'https://krugman.blogs.nytimes.com/2013/08/05/nate-silver-superstar/', 'snippet': 'Cutting out the middleman is problematic.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Over at Barry Ritholtz’s place, Bob Lefsetz argues that Nate Silver’s departure from the Times heralds a new age of journalism in which the individual journalist builds his or her own brand, and the middlemen — like newspapers — lose power. In fact, he compares Nate to Arcade Fire, who pioneered the modern indie rock movement by creating their own position rather than by relying on record companies.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Nate Silver, Superstar', 'kicker': 'Paul Krugman', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [], 'pub_date': '2013-08-05T10:40:34+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Opinion', 'byline': {'original': 'By Paul Krugman', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Paul', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Krugman', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/bc1ed7d5-f8bd-5353-a2b5-6e305dd69f49', 'word_count': 327, 'uri': 'nyt://article/bc1ed7d5-f8bd-5353-a2b5-6e305dd69f49'}, {'abstract': 'It’s not looking pretty.', 'web_url': 'https://krugman.blogs.nytimes.com/2013/08/05/budget-bedlam/', 'snippet': 'It’s not looking pretty.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Lest you think that I am exaggerating the mess created by the madness of the GOP, the very sober Stan Collender weighs in. Scary stuff.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Budget Bedlam', 'kicker': 'Paul Krugman', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [], 'pub_date': '2013-08-05T10:42:46+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Opinion', 'byline': {'original': 'By Paul Krugman', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Paul', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Krugman', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/b0e35b02-1df8-5b11-b947-b44c06656dad', 'word_count': 25, 'uri': 'nyt://article/b0e35b02-1df8-5b11-b947-b44c06656dad'}, {'abstract': 'German labor unions say Amazon has imported American-style business practices — in particular, an antipathy to organized labor — that stand at odds with European norms.', 'web_url': 'https://bits.blogs.nytimes.com/2013/08/05/daily-report-amazon-runs-up-against-germanys-labor-culture/', 'snippet': 'German labor unions say Amazon has imported American-style business practices — in particular, an antipathy to organized labor — that stand at odds with European norms.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Even as President Obama spoke about middle-class jobs last week at an Amazon warehouse in Tennessee, Amazon was facing strikes at warehouses in Germany, its second-biggest market. Unions there say the company has imported American-style business practices — in particular, an antipathy to organized labor — that stand at odds with European norms, Nick Wingfield and Melissa Eddy report.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/11/30/technology/bits-daily-rep