-08-05T12:29:35+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Business', 'section_name': 'Business Day', 'subsection_name': 'Global Business', 'byline': {'original': 'By Jonathan Hutchison', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Jonathan', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Hutchison', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/88d3d799-cf8f-5ef5-8158-d95359d98140', 'word_count': 874, 'uri': 'nyt://article/88d3d799-cf8f-5ef5-8158-d95359d98140'}, {'abstract': 'A weekend in the life of armed America.', 'web_url': 'https://nocera.blogs.nytimes.com/2013/08/05/weekend-gun-report-august-2-4-2013/', 'snippet': 'A weekend in the life of armed America.', 'lead_paragraph': 'The shooting of Trayvon Martin in a gated community in Sanford, Fla., drew public attention to the state’s self-defense statutes, and lawmakers there seem to be reacting: Florida’s House of Representatives will hold hearings this fall on its “Stand Your Ground” self-defense law, which was the first of its kind in the country when it was adopted in 2005.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Weekend Gun Report: August 2-4, 2013', 'kicker': 'Joe Nocera', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [], 'pub_date': '2013-08-05T12:53:02+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Opinion', 'byline': {'original': 'By Jennifer Mascia', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Jennifer', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Mascia', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/411a0a62-2128-596a-8dd4-622c891ad97b', 'word_count': 2466, 'uri': 'nyt://article/411a0a62-2128-596a-8dd4-622c891ad97b'}, {'abstract': 'Trevor Nunn’s well-received 2012 stage adaptation of the  Samuel Beckett radio play “All That Fall,” starring Eileen Atkins and Michael Gambon, will be presented in New York at the end of the year.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2013/08/05/theater/samuel-beckett-at-59e59-theaters.html', 'snippet': 'Trevor Nunn’s well-received 2012 stage adaptation of the  Samuel Beckett radio play “All That Fall,” starring Eileen Atkins and Michael Gambon, will be presented in New York at the end of the year.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Trevor Nunn’s well-received 2012 stage adaptation of the haunting Samuel Beckett radio play “All That Fall,” starring Eileen Atkins and Michael Gambon, will be presented in New York at the end of the year, following runs at two London theaters last fall. The show, a highlight of the coming season at 59E59 Theaters, features a deceptively simple plot: A woman heads to meet her blind husband at a train station. But this is Beckett, after all, and the story turns out to be more of a tragicomic meditation on life and death. Mr. Nunn’s production, because of restrictions set by the Beckett estate, is presented in the form of a live radio play, with actors holding scripts and using minimal props. The 2013-14 season at 59E59 Theaters, to be announced on Monday, is also to include “I Can See Clearly Now (The Wheelchair on My Face)” (Sept. 10 to 29), a solo show written and performed by Sonya Kelly, courtesy of the Dublin company Fishamble: The New Play Company; “Witnessed by the World” (Nov. 7 to Dec. 15), a play by Ronnie Cohen and Jane Beale, starring Tovah Feldshuh as a journalist fixated on the assassination of John F. Kennedy; Squonk Opera’s surreal meditation on the visualization of music, “Mayhem and Majesty” (Dec. 10 to 29); and “Beertown” (Jan. 30 to Feb. 16), about a town’s time capsule. “All That Fall” is scheduled to run Nov. 5 to Dec. 8 at 59E59 Theaters, which also produces the annual “Brits Off Broadway” festival, planned for April 1 to June 29.', 'print_section': 'C', 'print_page': '3', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Samuel Beckett at 59E59 Theaters', 'kicker': 'Arts, Briefly', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Samuel Beckett At 59E59 Theaters', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Theater', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'McElroy, Steven', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Beckett, Samuel', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': '59E59 THEATERS', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Nunn, Trevor', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Atkins, Eileen', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Gambon, Michael', 'rank': 7, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2013-08-05T13:00:05+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Culture', 'section_name': 'Theater', 'byline': {'original': 'By Steven McElroy', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Steven', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'McElroy', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Brief', '_id': 'nyt://article/28387690-22c2-58b7-9f6d-b8911db64bf6', 'word_count': 259, 'uri': 'nyt://article/28387690-22c2-58b7-9f6d-b8911db64bf6'}, {'abstract': 'The composer Alan Menken, who recently received his first Emmy nomination, reflects on winning Grammys, Oscars, a Tony and a Razzie for his contributions to works like “The Little Mermaid,” “Aladdin” and “Newsies.”', 'web_url': 'https://artsbeat.blogs.nytimes.com/2013/08/05/alan-menken-goes-for-the-egot-and-the-regot/', 'snippet': 'The composer Alan Menken, who recently received his first Emmy nomination, reflects on winning Grammys, Oscars, a Tony and a Razzie for his contributions to works like “The Little Mermaid,” “Aladdin” and “Newsies.”', 'lead_paragraph': 'Few composers are more decorated than Alan Menken, a winner of eight Academy Awards for the songs and scores he has helped create for the animated features “The Little Mermaid,” “Beauty and the Beast,” “Aladdin” and “Pocahontas.” He has 11 Grammys, too, for his work on those films and on “Tangled,” and a Tony for his score of Broadway’s “Newsies the Musical.” (The original film from which that show is adapted also nabbed Mr. Menken a Golden Raspberry Award — better known as a Razzie — for worst original song.)', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/08/04/arts/menken/menken-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 450, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2013/08/04/arts/menken/menken-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 450}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/08/04/arts/menken/menken-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 768, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/08/04/arts/menken/menken-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 1536, 'width': 2048, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/08/04/arts/menken/menken-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2013/08/04/arts/menken/menken-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/08/04/arts/menken/menken-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Alan Menken Goes for the EGOT (and the REGOT)', 'kicker': 'ArtsBeat', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': 