on': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/53dcdb2d-021f-57d1-bf83-622beccca7ae', 'word_count': 3614, 'uri': 'nyt://article/53dcdb2d-021f-57d1-bf83-622beccca7ae'}, {'abstract': 'The Kremlin expands such resources on fanning hatred that there is no thing as a protected space for gays, lesbians or the otherwise different in Russia.', 'web_url': 'https://latitude.blogs.nytimes.com/2013/08/05/gay-bashing-inside-the-garden-ring/', 'snippet': 'The Kremlin expands such resources on fanning hatred that there is no thing as a protected space for gays, lesbians or the otherwise different in Russia.', 'lead_paragraph': 'MOSCOW — After she got home at 5 a.m. Vera Kichanova wrote on Facebook,“I thought all those awful homophobia stories happened outside the Garden Ring,” referring to the avenue that circles central Moscow. “Inside the Garden Ring” is a geographic and social appellation: With its overpriced designer apartments, luxury boutiques and at least a couple of coffee shops on every block, the area is generally considered a world onto itself. Some use the term pejoratively, to indicate rich people who are out of touch with reality; others use it to denote a perceived protected space inside an increasingly reactionary country.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Gay Bashing Inside the Garden Ring', 'kicker': 'Latitude', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Discrimination', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Homosexuality', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Moscow (Russia)', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2013-08-05T16:06:54+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Opinion', 'byline': {'original': 'By Masha Gessen', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Masha', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Gessen', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/b56ff280-d9cc-5e63-a1a4-383aa96e5a89', 'word_count': 701, 'uri': 'nyt://article/b56ff280-d9cc-5e63-a1a4-383aa96e5a89'}, {'abstract': 'A selection of travel headlines from around the Web.', 'web_url': 'https://intransit.blogs.nytimes.com/2013/08/05/walkabout-thai-tourisms-wide-net-jetblues-premium-side/', 'snippet': 'A selection of travel headlines from around the Web.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Thai Tourism’s Wide Net\xa0In stark comparison to many of its neighboring countries,\xa0Thailand is marketing itself to a wide variety of tourists, including gay and Muslim travelers. (New York Times)', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Walkabout: Thai Tourism’s Wide Net; JetBlue’s Premium Side', 'kicker': 'In Transit', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Travel and Vacations', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2013-08-05T16:32:16+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Travel', 'byline': {'original': 'By The New York Times', 'person': [], 'organization': 'The New York Times'}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/a9ba997d-45dd-549a-a697-7a26649b8d0c', 'word_count': 145, 'uri': 'nyt://article/a9ba997d-45dd-549a-a697-7a26649b8d0c'}, {'abstract': 'How Sri Lanka’s president, Mahinda Rajapaksa, used cricket and construction to change his hometown.', 'web_url': 'https://india.blogs.nytimes.com/2013/08/05/cricket-and-power-politics-in-sri-lanka/', 'snippet': 'How Sri Lanka’s president, Mahinda Rajapaksa, used cricket and construction to change his hometown.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Until a few years ago, Hambantota was an obscure town in south-central Sri Lanka, around 190 kilometers (118 miles)\xa0 from the capital Colombo, with a population that barely exceeded 10,000. Its daily rhythms were defined by the twin activities of farming and fishing. But once Mahinda Rajapaksa, one of its native sons, became president in 2005, all that began to change.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/08/05/world/asia/5-Sri-Lanka-IndiaInk/5-Sri-Lanka-IndiaInk-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 323, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2013/08/05/world/asia/5-Sri-Lanka-IndiaInk/5-Sri-Lanka-IndiaInk-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 323}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/08/05/world/asia/5-Sri-Lanka-IndiaInk/5-Sri-Lanka-IndiaInk-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 551, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/08/05/world/asia/5-Sri-Lanka-IndiaInk/5-Sri-Lanka-IndiaInk-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 671, 'width': 1248, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/08/05/world/asia/5-Sri-Lanka-IndiaInk/5-Sri-Lanka-IndiaInk-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2013/08/05/world/asia/5-Sri-Lanka-IndiaInk/5-Sri-Lanka-IndiaInk-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/08/05/world/asia/5-Sri-Lanka-IndiaInk/5-Sri-Lanka-IndiaInk-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Cricket and Power Politics in Sri Lanka', 'kicker': 'India Ink', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [], 'pub_date': '2013-08-05T16:44:46+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'World', 'subsection_name': 'Asia Pacific', 'byline': {'original': 'By Vaibhav Vats', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Vaibhav', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Vats', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/c153fdf4-34e2-56e2-88cb-5bf378e1d67e', 'word_count': 1374, 'uri': 'nyt://article/c153fdf4-34e2-56e2-88cb-5bf378e1d67e'}, {'abstract': 'The stories behind two unforgettable images of the Civil War.', 'web_url': 'https://opinionator.blogs.nytimes.com/2013/08/05/icons-of-cruelty/', 'snippet': 'The stories behind two unforgettable images of the Civil War.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Two iconic photographs of former slaves documenting the torture inflicted on them by their owners were widely circulated during the Civil War as anti-slavery propaganda, and both appear in the current exhibit “Photography and the American Civil War” at the Metropolitan Museum of Art. Although the images were extensively reproduced and helped to turn public opinion against slavery, the stories of the two men in these shocking photographs are little known today.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Icons of Cruelty', 'kicker': 'Disunion', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Civil War (US) (1861-65)', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Photography', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Slavery', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2013-08-05T16:45:57+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Opinion', 'byline': {'original': 'By Joan Paulson Gage', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Joan', 'middlename': 'Paulson', 'lastname': 'Gage', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/3ec4b304-20af-5119-b969-e8957adfe6a2', 'word_count': 1205, 'uri': 'nyt://article/3ec4b304-20af-5119-b969-e8957adfe6a2'}, {'abstract': 'The insider trading case against an analyst who tipped off a trader at SAC Capital Advisors about Microsoft-Yahoo talks in 2009 is not typical because he also shared the information with others, possibly lifting the veil of confidentiality.', 'web_url': 'https://dealbook.nytimes.com/2013/08/05/the-sometimes-gray-line-of-confidential-information/', 'snippet': 'The insider trading case against an analyst who tipped off a trader at SAC Capital Advisors about Microsoft-Yahoo talks in 2009 is not typical because he also shared the information with others, possibly lifting the veil of confidentiality.', 'lead_paragraph': 'The insider trading case against SAC Capital Advisors seems to be all about the trading “edge,” a term the firm’s owner, Steven A. Cohen, once said he hated. Under the securities laws, proving a violation requires showing that the information used was nonpublic, which means it was not already available in the stock market — the edge that can make for lucrative profits and sidestepping big losses.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'The Gray Line of ‘Confidential’ Information', 'kicker': 'DealBook', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Hedge Funds', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Insider Trading', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Mergers, Acquisitions and Divestitures', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Securities and Commodities Violations', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Aggarwal, Sandeep', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'pers