', 'news_desk': 'Science', 'section_name': 'Science', 'byline': {'original': 'By Douglas Quenqua', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Douglas', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Quenqua', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/50de78df-bb9a-55c4-839b-6439099c08f8', 'word_count': 644, 'uri': 'nyt://article/50de78df-bb9a-55c4-839b-6439099c08f8'}, {'abstract': 'Mike Escamilla doesn’t just jump out of planes; he takes stunning pictures of himself while doing it.', 'web_url': 'https://6thfloor.blogs.nytimes.com/2013/08/05/gnarly-moments-in-the-life-of-an-extreme-photographer/', 'snippet': 'Mike Escamilla doesn’t just jump out of planes; he takes stunning pictures of himself while doing it.', 'lead_paragraph': 'The photographer Mike Escamilla describes himself as a stuntman, professional BMX rider, actor, photographer, filmmaker, director, producer and designer. (He’s also a former X-Games competitor. In 2005 he set world records for the longest back flip and longest 360 on a BMX bike.) And he keeps an itinerary that would exhaust many photojournalists (and make many more envious);\xa0since May of this year\xa0he has been to Spain, Denmark, Sweden, Finland, Russia, Estonia, Latvia, Puerto Rico, Dominica, Ecuador, Germany, San Francisco and Colorado. When I came across his images on Instagram, it was clear that they were shot by someone with daring and an eye for unusual angles, giving new definition to Robert Capa’s famous advice to photographers: “If your pictures aren’t good enough, you aren’t close enough.”', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/08/02/magazine/01-escamilla-photos-slide-E3XO/01-escamilla-photos-slide-E3XO-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 600, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/201