Ben', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Ratliff', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 3}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Review', '_id': 'nyt://article/f5428ad5-6f6a-5c4b-8224-ca28936e0c81', 'word_count': 1102, 'uri': 'nyt://article/f5428ad5-6f6a-5c4b-8224-ca28936e0c81'}, {'abstract': 'A new show at the Los Angeles County Museum of Art examines the diverse legacy of a multifarious artist.', 'web_url': 'https://tmagazine.blogs.nytimes.com/2013/08/05/on-view-a-reconsideration-of-katue-kitasono-japans-great-poet-of-surrealism/', 'snippet': 'A new show at the Los Angeles County Museum of Art examines the diverse legacy of a multifarious artist.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Until recently, Japan’s artistic reputation in the United States was limited mainly to the very old and the very new: Edo-period ceramics and paintings, and the manga-inspired “Superflat” movement of the 1990s that was ushered in by Takashi Murakami. Recently, however, American curators have been taking stock of Japan’s compelling postwar output with 2013 exhibits like “Tokyo 1955-1970: A New Avant-Garde” at MoMA, the Guggenheim’s “Gutai: Splendid Playground,” about the progressive art collective that operated in the Kansai region, and last year’s Yayoi Kusama pop art extravaganza at the Whitney.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'A Reconsideration of Katue Kitasono, Japan’s Great Poet of Surrealism', 'kicker': 'T Magazine', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Art', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Museums', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Los Angeles County Museum of Art', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Japan', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Los Angeles (Calif)', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2013-08-05T22:16:00+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'TStyle', 'section_name': 'T Magazine', 'byline': {'original': 'By Maxwell Williams', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Maxwell', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Williams', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/515f633d-16d1-5354-b550-a989cd97755b', 'word_count': 395, 'uri': 'nyt://article/515f633d-16d1-5354-b550-a989cd97755b'}, {'abstract': 'The New Black Fest will bring together writers to produce short works about race and privilege in the United States.', 'web_url': 'https://artsbeat.blogs.nytimes.com/2013/08/05/festival-of-short-plays-inspired-by-trayvon-martin-case/', 'snippet': 'The New Black Fest will bring together writers to produce short works about race and privilege in the United States.', 'lead_paragraph': 'The New Black Fest, which supports innovative plays by and about black people, has brought together seven playwrights to create six plays about race and privilege in the United States in the wake of the Trayvon Martin case.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Festival of Short Plays Inspired by Trayvon Martin Case', 'kicker': 'ArtsBeat', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [], 'pub_date': '2013-08-05T22:21:29+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Arts', 'byline': {'original': 'By Felicia R. Lee', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Felicia', 'middlename': 'R.', 'lastname': 'Lee', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/f3ad6217-6b78-5a3a-8251-0ede9205ed6e', 'word_count': 307, 'uri': 'nyt://article/f3ad6217-6b78-5a3a-8251-0ede9205ed6e'}, {'abstract': 'Anita Lo, chef and owner of Annisa in Greenwich Village, visits Flushing, Queens, to sample d