 'news_desk': 'Fashion & Style', 'section_name': 'Fashion & Style', 'byline': {'original': '', 'person': [], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Slideshow', '_id': 'nyt://slideshow/c6acec87-d462-5b41-abd1-369b0cc04c4c', 'word_count': 0, 'uri': 'nyt://slideshow/c6acec87-d462-5b41-abd1-369b0cc04c4c'}, {'abstract': 'In a new biography, Jeff Guinn provides a more probing view of the cult leader Charles Manson by delving deeper into his past and deeply troubled childhood.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2013/08/07/books/a-new-look-at-charles-manson-by-jeff-guinn.html', 'snippet': 'In a new biography, Jeff Guinn provides a more probing view of the cult leader Charles Manson by delving deeper into his past and deeply troubled childhood.', 'lead_paragraph': '“Little Charlie Manson was a disagreeable child,” Jeff Guinn writes in “Manson,” an otherwise brawny, deep-digging biography that’s much more riveting than might be expected. It’s no surprise that the Manson story no longer commands much attention. What a difference more than four decades make. Mr. Guinn is fascinating in his use of hindsight, and it allows him a more probing view of his subject than earlier biographers had. The book’s essential question is articulated in this opening quotation about the ’60s, which he attributes to Tom Hayden: “Over and over it came down to that question — what was reality in an unreal time?” ', 'print_section': 'C', 'print_page': '1', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/08/07/arts/07BOOK/07BOOK-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 939, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2013/08/07/arts/07BOOK/07BOOK-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 939}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/08/07/arts/07BOOK/07BOOK-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 102