2:34+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Metro', 'section_name': 'New York', 'byline': {'original': 'By Thomas Kaplan and Sarah Maslin Nir', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Thomas', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Kaplan', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}, {'firstname': 'Sarah', 'middlename': 'Maslin', 'lastname': 'Nir', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 2}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/24b5737f-6791-5ffa-8133-b15dc0132615', 'word_count': 355, 'uri': 'nyt://article/24b5737f-6791-5ffa-8133-b15dc0132615'}, {'abstract': 'Four people pretending to be a family smuggle marijuana from Mexico to Colorado in “We’re the Millers.”', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2013/08/07/movies/were-the-millers-with-jennifer-aniston-and-jason-sudeikis.html', 'snippet': 'Four people pretending to be a family smuggle marijuana from Mexico to Colorado in “We’re the Millers.”', 'lead_paragraph': '“We’re the Millers,” a loose, halfheartedly raunchy, occasionally hilarious new comedy, is about a lot of different things; it’s the usual grab bag of jokes about drugs and body parts. But what really drives the movie is its own search for something to make fun of, and for a comic style that can feel credibly naughty while remaining ultimately safe and affirmative. ', 'print_section': 'C', 'print_page': '5', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/08/07/arts/07MILLERS/07MILLERS-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 388, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2013/08/07/arts/07MILLERS/07MILLERS-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 388}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/08/07/arts/07MILLERS/07MILLERS-jumbo.jpg'