', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'New York City', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2013-08-07T00:57:01+0000', 'document_type': 'multimedia', 'news_desk': 'Opinion', 'section_name': 'Opinion', 'byline': {'original': 'Alison Klayman', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Alison', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Klayman', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Video', '_id': 'nyt://video/6d6a58a3-7952-5e1d-bc30-afe7fe3dabd3', 'word_count': 0, 'uri': 'nyt://video/6d6a58a3-7952-5e1d-bc30-afe7fe3dabd3'}, {'abstract': 'Lawmakers are meddling in the affairs of this important financial institution that serves millions of rural women.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2013/08/07/opinion/bangladesh-takes-aim-at-grameen-bank.html', 'snippet': 'Lawmakers are meddling in the affairs of this important financial institution that serves millions of rural women.', 'lead_paragraph': 'The government of Bangladesh is considering nationalizing and breaking up the widely admired Grameen Bank, which pioneered the business of lending small amounts of money to poor women who want to start and grow businesses. Lawmakers should reject these destructive ideas and stop meddling in the affairs of this important financial institution, which serves 8.4 million rural women.', 'print_section': 'A', 'print_page': '22', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Bangladesh Takes Aim at Grameen Bank', 'kicker': 'Editorial', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Bangladesh Takes Aim at Grameen Bank', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Grameen Bank', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Bangladesh', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Yunus, Muhammad', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Editorials', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Hasina, Sheikh', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2013-08-07T00:57:15+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Editorial', 'section_name': 'Opinion', 'byline': {'original': 'By The Editorial Board', 'person': [], 'organization': 'The Editorial Board'}, 'type_of_material': 'Editorial', '_id': 'nyt://article/20951d7f-1527-5abc-910a-62297910e30a', 'word_count': 424, 'uri': 'nyt://article/20951d7f-1527-5abc-910a-62297910e30a'}, {'abstract': 'Only a few states have acted this year to stanch the flood of illegal guns.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2013/08/07/opinion/the-iron-pipeline-thrives.html', 'snippet': 'Only a few states have acted this year to stanch the flood of illegal guns.', 'lead_paragraph': 'When somebody pulls a gun and commits a crime in New York City, that weapon almost certainly comes from somewhere else. Mayor Michael Bloomberg announced last week that 90 percent of the guns used to commit crimes were purchased in other states, according to the latest data from 2011, compared with 85 percent in 2009. This “iron pipeline,” as this illegal trade is called, is a booming and deadly business.', 'print_section': 'A', 'print_page': '22', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'The Iron Pipeline Thrives', 'kicker': 'Editorial', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'The Iron Pipeline Thrives', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Gun Control', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'States (US)', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Newtown, Conn, Shooting (2012)', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Law and Legislation', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Editorials', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2013-08-07T00:57:15+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Editorial', 'section_name': 'Opinion', 'byline': {'original': 'By The Editorial Board', 'p