By The New York Times', 'person': [], 'organization': 'The New York Times'}, 'type_of_material': 'Question', '_id': 'nyt://article/3a096b69-8c36-5329-a321-d47380cac077', 'word_count': 2071, 'uri': 'nyt://article/3a096b69-8c36-5329-a321-d47380cac077'}, {'abstract': 'Two buyout bids illustrate the rising tide of shareholder power. | The Justice Department sues Bank of America over mortgage securities. | It would not be surprising to see Jeffrey P. Bezos worry little about turning a quick profit at The Washington Post. | Cory A. Booker has obtained money for an Internet start-up from influential investors.', 'web_url': 'https://dealbook.nytimes.com/2013/08/07/morning-agenda-devilish-issues-of-management-led-buyouts/', 'snippet': 'Two buyout bids illustrate the rising tide of shareholder power. | The Justice Department sues Bank of America over mortgage securities. | It would not be surprising to see Jeffrey P. Bezos worry little about turning a quick profit at The Washingt...', 'lead_paragraph': 'The $612 million bid by the Weiss family to buy the American Greetings Corporation, the greeting card company the family controls, is not as well known as the headline-grabbing $24.4 billion bid by Michael S. Dell and the private equity firm Silver Lake to buy the computer company Mr. Dell founded. But both deals “illustrate the rising tide of shareholder power, as well as the devilish issues that emerge when management tries to buy a public company,” Steven M. Davidoff writes in the Deal Professor column. “And even though Dell’s board has changed the voting rules, both companies are being tested by shareholders who view the buyouts as undervaluing their companies.”', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Devilish Issues of Management-Led Buyouts', 'kicker': 'DealBook', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [], 'pub_date': '2013-08-07T12:22:06+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Business Day', 'byline': {'original': 'By William Alden', 'person': [{'firstname': 'William', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Alden', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/30e19b7e-456d-5a7a-b19c-f4f885ee4e7e', 'word_count': 1757, 'uri': 'nyt://article/30e19b7e-456d-5a7a-b19c-f4f885ee4e7e'}, {'abstract': 'AOL agreed on Wednesday to buy Adap.tv, a video advertising platform, for about $405 million in cash and stock, in its biggest acquisition since becoming an independent company.', 'web_url': 'https://dealbook.nytimes.com/2013/08/07/aol-to-buy-adap-tv-a-video-ad-platform-for-405-million/', 'snippet': 'AOL agreed on Wednesday to buy Adap.tv, a video advertising platform, for about $405 million in cash and stock, in its biggest acquisition since becoming an independent company.', 'lead_paragraph': 'AOL agreed on Wednesday to buy Adap.tv, a video advertising platform, for about $405 million in cash and stock, in its biggest acquisition since becoming an independent company.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'AOL to Buy Adap.tv, a Video Ad Platform, for $405 Million', 'kicker': 'DealBook', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'ADVERTISING AND MARKETING', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Mergers, Acquisitions and Divestitures', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Online Advertising', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Armstrong, Tim', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'AOL Inc', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Spark Capital', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2013-08-07T12:24:04+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Business Day', 'byline