on_name': 'Global Business', 'byline': {'original': 'By Amy Qin and Edward Wong', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Amy', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Qin', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}, {'firstname': 'Edward', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Wong', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 2}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/5d1015a3-ac71-5358-8969-713b52302194', 'word_count': 879, 'uri': 'nyt://article/5d1015a3-ac71-5358-8969-713b52302194'}, {'abstract': 'After plugging our information into the Economic Policy Institute’s budget calculator, I was stunned. The rent estimate was half what we pay, the grocery stipend looked like a typo, and the child-care cost was a fantasy.', 'web_url': 'https://parenting.blogs.nytimes.com/2013/08/07/the-price-of-urban-family-living/', 'snippet': 'After plugging our information into the Economic Policy Institute’s budget calculator, I was stunned. The rent estimate was half what we pay, the grocery stipend looked like a typo, and the child-care cost was a fantasy.', 'lead_paragraph': 'When the Economic Policy Institute released a budget calculator last month to help estimate the income families need to live modestly, I could not resist entering our statistics. As a young family of four living in the pricey San Francisco Bay Area, we are constantly juggling our monthly expenses.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'The Price of Urban Family Living', 'kicker': 'Motherlode', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Budgets and Budgeting', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Child Care', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Children and Childhood', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Families and Family Life', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Parenting', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Economic Policy Institute', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Berkeley (Calif)', 'rank': 7, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2013-08-07T14:31:02+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Style', 'byline': {'original': 'By Aimee Phan', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Aimee', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Phan', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/209b79b5-9279-58b2-aa31-949c182eebe9', 'word_count': 828, 'uri': 'nyt://article/209b79b5-9279-58b2-aa31-949c182eebe9'}, {'abstract': 'The success of Lending Club, which links up borrowers with investors who are willing to make loans, has spawned imitators and venture capital backers that are looking for similar results.', 'web_url': 'https://dealbook.nytimes.com/2013/08/07/not-banks-but-still-lending-and-drawing-investors/', 'snippet': 'The success of Lending Club, which links up borrowers with investors who are willing to make loans, has spawned imitators and venture capital backers that are looking for similar results.', 'lead_paragraph': 'In one sense, much of Internet commerce is largely a matchmaking service, connecting people looking for relationships, taxis or spare bedrooms. One of the hottest areas is the market for personal loans, which is attracting money from venture capital investors, spurred by the fast growth of the largest online platform, Lending Club, and the paper profits for its early backers.', 'print_section': 'B', 'print_page': '5', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Not Banks, but Still Lending Money and Drawing Investors', 'kicker': 'DealBook', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Not Banks, but Still Lending Money and Drawing Investors', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None