ation': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Question', '_id': 'nyt://article/1ef19095-d3de-50f6-8873-d138188a6302', 'word_count': 466, 'uri': 'nyt://article/1ef19095-d3de-50f6-8873-d138188a6302'}, {'abstract': 'With the breadbox poised for a revival, the MoMA Store brings back a classic.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2013/08/08/garden/the-retro-breadbox-is-stale-no-more.html', 'snippet': 'With the breadbox poised for a revival, the MoMA Store brings back a classic.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Many baby boomers have fond memories of their parents’ kitchen gadgets: rotary egg beaters, electric percolators, Salton Hot Trays and various kinds of breadboxes. Countertop containers for baked goods were rife in the ’50s and ’60s, but they took on a minor role with the rise of commercially produced bread wrapped in plastic and loaded with preservatives. Now, with artisanal bread a staple in many homes, the breadbox is poised for a revival. Wesco, a German company, has reintroduced its Bauhaus-style Grandy model, designed in 1946 by Josef Neuhaus. Made of powder-coated steel, it has ventilation holes in the back, nonslip feet and a metal handle and hinges. The 16-inch-wide box comes in a glossy apple red from the Museum of Modern Art store, for $100. Available on Aug. 15 (orders starting Friday): (800) 851-4509 or momastore.org.\xa0', 'print_section': 'D', 'print_page': '3', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/08/08/garden/08GOODS_SPAN/CURR-2-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 377, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2013/08/08/garden/08GOODS_SPAN/CURR-2-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 377}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/08/08/garden/08GOODS_SPAN/CURR-2-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 643, 'width': 1024,