03:53+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Home', 'section_name': 'Home & Garden', 'byline': {'original': 'By Joanne Furio', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Joanne', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Furio', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/acf02c1b-971a-541d-a011-bb0386bdae32', 'word_count': 127, 'uri': 'nyt://article/acf02c1b-971a-541d-a011-bb0386bdae32'}, {'abstract': 'In search of solitude? The Sawtooths offer natural splendor without the crowds — and you don’t even have to pitch a tent.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2013/08/11/travel/your-own-private-idaho.html', 'snippet': 'In search of solitude? The Sawtooths offer natural splendor without the crowds — and you don’t even have to pitch a tent.', 'lead_paragraph': 'The “Entering Stanley, Idaho” sign seemed more like a friendly warning than a welcome. “Population 63,” it read, as if to say: Congratulations, you’ve made it to the middle of nowhere. Stanley is the entry point to the Sawtooth Valley, a time warp of a place with four saloons, five mountain ranges and not much else. My husband, Josh, our two children and I had driven three hours from Boise along an empty, winding two-lane scenic byway for a week of summer adventure. Still, as we strolled down deserted, dusty Wall Street looking for a lunch spot, it was hard not to wonder: Where is everyone?', 'print_section': 'TR', 'print_page': '10', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/08/11/travel/11EXPLORER_SPAN/11EXPLORER_SPAN-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 387, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2013/08/11/travel/11EXPLORER_SPAN/11EXPLORER_SPAN-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 387}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'cre