 'byline': {'original': 'By Steven Petrow', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Steven', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Petrow', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/f7fffb5d-ade0-5147-b5af-dec407ec9f40', 'word_count': 1513, 'uri': 'nyt://article/f7fffb5d-ade0-5147-b5af-dec407ec9f40'}, {'abstract': 'In David Gordon Green’s offbeat film “Prince Avalanche,” two young men — one pompous, one slow on the uptake — take a job painting lines on a stretch of highway.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2013/08/09/movies/prince-avalanche-features-paul-rudd-and-emile-hirsch.html', 'snippet': 'In David Gordon Green’s offbeat film “Prince Avalanche,” two young men — one pompous, one slow on the uptake — take a job painting lines on a stretch of highway.', 'lead_paragraph': 'David Gordon Green, who is 38 and has directed eight feature films, is a relatively young man in no particular hurry. In a career that has ambled from sensitive regional realism (“George Washington”) to Southern Gothic (“Undertow”) to stoner comedy (“Pineapple Express,” “The Sitter”), he has shown a notable knack for taking his time, pausing to appreciate offbeat moments and unusual sights. He is a hard-working filmmaker — with some interesting television on his résumé as well — who rigorously communicates the pleasures of slacking. ', 'print_section': 'C', 'print_page': '6', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/08/09/arts/0809PRINCE/0809PRINCE-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 600, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2013/08/09/arts/0809PRINCE/0809PRINCE-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 600}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/08/09/arts/0809P