them have breakfast each morning in the same cafe:', 'print_section': 'BR', 'print_page': '1', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/08/11/books/review/0811-bks-STAUBYNsub/0811-bks-STAUBYNsub-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 806, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2013/08/11/books/review/0811-bks-STAUBYNsub/0811-bks-STAUBYNsub-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 806}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/08/11/books/review/0811-bks-STAUBYNsub/0811-bks-STAUBYNsub-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 1024, 'width': 763, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/08/11/books/review/0811-bks-STAUBYNsub/0811-bks-STAUBYNsub-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 2048, 'width': 1525, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/08/11/books/review/0811-bks-STAUBYNsub/0811-bks-STAUBYNsub-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2013/08/11/books/review/0811-bks-STAUBYNsub/0811-bks-STAUBYNsub-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/08/11/books/review/0811-bks-STAUBYNsub/0811-bks-STAUBYNsub-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Taken to Heart', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Taken To Heart', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Marias, Javier', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Books and Literature', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2013-08-08T19:23:45+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'BookReview', 'section_name': 'Books', 'subsection_name': 'Book Review', 'byline': {'original': 'By Edward St. Aubyn', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Edward', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'St', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Review', '_id': 'nyt://article/2d914d88-d8b0-5535-96bb-b4132d9e1c3a', 'word_count': 1870, 'uri': 'nyt://article/2d914d88-d8b0-5535-96bb-b4132d9e1c3a'}, {'abstract': 'Historians have never known what to make of this scholar, writer, soldier and imperialist.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2013/08/11/books/review/lawrence-in-arabia-by-scott-anderson.html', 'snippet': 'Historians have never known what to make of this scholar, writer, soldier and imperialist.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Among the many individual stories of World War I that will doubtless be told and retold for the centenary years between 2014 and 2018, that of T. E. Lawrence stands out from all the rest. As Scott Anderson says at the beginning of “Lawrence in Arabia,” “historians have never quite decided what to make of the young, bashful Oxford scholar who rode into battle at the head of an Arab army and changed history.”', 'print_section': 'BR', 'print_page': '16', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/08/11/books/review/11VONTUNZELMAN/11VONTUNZELMAN-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 857, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2013/08/11/books/review/11VONTUNZELMAN/11VONTUNZELMAN-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 857}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 