[{'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'National Security Agency', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Surveillance of Citizens by Government', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Foreign Intelligence Surveillance Court', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'United States Politics and Government', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Editorials', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Privacy', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2013-08-09T01:11:18+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Editorial', 'section_name': 'Opinion', 'byline': {'original': 'By The Editorial Board', 'person': [], 'organization': 'The Editorial Board'}, 'type_of_material': 'Editorial', '_id': 'nyt://article/f899ecae-58a1-5f59-9c30-6ff574165fff', 'word_count': 432, 'uri': 'nyt://article/f899ecae-58a1-5f59-9c30-6ff574165fff'}, {'abstract': 'The Justice Department wants to restore competition in the e-book market. But it has not addressed the bigger anticompetitive force that is Amazon.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2013/08/09/opinion/apple-pays-dearly-for-price-fixing.html', 'snippet': 'The Justice Department wants to restore competition in the e-book market. But it has not addressed the bigger anticompetitive force that is Amazon.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Last month the Justice Department won an antitrust suit against Apple, convincing Judge Denise Cote of United States District Court that the company had colluded with five major publishers to fix e-book prices. At a hearing on Friday, the department will argue that its plan to remedy Apple’s misconduct will “restore lost competition.” In a narrow sense it may, but the problem with this case all along was that the department ignored the potentially bigger anticompetitive force in the e-book market — Amazon — while focusing on Apple. ', 'print_section': 'A', 'print_page': '18', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Apple Pays Dearly for Price-Fixing', 'kicker': 'Editorial', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Apple Pays Dearly for Price-Fixing', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Apple Inc', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'E-Books and Readers', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Antitrust Laws and Competition Issues', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'AMAZON.COM INC', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Justice Department', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Cote, Denise', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Editorials', 'rank': 7, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2013-08-09T01:11:20+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Editorial', 'section_name': 'Opinion', 'byline': {'original': 'By The Editorial Board', 'person': [], 'organization': 'The Editorial Board'}, 'type_of_material': 'Editorial', '_id': 'nyt://article/6461552a-d0e4-500c-a864-598dce0f7161', 'word_count': 357, 'uri': 'nyt://article/6461552a-d0e4-500c-a864-598dce0f7161'}, {'abstract': 'After talks on a compromise collapsed, worse bloodshed is possible.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2013/08/09/opinion/egypt-on-the-edge.html', 'snippet': 'After talks on a compromise collapsed, worse bloodshed is possible.', 'lead_paragraph': 'It’s hard to imagine things getting worse in Egypt, but they could. The generals who now call the shots in the world’s leading Arab country and their handpicked civilian government have halted efforts to reach a compromise with the Islamist supporters of the man they ousted — Mohamed Morsi, the country’s first democratically elected president. Instead, they have threatened to forcibly disperse tens of thousands of pro-Morsi civilians from two sit-ins in Cairo.', 'print_section': 'A', 'print_page': '18', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {