arlotte’s Web.” More than a decade earlier, librarians bet that the same principle might apply to illustrators when on June 24, 1937, a committee of the American Library Association met at the Pierre Hotel in New York to lay plans for an annual award honoring the year’s most distinguished contribution to children’s book illustration by an artist who is an American citizen or resident — the Randolph Caldecott Medal.', 'print_section': 'BR', 'print_page': '26', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/08/11/books/review/0811-bks-MARCUS/0811-bks-MARCUS-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 768, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2013/08/11/books/review/0811-bks-MARCUS/0811-bks-MARCUS-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 768}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/08/11/books/review/0811-bks-MARCUS/0811-bks-MARCUS-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 1024, 'width': 800, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/08/11/books/review/0811-bks-MARCUS/0811-bks-MARCUS-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 1237, 'width': 966, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/08/11/books/review/0811-bks-MARCUS/0811-bks-MARCUS-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2013/08/11/books/review/0811-bks-MARCUS/0811-bks-MARCUS-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/08/11/books/review/0811-bks-MARCUS/0811-bks-MARCUS-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Seal of Approval', 'kicker': 'Essay', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Seal of Approval', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Caldecott, Randolph', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Marcus, Leonard S', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Caldecott Medal', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Books and Literature', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2013-08-09T14:52:51+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'BookReview', 'section_name': 'Books', 'subsection_name': 'Book Review', 'byline': {'original': 'By Leonard S. Marcus', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Leonard', 'middlename': 'S.', 'lastname': 'Marcus', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/1e6061f3-332f-52c4-9968-4e2c5e047316', 'word_count': 1158, 'uri': 'nyt://article/1e6061f3-332f-52c4-9968-4e2c5e047316'}, {'abstract': 'The seeming randomness of literary awards torments some writers, driving them into fits of paranoia.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2013/08/11/books/review/midlist-crisis.html', 'snippet': 'The seeming randomness of literary awards torments some writers, driving them into fits of paranoia.', 'lead_paragraph': 'There is nothing more becoming in an author than modesty. Regardless of what egotistic airs they may put on, most writers, I suspect, have a fairly accurate assessment of their own worth and achievement. Still, they — we — are dependent to an uncomfortable extent on the world’s judgment, perhaps hoping that by some fluke the world will rate us higher than we rate ourselves. The problem is not that the world so often ignores literary effort (though that is certainly the case), but that to the degree it breaks its silence it tends to distribute the rewards in a mystifyingly erratic manner. ', 'print_section': 'BR', 'print_page': '27', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/08/11/books/review/0811-bks-LOPATE/0811-bks-LOPATE-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 162, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2013/08/11/books/review/0811-bks-LOPATE/0811-bks-LOPATE-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 162}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/08/11/books/review/0811-bks-LOPATE/0811-bks-LOPATE-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 276, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/08/11/books/review/0811-bks-LOPATE/0811-bks-LOPATE-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 552, 'width': 2048, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/08/11/books/review/0811-bks-LOPATE/0811-bks-LOPATE-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2013/08/11/books/review/0811-bks-LOPATE/0811-bks-LOPATE-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/08/11/books/review/0811-bks-LOPATE/0811-bks-LOPATE-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Midlist Crisis', 'kicker': 'Essay', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Midlist Crisis', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Books and Literature', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Writing and Writers', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2013-08-09T14:53:20+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'BookReview', 'section_name': 'Books', 'subsection_name': 'Book Review', 'byline': {'original': 'By Phillip Lopate', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Phillip', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Lopate', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/969e3c34-47c8-5926-b1c3-4d10669fed22', 'word_count': 1159, 'uri': 'nyt://article/969e3c34-47c8-5926-b1c3-4d10669fed22'}, {'abstract': 'Paperback books of particular interest.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2013/08/11/books/review/paperback-row.html', 'snippet': 'Paperback books of particular interest.', 'lead_paragraph': 'TOBY’S ROOM,  by Pat Barker. (Anchor, $15.95.) Barker has traced the psychic reverberations of World War I through a remarkable series of novels: the “Regeneration” trilogy, “Life Class” and now “Toby’s Room.” A sequel to “Life Class,” “Toby’s Room” portrays a group of students at the Slade School of Fine Art in London; at its center is Elinor Brooke, a painter who works as a medical illustrator while unraveling the mystery of what happened to her brother, Toby, a doctor serving at the front.', 'print_section': 'BR', 'print_page': '24', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Paperback Row', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Paperback Row', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Books and Literature', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2013-08-09T14:57:32+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'BookReview', 'section_name': 'Books', 'subsection_name': 'Book Review', 'byline': {'original': 'By Ihsan Taylor', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Ihsan', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Taylor', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'List', '_id': 'nyt://article/1347ea4e-bfaa-538c-a685-a04e74afc6a7', 'word_count': 511, 'uri': 'nyt://article/1347ea4e-bfaa-538c-a685-a04e74afc6a7'}, {'abstract': 'Recently reviewed books of particular interest.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2013/08/11/books/review/editors-choice.html', 'snippet': 'Recently reviewed books of particular interest.', 'lead_paragraph': 'THE SOUND OF THINGS FALLING, by Juan Gabriel Vásquez. Translated by Anne McLean. (Riverhead, $27.95.) This gripping Colombian novel, built on the country’s tragic history with the drug trade, meditates on love, fate and death.', 'print_section': 'BR', 'print_page': '22', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Editors’ Choice', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Editors’ Choice | Recent Books of Particular Interest', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Books and Literature', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2013-08-09T14:57:49+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'BookReview', 'section_name': 'Books', 'subsection_name': 'Book Review', 'byline': {'original': '', 'person': [], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'List', '_id': 'nyt://article/4e2c2b4a-e436-5d59-899f-3993eeb33217', 'word_count': 250, 'uri': 'nyt://article/4e2c2b4a-e436-5d59-899f-3993eeb33217'}, {'abstract': 'A family tragedy in the Arizona desert split Justin St. Germain’s world into before and after.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2013/08/11/books/review/justin-st-germains-son-of-a-gun.html', 'snippet': 'A family tragedy in the Arizona desert split Justin St. Germain’s world into before and after.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Justin St. Germain’s spectacular memoir, “Son of a Gun,” calls to mind two others of the past decade: J. R. Moehringer’s “Tender Bar” and Nick Flynn’s “Another Bull____ Night in Suck City.” All three are about boys becoming men in a broken world. All three feature disappeared fathers. And all three dig into the reality behind the facade of the American dream: namely, that there is no glory in being a blue-collar grunt in this country, that to be working class is to live a tenuous existence, one in which the authors and half the people they know are one misstep away from surfing garage-sale sofas in other people’s living rooms.', 'print_section': 'BR', 'print_page': '7', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/08/11/books/review/11FULLER/11FULLER-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 718, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2013/08/11/books/review/11FULLER/11FULLER-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 718}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/08/11/books/review/11FULLER/11FULLER-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 1024, 'width': 857, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/08/11/books/review/11FULLER/11FULLER-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 1592, 'width': 1331, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/08/11/books/review/11FULLER/11FULLER-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2013/08/11/books/review/11FULLER/11FULLER-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/08/11/books/review/11FULLER/11FULLER-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height': 150, 