'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Review', '_id': 'nyt://article/0ec3ee72-dd09-5c73-9dda-c4aa70e9e30b', 'word_count': 1192, 'uri': 'nyt://article/0ec3ee72-dd09-5c73-9dda-c4aa70e9e30b'}, {'abstract': 'A multidisciplinary dissection of the human body through art, science, literature and history.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2013/08/11/books/review/anatomies-by-hugh-aldersey-williams.html', 'snippet': 'A multidisciplinary dissection of the human body through art, science, literature and history.', 'lead_paragraph': 'For a species so pleased with its own brain, we are surprisingly ambivalent about the rest of our body. We tend to admire other people’s bodies, especially if they’re Olympians or shirtless Russian presidents, but most of us are constantly seeking to improve our own by starving or stuffing, injecting, waxing, straightening, piercing, lifting and squeezing. In 2010 in the United States alone, we spent $10 billion on cosmetic surgery, and that’s not including Brazilian Blowouts.', 'print_section': 'BR', 'print_page': '14', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/08/10/books/review/0811-bks-williams/0811-bks-williams-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 792, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2013/08/10/books/review/0811-bks-williams/0811-bks-williams-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 792}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/08/10/books/review/0811-bks-williams/0811-bks-williams-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 1024, 'width': 776, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/08/10/books/review/0811-bks-williams/0811-bks-williams-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 1647, 'width': 1248, 's