ac-5bcb-aa54-75e4db78022b'}, {'abstract': 'In Mark Slouka’s novel, a close friendship offers two teenagers respite from their unhappy homes.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2013/08/11/books/review/brewster-by-mark-slouka.html', 'snippet': 'In Mark Slouka’s novel, a close friendship offers two teenagers respite from their unhappy homes.', 'lead_paragraph': 'A familiar music beats throughout “Brewster,” Mark Slouka’s intense and elegiac novel about the friendship between two teenage boys during the late 1960s and early ’70s. The Beatles and the Stones are on every radio. Every cafeteria conversation finds a way to Muhammad Ali or Charles Manson, Kent State or the draft.', 'print_section': 'BR', 'print_page': '17', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/08/11/books/review/0811-bks-Henderson-Sub/0811-bks-Henderson-Sub-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 750, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2013/08/11/books/review/0811-bks-Henderson-Sub/0811-bks-Henderson-Sub-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 750}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/08/11/books/review/0811-bks-Henderson-Sub/0811-bks-Henderson-Sub-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 1024, 'width': 820, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/08/11/books/review/0811-bks-Henderson-Sub/0811-bks-Henderson-Sub-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 2048, 'width': 1640, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/08/11/books/review/0811-bks-Henderson-Sub/0811-bks-Henderson-Sub-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2013/08/11/books/review/0811-bks-Henderson-Sub/0811-bks-Henderson-Sub-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/08/11/books/review/0811-bks-Henderson-Sub/0811-bks-Henderson-Sub-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'That First Bromance', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'That First Bromance', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Slouka, Mark', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Books and Literature', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2013-08-09T15:03:38+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'BookReview', 'section_name': 'Books', 'subsection_name': 'Book Review', 'byline': {'original': 'By Eleanor Henderson', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Eleanor', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Henderson', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Review', '_id': 'nyt://article/7d932b2f-0a21-53c3-bde8-bc1c91bd7d12', 'word_count': 1214, 'uri': 'nyt://article/7d932b2f-0a21-53c3-bde8-bc1c91bd7d12'}, {'abstract': 'In Lea Carpenter’s novel, a mother awaits news of her son, missing in action.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2013/08/11/books/review/eleven-days-by-lea-carpenter.html', 'snippet': 'In Lea Carpenter’s novel, a mother awaits news of her son, missing in action.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Through the heart of Lea Carpenter’s earnest first novel, “Eleven Days,” courses the myth of a mother and son: the sea nymph Thetis and her warlike child Achilles. Thetis, so legend runs, submerged Achilles in the River Styx to inoculate him from harm but neglected to dip the back of the ankle by which she held him fast. The prophecy that Achilles would prove a warrior greater than his father, but would also die prematurely in his bid for fame, remained a burden to the vigilant Thetis, who did her utmost to circumvent this destiny.', 'print_section': 'BR', 'print_page': '18', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/08/11/books/review/11SAMET/11SAMET-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 317, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2013/08/11/books/review/11SAMET/11SAMET-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 317}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/08/11/books/review/11SAMET/11SAMET-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 541, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/08/11/books/review/11SAMET/11SAMET-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 1082, 'width': 2048, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/08/11/books/review/11SAMET/11SAMET-thumbStandard-v2.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2013/08/11/books/review/11SAMET/11SAMET-thumbStandard-v2.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/08/11/books/review/11SAMET/11SAMET-thumbLarge-v2.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Covert Losses', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Covert Losses', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Samet, Elizabeth D', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Carpenter, Lea', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Books and Literature', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2013-08-09T15:03:49+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'BookReview', 'section_name': 'Books', 'subsection_name': 'Book Review', 'byline': {'original': 'By Elizabeth D. Samet', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Elizabeth', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': '', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Review', '_id': 'nyt://article/7675560a-2b66-59b6-9de1-cb3b448b3270', 'word_count': 1179, 'uri': 'nyt://article/7675560a-2b66-59b6-9de1-cb3b448b3270'}, {'abstract': 'A memoir, presented as a self-interview, by the Nobel laureate Imre Kertesz.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2013/08/11/books/review/dossier-k-by-imre-kertesz.html', 'snippet': 'A memoir, presented as a self-interview, by the Nobel laureate Imre Kertesz.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Two of the great pessimistic proclamations of 20th-century literature — Adorno’s “To write a poem after Auschwitz is barbaric” and Beckett’s “I can’t go on, I’ll go on” — have at least one thing in common. They both address the inadequacy of language to articulate reality. Better to say nothing, they both say, or at least that’s the first half of what they say. If Adorno leaves off the productive half of the equation — “I’ll go on” — other writers have supplied it for him, in the form of a very large body of work concerned with the Holocaust that is not only ethically accountable but also incredibly rich and inventive. In fact it is not too much to claim, of Holocaust literature, that the struggle to say what is unsayable has paradoxically yielded some of the most extraordinary literary works we have.', 'print_section': 'BR', 'print_page': '19', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/08/11/books/review/11RIKER/11RIKER-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 357, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2013/08/11/books/review/11RIKER/11RIKER-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 357}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/08/11/books/review/11RIKER/11RIKER-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 609, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/08/11/books/review/11RIKER/11RIKER-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 1218, 'width': 2048, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/08/11/books/review/11RIKER/11RIKER-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2013/08/11/books/review/11RIKER/11RIKER-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/08/11/books/review/11RIKER/11RIKER-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Raw Materials', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Raw Materials', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Books and Literature', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Kertesz, Imre', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Wilkinson, Tim', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2013-08-09T15:04:15+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'BookReview', 'section_name': 'Books', 'subsection_name': 'Book Review', 'byline': {'original': 'By Martin Riker', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Martin', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Riker', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Review', '_id': 'nyt://article/f4d5d85e-9b8f-5080-b3ac-42181943ed7e', 'word_count': 1137, 'uri': 'nyt://article/f4d5d85e-9b8f-5080-b3ac-42181943ed7e'}, {'abstract': 'Liane Moriarty, whose novel “The Husband’s Secret” is on the extended hardcover list, has said her first kiss “blew my good little Catholic girl mind.”', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2013/08/18/books/review/inside-the-list.html', 'snippet': 'Liane Moriarty, whose novel “The Husband’s Secret” is on the extended hardcover list, has said her first kiss “blew my good little Catholic girl mind.”', 'lead_paragraph': 'PANDORA’S ENVELOPE: The Australian writer Liane Moriarty has won a devoted following for her novels chronicling the lives of “typical suburban mums,” but she’s never cracked the New York Times best-seller list. That may change soon, though — “The Husband’s Secret,” Moriarty’s fifth novel for adults, is hovering at No. 17 on the extended hardcover list after a wave of endorsements and strong reviews. The plot is, essentially, a version of Pandora’s box: a happily married woman finds an envelope from her husband with instructions to open it only after his death, and decides to peek ahead of time.', 'print_section': 'BR', 'print_page': '22', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/08/11/books/review/11list-1/11list-1-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 450, 'width': 449, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/08/11/books/review/11list-1/11list-1-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 450, 'width': 449, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'cr