makes-a-better-role-model.html', 'snippet': 'Recent research suggests we may be choosing the wrong people to emulate, and that could be hurting us professionally.', 'lead_paragraph': 'RECENTLY the Yankee third baseman Alex Rodriguez, who was just suspended for 211 games for steroid use, proclaimed that he still wanted to be a role model, despite “all the noise.”', 'print_section': 'B', 'print_page': '5', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/08/10/business/Shortcuts1/Shortcuts1-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 400, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2013/08/10/business/Shortcuts1/Shortcuts1-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 400}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/08/10/business/Shortcuts1/Shortcuts1-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 682, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/08/10/business/Shortcuts1/Shortcuts1-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 1365, 'width': 2048, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Sometimes Second-Best Makes a Better Role Model', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Sometimes Second-Best Makes a Better Role Model', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Mentors', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Tugend, Alina', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Executives and Management (Theory)', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Careers and Professions', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Paul, Annie Murphy', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2013-08-09T17:01:37+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Business', 'section_name': 'Your Money', 'byline': {'original': 'By Alina Tugend', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Alina', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Tugend', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/1b6c255c-c27e-5d0d-a483-05955b515cad', 'word_count': 1021, 'uri': 'nyt://article/1b6c255c-c27e-5d0d-a483-05955b515cad'}, {'abstract': 'The newspaper used to be better. But passionate journalists are still succeeding, not because of management but in spite of it.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2013/08/10/opinion/the-washington-post-that-was.html', 'snippet': 'The newspaper used to be better. But passionate journalists are still succeeding, not because of management but in spite of it.', 'lead_paragraph': 'TAKOMA PARK, Md. — WHEN I arrived in 1970, The Washington Post had just achieved escape velocity. The executive editor, Benjamin C. Bradlee, and the publisher, Katharine Graham, had launched a shabby local paper into the orbit of fame, hipness, power and phone-slamming Kennedy-style pugnacity. Watch out, New York.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'The Washington Post That Was', 'kicker': 'Op-Ed Contributor', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'The Washington Post That Was', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Washington Post', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Newspapers', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Bradlee, Benjamin C', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2013-08-09T17:03:51+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'OpEd', 'section_name': 'Opinion', 'byline': {'original': 'By Henry Allen', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Henry', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Allen', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'orga