, 'lastname': 'Ryzik', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/c5565eff-1555-5acd-93ed-e04bcac66c9b', 'word_count': 893, 'uri': 'nyt://article/c5565eff-1555-5acd-93ed-e04bcac66c9b'}, {'abstract': 'The ill-fated, computer-animated “Foodfight!” took multiple food-brand corporate mascots and made them characters.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2013/08/11/movies/the-rise-and-fall-of-the-computer-animated-foodfight.html', 'snippet': 'The ill-fated, computer-animated “Foodfight!” took multiple food-brand corporate mascots and made them characters.', 'lead_paragraph': 'The brand managers at Georgia-Pacific were not about to budge. They agreed that Brawny Man, the plaid-shirted character adorning millions of paper-towel rolls, could come to life and share a moment with Mr. Clean. But their Angel Soft Baby, who had sold countless reams of toilet tissue, could never be displaced from her perch in the clouds. ', 'print_section': 'AR', 'print_page': '10', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/08/11/arts/11FOODFIGHT1_SPAN/11FOODFIGHT1-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 400, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2013/08/11/arts/11FOODFIGHT1_SPAN/11FOODFIGHT1-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 400}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/08/11/arts/11FOODFIGHT1_SPAN/11FOODFIGHT1-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 683, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/08/11/arts/11FOODFIGHT1_SPAN/11FOODFIGHT1-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 1365, 'width': 2048, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/08/11/arts/11FOODFIGHT1_SPAN/11FOODFIGHT1-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2013/08/11/arts/11FOODFIGHT1_SPAN/11FOODFIGHT1-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/08/11/arts/11FOODFIGHT1_SPAN/11FOODFIGHT1-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Placing Products? Try Casting Them', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Placing Products? Try Casting Them', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Movies', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Kasanoff, Larry', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'creative_works', 'value': 'Foodfight! (Movie)', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'ANIMATED FILMS', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2013-08-09T18:09:10+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Arts&Leisure', 'section_name': 'Movies', 'byline': {'original': 'By Jake Rossen', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Jake', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Rossen', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/6643ab9f-efbe-5ecd-9879-d03bc456e468', 'word_count': 1520, 'uri': 'nyt://article/6643ab9f-efbe-5ecd-9879-d03bc456e468'}, {'abstract': 'Four salads and a cold noodle soup that make refreshing meals during warm weather.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2013/08/12/health/on-hot-days-cold-noodles-are-a-welcome-choice.html', 'snippet': 'Four salads and a cold noodle soup that make refreshing meals during warm weather.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Some people just don’t get hungry during a heat wave, but I’m not one of them. During the last run of hot weather I craved cold Asian noodles; I loved the way they were both cooling and filling, and the way they served as a vehicle for refreshing vegetables like cucumbers and crisp shredded cabbage. Asian noodles don’t need to be cooked al dente the way Italian pasta does, so they won’t suffer from being cooked ahead, tossed with a little sesame oil or dressing and refrigerated for a day or two. This is really helpful when the weather’s hot and you’re hungry. You reach into the refrigerator and grab a bowl of noodle salad that is no worse for wear from having been there for a day.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/08/10/science/12recipehealth/12recipehealth-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 400, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2013/08/10/science/12recipehealth/12recipehealth-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 400}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/08/10/science/12recipehealth/12recipehealth-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 682, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/08/10/science/12recipehealth/12recipehealth-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 1365, 'width': 2048, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/08/10/science/12recipehealth/12recipehealth-thumbStandard-v2.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2013/08/10/science/12recipehealth/12recipehealth-thumbStandard-v2.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/08/10/science/12recipehealth/12recipehealth-thumbLarge-v2.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'On Hot Days, Cold Noodles Are a Welcome Choice', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Medicine and Health', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Diet and Nutrition', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Recipes', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2013-08-09T18:14:49+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Science', 'section_name': 'Health', 'byline': {'original': 'By Martha Rose Shulman', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Martha', 'middlename': 'Rose', 'lastname': 'Shulman', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'recipe', '_id': 'nyt://article/990c5e71-423f-5f77-a4cc-6f11a81fb7f0', 'word_count': 832, 'uri': 'nyt://article/990c5e71-423f-5f77-a4cc-6f11a81fb7f0'}, {'abstract': 'Why Tennessee blacks celebrated Aug. 8, 1863 as Emancipation Day.', 'web_url': 'https://opinionator.blogs.nytimes.com/2013/08/09/when-andrew-johnson-freed-his-slaves/', 'snippet': 'Why Tennessee blacks celebrated Aug. 8, 1863 as Emancipation Day.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Eliza Johnson gathered her family’s slaves together at their East Tennessee farm on Aug. 8, 1863, to deliver the news. As one slave, William, remembered it, “She said we were free to go, or we could stay if we wanted.” With the dangers of continued Confederate military occupation and guerrilla war in the region, the slaves opted to stay put for the time being. ', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'When Andrew Johnson Freed His Slaves', 'kicker': 'Disunion', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Civil War (US) (1861-65)', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Slavery', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Johnson, Andrew', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Tennessee', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2013-08-09T18:18:49+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Opinion', 'byline': {'original': 'By Aaron Astor', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Aaron', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Astor', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/634bbf92-c66a-541b-a0b2-9fdaf8124347', 'word_count': 1436, 'uri': 'nyt://article/634bbf92-c66a-541b-a0b2-9fdaf8124347'}, {'abstract': 'On Wednesday at Jacob’s Pillow Dance Festival, Wendy Whelan unveils “Restless Creature,” a suite of duets.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2013/08/11/arts/dance/a-ballerina-in-a-new-realm.html', 'snippet': 'On Wednesday at Jacob’s Pillow Dance Festival, Wendy Whelan unveils “Restless Creature,” a suite of duets.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Wendy Whelan has been a member of New York City Ballet since 1986, so it’s really no wonder that she’s feeling a little fidgety these days. On Wednesday at Jacob’s Pillow Dance Festival, Ms. Whelan unveils “Restless Creature,” a suite of duets choreographed by and performed with Kyle Abraham, Joshua Beamish, Brian Brooks and Alejandro Cerrudo. In the show, which will appear beginning next March in several cities — including New York, at the Joyce Theater — Ms. Whelan, following the path of Mikhail Baryshnikov, is keen to stretch her limits.', 'print_section': 'AR', 'print_page': '2', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'A Ballerina in a New Realm', 'kicker': 'The Week Ahead | Dance', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'A Ballerina In a New Realm', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Dancing', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2013-08-09T18:23:56+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Arts&Leisure', 'section_name': 'Arts', 'subsection_name': 'Dance', 'byline': {'original': 'By Gia Kourlas', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Gia', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Kourlas', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/ee66a10e-fde3-5274-8429-9a20a3c53738', 'word_count': 177, 'uri': 'nyt://article/ee66a10e-fde3-5274-8429-9a20a3c53738'}, {'abstract': 'Has it really been 17 years since Film Forum last hosted its late-summer extravaganza Son of Summer Sci-Fi, Fantasy & Horror?', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2013/08/11/movies/sci-fi-horror-fest-with-nod-to-a-titan.html', 'snippet': 'Has it really been 17 years since Film Forum last hosted its late-summer extravaganza Son of Summer Sci-Fi, Fantasy & Horror?', 'lead_paragraph': 'Has it really been 17 years since Film Forum last hosted its late-summer extravaganza Son of Summer Sci-Fi, Fantasy & Horror, this year through Sept. 5? Well, it’s high time that this home of cinematic sophistication got its nerd on once again. And there’s no better way to mark the event’s return than with the family-friendly sub-festival Wild About Harryhausen, double features (for the most part) showcasing the stop-motion-animation giant Ray Harryhausen, through Sept. 8.', 'print_section': 'AR', 'print_page': '2', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Sci-Fi Horror Fest With Nod to a Titan', 'kicker': 'The Week Ahead | Film', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Sci-Fi Horror Fest With Nod to a Titan', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Movies', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Film Forum', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2013-08-09T18:25:07+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Arts&Leisure', 'section_name': 'Movies', 'byline': {'original': 'By Andy Webster', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Andy', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Webster', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/294d8f56-c4b8-53c2-8967-e0c8985863c9', 'word_count': 174, 'uri': 'nyt://article/294d8f56-c4b8-53c2-8967-e0c8985863c9'}, {'abstract': 'The timing is exactly right for the small, powerful retrospective show of paintings and drawings, “In the Tower: Kerry James Marshall,” at the National Gallery of Art in Washington.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2013/08/11/arts/design/being-black-in-america.html', 'snippet': 'The timing is exactly right for the small, powerful retrospective show of paintings and drawings, “In the Tower: Kerry James Marshall,” at the National Gallery of Art in Washington.', 'lead_paragraph': 'With the 50th anniversary of the March on Washington coming at the end of the month, and the emotions generated by the trial of George Zimmerman in the killing of Trayvon Martin still crackling, the timing is exactly right for the small, powerful retrospective show of paintings and drawings, “In the Tower: Kerry James Marshall,” at the National Gallery of Art in Washington. ', 'print_section': 'AR', 'print_page': '2', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Being Black in America', 'kicker': 'The Week Ahead | Art', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Being Black In America', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Art', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Blacks', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Black People', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2013-08-09T18:25:54+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Arts&Leisure', 'section_name': 'Arts', 'subsection_name': 'Art & Design', 'byline': {'original': 'By Holland Cotter', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Holland', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Cotter', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/d7a77079-fba9-597a-ae16-4c43955b7843', 'word_count': 213, 'uri': 'nyt://article/d7a77079-fba9-597a-ae16-4c43955b7843'}, {'abstract': 'Each summer in the silver-roofed, Gehry-designed Fisher Center, the Bard Music Festival spotlights one composer.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2013/08/11/arts/music/stravinsky-salute-at-bard-festival.html', 'snippet': 'Each summer in the silver-roofed, Gehry-designed Fisher Center, the Bard Music Festival spotlights one composer.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Each summer in the silver-roofed, Gehry-designed Fisher Center, the Bard Music Festival spotlights one composer. The intense weekend lineups feature well-known and obscure repertory performed by rising and established musicians, as well as panels and talks by scholars. Stravinsky, whose “Rite of Spring” turns 100 this year, is being honored at the festival. Sunday’s afternoon lineup includes musicians like the pianist Alessio Bax, and works by Stravinsky, Debussy, Schoenberg, Ravel and Satie. That evening, Leon Botstein conducts members of the American Symphony Orchestra and soloists, including the tenor Nicholas Phan, in works by Milhaud, Poulenc, Satie and Stravinsky. ', 'print_section': 'AR', 'print_page': '2', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Stravinsky Salute at Bard Festival', 'kicker': 'The Week Ahead | Classical', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Stravinsky Salute At Bard Festival', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Bard Music Festival', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Classical Music', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2013-08-09T18:27:15+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Arts&Leisure', 'section_name': 'Arts', 'subsection_name': 'Music', 'byline': {'original': 'By Vivien Schweitzer', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Vivien', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Schweitzer', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/6c524f8b-51a3-52b6-bd8e-e262b37b7a74', 'word_count': 133, 'uri': 'nyt://article/6c524f8b-51a3-52b6-bd8e-e262b37b7a74'}, {'abstract': 'Since 2006, more than 100 albinos have been maimed or killed for their body parts in Tanzania.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2013/08/11/arts/television/the-hunted-in-tanzania.html', 'snippet': 'Since 2006, more than 100 albinos have been maimed or killed for their body parts in Tanzania.', 'lead_paragraph': 'In his homeland, Josephat Torner is said to be a ghost. Born with albinism, Mr. Torner is among the 170,000 Tanzanians with the congenital disorder — an absence of pigment in the skin, hair and eyes — who have been hunted for their body parts, which are believed by traditional healers to bring riches to those who possess them. Since 2006, more than 100 albinos have been maimed or killed by men who hack off arms, legs, heads and genitals, forcing the government to open boarding schools to protect children and communities to build walled-in camps where families are guarded, making them refugees in their own country.', 'print_section': 'AR', 'print_page': '2', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'The Hunted in Tanzania', 'kicker': 'The Week Ahead | Television', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'The Hunted In Tanzania', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Albinism', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, 