 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2013-08-10T17:47:01+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'OpEd', 'section_name': 'Opinion', 'subsection_name': 'Sunday Review', 'byline': {'original': 'By Sheri Berman', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Sheri', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Berman', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Op-Ed', '_id': 'nyt://article/7999c017-0a22-5b8f-a1a8-e2b1eb1c17ac', 'word_count': 1126, 'uri': 'nyt://article/7999c017-0a22-5b8f-a1a8-e2b1eb1c17ac'}, {'abstract': 'Are medical professionals biased against the mentally ill?', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2013/08/11/opinion/sunday/when-doctors-discriminate.html', 'snippet': 'Are medical professionals biased against the mentally ill?', 'lead_paragraph': 'THE first time it was an ear, nose and throat doctor. I had an emergency visit for an ear infection, which was causing a level of pain I hadn’t experienced since giving birth. He looked at the list of drugs I was taking for my bipolar disorder and closed my chart. ', 'print_section': 'SR', 'print_page': '6', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/08/11/sunday-review/11STIGMA/11STIGMA-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 786, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2013/08/11/sunday-review/11STIGMA/11STIGMA-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 786}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/08/11/sunday-review/11STIGMA/11STIGMA-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 1024, 'width': 782, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/08/11/sunday-review/11STIGMA/11STIGMA-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 2048, 'width': 1563, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/08/11/sunday-review/11STIGMA/11STIGMA-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2013/08/11/sunday-review/11STIGMA/11STIGMA-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/08/11/sunday-review/11STIGMA/11STIGMA-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'When Doctors Discriminate', 'kicker': 'Opinion', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'When Doctors Discriminate', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Mental Health and Disorders', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Doctors', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Medicine and Health', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Psychiatry and Psychiatrists', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2013-08-10T17:47:24+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'OpEd', 'section_name': 'Opinion', 'subsection_name': 'Sunday Review', 'byline': {'original': 'By Juliann Garey', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Juliann', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Garey', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Op-Ed', '_id': 'nyt://article/2d18b7aa-e974-5c61-a8dc-9004ed32af1b', 'word_count': 1147, 'uri': 'nyt://article/2d18b7aa-e974-5c61-a8dc-9004ed32af1b'}, {'abstract': 'The psychiatrist finds beauty in 1,000-plus pages of Mailer, has ears only for Springsteen and thinks opera and Facebook share a common denominator.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2013/08/11/opinion/sunday/thomas-lewis.html', 'snippet': 'The psychiatrist finds beauty in 1,000-plus pages of Mailer, has ears only for Springsteen and thinks opera and Facebook share a common denominator.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Thomas Lewis is a psychiatrist who is best known as an author of “A General Theory of Love,” which was published in 2001 and has been reissued twice and translated into eight languages. READING “Harlot’s Ghost” by Norman Mailer is tremendous, more than 1,000 pages, beautifully written and in a setting you’d think no one could ever use as a basis for literature. It tracks the career of a C.I.A. agent from 1955 to 1965. Also, “The Ocean at the End of the Lane” by Neil Gaiman. He’s kind of a macabre fantasist. I’ve only gotten to the part where the person actually gets to the ocean at the end of the lane, so I don’t know what happens after that.', 'print_section': 'SR', 'print_page': '2', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/08/11/sunday-review/11download/11download-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 1737, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2013/08/11/sunday-review/11download/11download-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 1737}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/08/11/sunday-review/11download/11download-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 1024, 'width': 354, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/08/11/sunday-review/11download/11download-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 2048, 'width': 708, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/08/11/sunday-review/11download/11download-thumbStandard-v2.j