/article/4f61ad1c-faee-5156-b44c-ff072ec44dad'}, {'abstract': 'Union General Lorenzo Thomas enjoyed asking those who opposed the enlistment of African Americans to step out of their ranks on the parade ground, then having them hauled off to the stockade.', 'web_url': 'https://opinionator.blogs.nytimes.com/2013/08/12/born-again-in-mississippi/', 'snippet': 'Union General Lorenzo Thomas enjoyed asking those who opposed the enlistment of African Americans to step out of their ranks on the parade ground, then having them hauled off to the stockade.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Adjutant Gen. Lorenzo Thomas had assumed that General Ulysses S. Grant had lost his way. For two days, his small party of officers and orderlies had ridden around and across the same pair of bayous, making use of bridges and fords and sometimes swimming their horses. Caught in a terrific storm, they had spent a night on the ground — notwithstanding the distinguished guests in the company, General Thomas himself, on special assignment from Washington, and Congressman Elihu Washburne, Grant’s friend and patron from his home state of Illinois. Washburne later recalled with admiration how the commander traveled six days in May of ’63 with nothing more than a toothbrush, an example to his troops of military grit and mobility [1] . ', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Born Again in Mississippi', 'kicker': 'Disunion', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Civil War (US) (1861-65)', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Grant, Ulysses S', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Lincoln, Abraham', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2013-08-12T16:30:49+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Opinion', 'byline': {'original': 'By LeeAnna Keith', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Leeanna', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Keith', '