ogs.nytimes.com/2013/08/12/synthesis-lost/', 'snippet': 'So much for the classical verities.', 'lead_paragraph': 'A few more notes on the fading of Milton Friedman, which is actually part of a larger problem with economics as practiced for the past few decades. I’ve covered this ground before, but it’s been a while, and I think the case against the neoclassical synthesis (I’ll explain in a minute) is even stronger now.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Synthesis Lost', 'kicker': 'Paul Krugman', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [], 'pub_date': '2013-08-12T17:35:44+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Opinion', 'byline': {'original': 'By Paul Krugman', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Paul', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Krugman', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/e56353df-f89e-579f-9dab-956e360869c8', 'word_count': 673, 'uri': 'nyt://article/e56353df-f89e-579f-9dab-956e360869c8'}, {'abstract': 'James (Whitey) Bulger, the mobster who terrorized South Boston in the 1970s and ’80s as leader of the Winter Hill Gang, faces a sentence of life in prison.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2013/08/13/us/james-whitey-bulger-verdict.html', 'snippet': 'James (Whitey) Bulger, the mobster who terrorized South Boston in the 1970s and ’80s as leader of the Winter Hill Gang, faces a sentence of life in prison.', 'lead_paragraph': 'BOSTON — James (Whitey) Bulger, the mobster who terrorized South Boston in the 1970s and ‘80s, holding the city in his thrall even after he disappeared, was convicted Monday of a sweeping array of gangland crimes, including 11 murders. He faces the prospect of spending the rest of his life in prison.', 'print_section': 'A', 'print_page': '1', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 