nfair to Cassandra.', 'web_url': 'https://krugman.blogs.nytimes.com/2013/08/12/the-chicken-little-scale/', 'snippet': 'Don’t be unfair to Cassandra.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Brad DeLong sends us to Dan Drezner, who rates Hubbard-Kane along what he calls the Cassandra Scale of excessive debt phobia. But while I like the scale, Drezner kind of messes up his mythology; after all, the point about Cassandra was that although nobody would believe her, she was always right — whereas the people he’s talking about are always wrong. ', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'The Chicken Little Scale', 'kicker': 'Paul Krugman', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [], 'pub_date': '2013-08-12T21:35:58+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Opinion', 'byline': {'original': 'By Paul Krugman', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Paul', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Krugman', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/fdea3c11-2606-51de-92c5-33b71353b464', 'word_count': 103, 'uri': 'nyt://article/fdea3c11-2606-51de-92c5-33b71353b464'}, {'abstract': "A mother in Tennessee has promised to fight a court order to change her son';s name from Messiah to Martin.", 'web_url': 'https://thelede.blogs.nytimes.com/2013/08/12/mother-says-son-is-messiah-judge-says-no/', 'snippet': "A mother in Tennessee has promised to fight a court order to change her son';s name from Messiah to Martin.", 'lead_paragraph': 'Updated, 6:14 p.m. | Asked to settle a dispute between a mother and a father over what their child’s last name should be, Martin or McCullough, a magistrate judge in Tennessee in search of a Solomonic compromise decreed last week that the boy should be known as Martin McCullough. The child’s mother, Jaleesa Martin, told the NBC News affiliate WBIR-Knoxville that she would appeal the ruling, mainly because the court order meant scrapping the first name she had given her son, Messiah.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Mother Says Son Is Messiah; Judge Says No', 'kicker': 'The Lede', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Tennessee', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2013-08-12T21:39:22+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Blogs', 'byline': {'original': 'By Robert Mackey', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Robert', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Mackey', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/d9f70602-66f8-523a-a4fe-88a029f3141f', 'word_count': 302, 'uri': 'nyt://article/d9f70602-66f8-523a-a4fe-88a029f3141f'}, {'abstract': 'The Argentine tango dancers Gabriel Missé and Analía Centurión performed at Dardo Galletto Studios in Manhattan on Friday and Saturday.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2013/08/13/arts/dance/gabriel-misse-and-analia-centurion-dance-tango.html', 'snippet': 'The Argentine tango dancers Gabriel Missé and Analía Centurión performed at Dardo Galletto Studios in Manhattan on Friday and Saturday.', 'lead_paragraph': 'On Sept. 25 and 26, the Argentine dancers Gabriel Missé and Analía Centurión will appear in City Center’s Fall for Dance opening program. Since they are among the world’s most wonderful dancers today, I look forward to it, just as I cheered them recently when they performed at the Vail International Dance Festival. Yet, although I have loved Argentine tango as a theatrical form since the 1980s, I increasingly have learned that tango is best seen at close quarters. Yes, tango is inherently dramatic and scintillatingly musical, but its whole emphasis can be distorted by rendering it theatrical or exhibitionistic.', 'pri