value': 'Obama, Barack', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Obama, Michelle', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': "Martha's Vineyard (Mass)", 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Massachusetts', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2013-08-13T00:55:52+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'National', 'section_name': 'U.S.', 'subsection_name': 'Politics', 'byline': {'original': 'By Mark Landler', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Mark', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Landler', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/834d0473-8b82-581e-856f-0ecdf2c8bb1c', 'word_count': 949, 'uri': 'nyt://article/834d0473-8b82-581e-856f-0ecdf2c8bb1c'}, {'abstract': 'There are better ways than stop-and-frisk practices to reduce crime and respect civil liberties.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2013/08/13/opinion/moving-beyond-stop-and-frisk.html', 'snippet': 'There are better ways than stop-and-frisk practices to reduce crime and respect civil liberties.', 'lead_paragraph': 'MY husband and I are about the same age and build, wear the same clothes and share the same gender, but I am far more likely to be stopped by the police. This isn’t because I have a criminal record or engage in furtive movements. Nor is my husband a choirboy. Statistically speaking, it’s because I’m black and he’s white. ', 'print_section': 'A', 'print_page': '23', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/08/13/opinion/0813OPEDrusso/0813OPEDrusso-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 933, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2013/08/13/opinion/0813OPEDrusso/0813OPEDrusso-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 933}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/08/13/opinion/0813OPEDrusso/0813OPEDrusso-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 768, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/08/13/opinion/0813OPEDrusso/0813OPEDrusso-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 1536, 'width': 2048, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/08/13/opinion/0813OPEDrusso/0813OPEDrusso-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2013/08/13/opinion/0813OPEDrusso/0813OPEDrusso-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/08/13/opinion/0813OPEDrusso/0813OPEDrusso-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Moving Beyond Stop-and-Frisk', 'kicker': 'Op-Ed Contributor', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Moving Beyond Stop-and-Frisk', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Capers, I Bennett', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'New York City', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Crime and Criminals', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Search and Seizure', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Blacks', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Black People', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Bloomberg, Michael R', 'rank': 7, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Police Department (NYC)', 'rank': 8, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Scheindlin, Shira A', 'rank': 9, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2013-08-13T01:01:49+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'OpEd', 'section_name': 'Opinion', 'byline': {'original': 'By I Bennett Capers', 'person': [{'firstname': '', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': '', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Op-Ed', '_id': 'nyt://article/ceac6e9f-9204-533f-aa21-3b47b04f8ac4', 'word_count': 880, 'uri': 'nyt://article/ceac6e9f-9204-533f-aa21-3b47b04f8ac4'}, {'abstract': 'The International Olympic Committee needs a president who cares about human rights.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2013/08/13/opinion/the-olympics-leadership-mess.html', 'snippet': 'The International Olympic Committee needs a president who cares about human rights.', 'lead_paragraph': 'ON Sept. 10, members of the International Olympic Committee will vote for a new president for the first time in 12 years. The campaign for the I.O.C. presidency is a quiet event that does not attract much news coverage (how many of the six candidates can you name?), but if you care about human rights, the stakes are high. This may be the last chance for many years to reform the committee’s approach to repressive governments that seek to host the games. It is imperative that the committee elect a president willing to lead, not cave in, on this issue.', 'print_section': 'A', 'print_page': '23', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'The Olympics’ Leadership Mess', 'kicker': 'Op-Ed Contributor', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'The Olympics’ Leadership Mess', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'International Olympic Committee', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Worden, Minky', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Rogge, Jacques', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Olympic Games', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Human Rights and Human Rights Violations', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Olympic Games (2014)', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Russia', 'rank': 7, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2013-08-13T01:02:03+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'OpEd', 'section_name': 'Opinion', 'byline': {'original': 'By Minky Worden', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Minky', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Worden', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Op-Ed', '_id': 'nyt://article/48a24545-4849-5d46-9422-875c47fb5da5', 'word_count': 809, 'uri': 'nyt://article/48a24545-4849-5d46-9422-875c47fb5da5'}, {'abstract': 'An academic who advises the armed services says that certain cultural dynamics, magnified in the military, explain and abet sexual violence.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2013/08/13/opinion/bruni-tackling-the-roots-of-rape.html', 'snippet': 'An academic who advises the armed services says that certain cultural dynamics, magnified in the military, explain and abet sexual violence.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Steubenville. The Naval Academy. Vanderbilt University. The stories of young men sexually assaulting young women seem never to stop, despite all the education we’ve had and all the progress we’ve supposedly made, and there are times when I find myself darkly wondering if there’s some ineradicable predatory streak in the male subset of our species.', 'print_section': 'A', 'print_page': '23', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2018/04/03/opinion/frank-bruni/frank-bruni-articleLarge.png', 'height': 300, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2018/04/03/opinion/frank-bruni/frank-bruni-articleLarge.png', 'xlargewidth': 600, 