rk-fringe-festival-report-slaughterhouse-five/', 'snippet': 'Daria Tavana’s play is a stage adaptation of Kurt Vonnegut’s novel.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Reviews of shows from the New York International Fringe Festival will appear on ArtsBeat through the festival’s close on Aug. 25. For more information, go to fringenyc.org.', 'print_section': 'C', 'print_page': '6', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/08/12/theater/12slaughter/12slaughter-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 400, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2013/08/12/theater/12slaughter/12slaughter-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 400}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/08/12/theater/12slaughter/12slaughter-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 683, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/08/12/theater/12slaughter/12slaughter-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 1365, 'width': 2048, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/08/12/theater/12slaughter/12slaughter-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2013/08/12/theater/12slaughter/12slaughter-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/08/12/theater/12slaughter/12slaughter-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'New York Fringe Festival Report: ‘Slaughterhouse-Five’', 'kicker': 'ArtsBeat', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Slaughterhouse-Five', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'New York International Fringe Festival', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Vonnegut, Kurt Jr', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2013-08-13T21:43:52+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Arts', 'byline': {'original': 'By Neil Genzlinger', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Neil', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Genzlinger', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/d870ce2b-b5b5-58f5-857d-61026b301474', 'word_count': 260, 'uri': 'nyt://article/d870ce2b-b5b5-58f5-857d-61026b301474'}, {'abstract': 'In Alexander Harrington’s new drama, “The Great Society,” the complex and conflicted Lyndon B. Johnson comes across as the biggest Vietnam-era pacifist in the White House.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2013/08/14/theater/reviews/in-the-great-society-a-conflicted-lyndon-b-johnson.html', 'snippet': 'In Alexander Harrington’s new drama, “The Great Society,” the complex and conflicted Lyndon B. Johnson comes across as the biggest Vietnam-era pacifist in the White House.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Lyndon Baines Johnson has long been recognized as a figure of Shakespearean dimensions, perhaps first in Barbara Garson’s 1967 blistering satire, “MacBird!” — in which Johnson was Macbeth and behind the murder of his leader, John Ken O’Dunc. ', 'print_section': 'C', 'print_page': '3', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/08/14/arts/greatsoc/greatsoc-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 698, 'width': 577, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/08/14/arts/greatsoc/greatsoc-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 698, 'width': 577, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/08/14/arts/greatsoc/greatsoc-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2013/08/14/arts/greatsoc/greatsoc-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/08/14/arts/greatsoc/greatsoc-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'A White House Warrior on Poverty, Vietnam and Race', 'kicker': 'Theater Review', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'A White House Warrior on Poverty, Vietnam and Race', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Theater', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Johnson, Lyndon Baines', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Harrington, Alexander', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Tebo, Mitch', 'rank': 7, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2013-08-13T21:45:46+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Culture', 'section_name': 'Theater', 'subsection_name': 'Theater Reviews', 'byline': {'original': 'By Daniel M. Gold', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Daniel', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': '', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Review', '_id': 'nyt://article/85d5d9f0-6a26-5cad-a569-d2e20b8302c5', 'word_count': 527, 'uri': 'nyt://article/85d5d9f0-6a26-5cad-a569-d2e20b8302c5'}, {'abstract': 'The New York coffee company Vassilaros and Sons, whose coffee has been a staple in corner stores for nearly a century, is now selling its beans direct to consumers.', 'web_url': 'https://tmagazine.blogs.nytimes.com/2013/08/13/food-matters-bodega-coffee-flavor-brewed-right-in-your-kitchen/', 'snippet': 'The New York coffee company Vassilaros and Sons, whose coffee has been a staple in corner stores for nearly a century, is now selling its beans direct to consumers.', 'lead_paragraph': '“I’ve been a salesman all my life,” said John Vassilaros, a third-generation coffee maker whose family company, Vassilaros and Sons, has been peddling its home-roasted, hand-blended beans around New York, New Jersey and Connecticut for nearly a century. “The trick to selling is don’t talk too much,” he added. “Just let them taste it.” He wouldn’t dream of leaving a pound of coffee behind for prospective clients to try later. Instead, he gets behind the counter to brew up Vassilaros and Sons’ trademark brew for side-by-side taste tests. He claims he usually wins. The proof is in the ubiquity of his familiar-tasting medium roast, a complex blend of South American beans prepared every Tuesday in Queens, which can be found in hundreds of New York City delis, bodegas and coffee shops, not to mention restaurants like the Boathouse Restaurant in Central Park.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Food Matters | Bodega Coffee Flavor, Brewed Right in Your Kitchen', 'kicker': 'T Magazine', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Coffee', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'New York City', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2013-08-13T21:48:10+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'T Magazine', 'byline': {'original': 'By Sara Ruffin Costello', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Sara', 'middlename': 'Ruffin', 'lastname': 'Costello', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/e8f7fa99-4a83-5b9e-9d3c-c83367260924', 'word_count': 248, 'uri': 'nyt://article/e8f7fa99-4a83-5b9e-9d3c-c83367260924'}, {'abstract': 'When Banksy, the pseudonymous British street artist, paints a surface, that surface is increasingly likely to be dismantled and sold.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2013/08/14/arts/design/another-banksy-mural-to-go-from-wall-to-auction.html', 'snippet': 'When Banksy, the pseudonymous British street artist, paints a surface, that surface is increasingly likely to be dismantled and sold.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Under cover of night, in 2008, Banksy, the pseudonymous British artist, stenciled a mural on a wall of a gas station in Los Angeles: the silhouette of a girl holding a basket of flowers and peering up at a security camera. About nine months ago, that 5,000-pound, 9-by-8-foot chunk of brick wall was removed, and, in the latest of a string of controversial sales of Banksy street work, will soon be on the block. In December, Julien’s Auctions in Beverly Hills, Calif., will offer “Flower Girl,” the first Banksy street art to be sold in the United States, the auctioneers said.', 'print_section': 'C', 'print_page': '1', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/08/14/arts/jpbanksy/jpbanksy-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 289, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2013/08/14/arts/jpbanksy/jpbanksy-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 289}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/08/14/arts/jpbanksy/jpbanksy-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 493, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/08/14/arts/jpbanksy