nion', 'byline': {'original': 'By Piper Kerman', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Piper', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Kerman', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Op-Ed', '_id': 'nyt://article/ee5fb9be-56cb-522d-97bd-520bbb488ffc', 'word_count': 830, 'uri': 'nyt://article/ee5fb9be-56cb-522d-97bd-520bbb488ffc'}, {'abstract': 'With Congress gridlocked, political decision making has moved to other governmental bodies. What we’re losing in the process isn’t government — it’s democracy.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2013/08/14/opinion/the-real-price-of-congresss-gridlock.html', 'snippet': 'With Congress gridlocked, political decision making has moved to other governmental bodies. What we’re losing in the process isn’t government — it’s democracy.', 'lead_paragraph': 'BERKELEY, Calif. — CONGRESS began its summer recess last week and won’t reconvene until after Labor Day. You’d be forgiven for not noticing a difference. With just 15 bills signed into law so far this year, the 113th Congress is on pace to be the most unproductive since at least the 1940s. ', 'print_section': 'A', 'print_page': '23', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'The Real Price of Congress’s Gridlock', 'kicker': 'Op-Ed Contributor', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'The Real Price of Congress&#8217;s Gridlock', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'United States Politics and Government', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Reich, Robert B', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'United States Economy', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Law and Legislation', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'House of Representatives', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'United States', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date':