larious premise: they all are THINGS WITH HOLES.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'You Can Hardly Believe It', 'kicker': 'Wordplay', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Crossword Puzzles', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2013-08-14T02:00:08+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Crosswords & Games', 'byline': {'original': 'By Deb Amlen', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Deb', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Amlen', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/e1a85f44-e7b5-5b45-a39b-6a0ee38fc694', 'word_count': 189, 'uri': 'nyt://article/e1a85f44-e7b5-5b45-a39b-6a0ee38fc694'}, {'abstract': 'Sergio Castro, 72, is neither doctor nor priest, but he fills a gap in Mexico’s ragged safety net in health care, accepting no money from his patients, who are mostly Mayans.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2013/08/14/world/americas/in-mexico-a-healer-who-asks-for-nothing-in-return.html', 'snippet': 'Sergio Castro, 72, is neither doctor nor priest, but he fills a gap in Mexico’s ragged safety net in health care, accepting no money from his patients, who are mostly Mayans.', 'lead_paragraph': 'SAN CRISTÓBAL DE LAS CASAS, Mexico — Every morning, Sergio Castro crisscrosses this city to treat the intimate struggles behind its closed doors.', 'print_section': 'A', 'print_page': '4', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'In Mexico, a Healer Who Asks for Nothing in Return', 'kicker': 'San Cristóbal de Las Casas Journal', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'A Healer Who Asks For Nothing In Return', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Chiapas (Mexico)', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Medicine and Health', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Mayans', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Mexico', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Castro, Sergio', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2013-08-14T02:02:56+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Foreign', 'section_name': 'World', 'subsection_name': 'Americas', 'byline': {'original': 'By Elisabeth Malkin', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Elisabeth', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Malkin', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/e3e16735-009b-598b-a2fc-c3afb638d148', 'word_count': 1194, 'uri': 'nyt://article/e3e16735-009b-598b-a2fc-c3afb638d148'}, {'abstract': 'Instead of being kept in the dark about hospital reimbursement prices, some employers are moving away from the P.P.O. model.', 'web_url': 'https://opinionator.blogs.nytimes.com/2013/08/13/the-cure-for-the-1000-toothbrush/', 'snippet': 'Instead of being kept in the dark about hospital reimbursement prices, some employers are moving away from the P.P.O. model.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Here is a basic fact of health care in the United States: Doctors and hospitals know what they charge, but patients don’t know what they pay. As in any market, when one side has no information, that side loses: price secrecy is a major reason medical bills are so high. In my previous column, I wrote about the effect of this lack of transparency on the bills patients pay out of pocket.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'The Cure for the $1,000 Toothbrush', 'kicker': 'Fixes', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Health Insurance and Managed Care', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Hospitals', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Prices (Fares, Fees and Rates)', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2013-08-14T02:08:20+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Opinion', 'byline': {'original': 'By Tina Rosenberg', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Tina', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Rosenberg', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/fc36cbec-7418-597a-b59e-2f50cad22bb0', 'word_count': 2058, 'uri': 'nyt://article/fc36cbec-7418-597a-b59e-2f50cad22bb0'}, {'abstract': 'Sergio Castro, who arrived almost half a century ago to work as an agronomist and veterinarian, fills a hole in Mexico´s ragged safety net for the sick in Chiapas State.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/slideshow/2013/08/13/world/americas/20130814-MEXICO.html', 'snippet': 'Sergio Castro, who arrived almost half a century ago to work as an agronomist and veterinarian, fills a hole in Mexico´s ragged safety net for the sick in Chiapas State.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Sergio Castro, who arrived almost half a century ago to work as an agronomist and veterinarian, fills a hole in Mexico´s ragged safety net for the sick in Chiapas State.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/08/13/world/americas/20130814-MEXICO-slide-OH7M/20130814-MEXICO-slide-OH7M-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 400, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2013/08/13/world/americas/20130814-MEXICO-slide-OH7M/20130814-MEXICO-slide-OH7M-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 400}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/08/13/world/americas/20130814-MEXICO-slide-OH7M/20130814-MEXICO-slide-OH7M-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 683, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy'