e': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/43da8ca3-0da9-5baf-ba1a-9d6f3adee105', 'word_count': 675, 'uri': 'nyt://article/43da8ca3-0da9-5baf-ba1a-9d6f3adee105'}, {'abstract': 'The protagonists of “Digby Differs” and “Maude the Not-So-Noticeable Shrimpton” feel at odds with their environments.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2013/08/14/books/digby-differs-and-maude-the-not-so-noticeable-shrimpton.html', 'snippet': 'The protagonists of “Digby Differs” and “Maude the Not-So-Noticeable Shrimpton” feel at odds with their environments.', 'lead_paragraph': 'It seems right that books about difference should be unusual themselves. That’s certainly true of “Digby Differs” and “Maude the Not-So-Noticeable Shrimpton,” both of which take as subjects protagonists who feel at odds with their environments. These books are out of the ordinary in appearance and tone, too, and suited, perhaps, not to the typical child (if such a person exists) but rather to older readers who sympathize with that feeling of disconnection.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/08/14/books/14digby/14digby-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 221, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2013/08/14/books/14digby/14digby-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 221}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/08/14/books/14digby/14digby-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 378, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/08/14/books/14digby/14digby-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 755, 'width': 2048, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'