ject', 'value': 'Manchester International Festival', 'rank': 8, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Manchester (England)', 'rank': 9, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'England', 'rank': 10, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Art', 'rank': 11, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2013-08-14T22:12:20+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Arts&Leisure', 'section_name': 'Theater', 'byline': {'original': 'By Roslyn Sulcas', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Roslyn', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Sulcas', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/0871720c-77d1-5f06-9b2b-7b84fd44e93c', 'word_count': 1296, 'uri': 'nyt://article/0871720c-77d1-5f06-9b2b-7b84fd44e93c'}, {'abstract': 'L. C. King Manufacturing in Bristol, Tenn., has been called a diamond in the rough for its old ways. Even Tokyo hipsters love its workwear. Now, as fashion embraces Made in America, can it be a 21st-century brand?', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2013/08/15/fashion/a-tennessee-clothing-factory-keeps-up-the-old-ways.html', 'snippet': 'L. C. King Manufacturing in Bristol, Tenn., has been called a diamond in the rough for its old ways. Even Tokyo hipsters love its workwear. Now, as fashion embraces Made in America, can it be a 21st-century brand?', 'lead_paragraph': 'Not long ago, I got an e-mail from a man thanking me for mentioning his label, Pointer Brand, in an article in 2006. This was no thank-you note. I read on: “With a lot of hard work and persistence, we recently celebrated 100 years of manufacturing in Bristol Tennessee.” It was signed: “Jack King, fourth generation, L. C. King Manufacturing Company.”', 'print_section': 'E', 'print_page': '1', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/08/15/fashion/15ZFACTORY_SPAN/15zFACTORYD-art