me': 'Fackler', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/271152b8-373d-5c53-8357-8d58cd1a2e45', 'word_count': 849, 'uri': 'nyt://article/271152b8-373d-5c53-8357-8d58cd1a2e45'}, {'abstract': 'Television highlights.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2013/08/15/arts/television/whats-on-thursday.html', 'snippet': 'Television highlights.', 'lead_paragraph': '4:15 P.M. (Sundance) ORLANDO (1993) A beautiful young nobleman (Tilda Swinton, above) adored by an aging Queen Elizabeth I (Quentin Crisp) falls asleep one night in the 17th century and wakes up five days later as a woman. “Same person, no difference at all,” she says, unflustered. “Just a different sex.” She continues to learn about life through the centuries up until the present, pondering the differences between men and women — as well as their similarities — in this adaptation by Sally Potter of Virginia Woolf’s 1928 novel. Charlotte Valandrey plays Sasha, a wanton Muscovite princess with whom the male Orlando falls in love. Billy Zane is the American with whom Orlando’s female version has a more enduring if less sexually demanding relationship. “This ravishing and witty spectacle invades the mind through eyes that are dazzled without ever being anesthetized,” Vincent Canby wrote in The New York Times. “Throughout Ms. Potter’s ‘Orlando,’ as in Woolf’s, there are a piercing kind of common sense and a joy that, because they are so rare these days in any medium, create their own kind of cinematic suspense and delightedly surprised laughter. ‘Orlando’ could well become a classic of a very special kind, not mainstream perhaps, but a model for independent filmmakers who follow their own irrational muses, sometimes to unmourned obscurity, occasionally to glory.” More than anything else, he added: “\xa0‘Orlando’ is Ms. Swinton’s triumph. With the firmest but lightest of touches, she has spun gossamer.”', 'print_section': 'C', 'print_page': '8', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'What’s on Thursday', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'What’s On Thursday', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [], 'pub_date': '2013-08-15T05:00:05+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Culture', 'section_name': 'Arts', 'subsection_name': 'Television', 'byline': {'original': 'By Kathryn Shattuck', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Kathryn', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Shattuck', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Schedule', '_id': 'nyt://article/7bd86562-e015-5d9d-9454-486172e282c4', 'word_count': 749, 'uri': 'nyt://article/7bd86562-e015-5d9d-9454-486172e282c4'}, {'abstract': 'President Park Geun-hye’s conciliatory overtures Thursday came amid signs that South and North Korea were moving toward a thaw after months of tensions.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2013/08/16/world/asia/s-korea-proposes-talks-to-reunite-war-divided-families.html', 'snippet': 'President Park Geun-hye’s conciliatory overtures Thursday came amid signs that South and North Korea were moving toward a thaw after months of tensions.', 'lead_paragraph': 'SEOUL, South Korea — President Park Geun-hye of South Korea said Thursday that South and North Korea should resume arranging reunions of families separated by the Korean War six decades ago, and she renewed a proposal to build an “international peace park” straddling their heavily armed border.', 'print_section': 'A', 'print_page': '6', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'South Korea Proposes Talks to Reunite Families Divided by War', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'South Korea Proposes Talks to Reunite War-Divided Families', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'South Korea', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'North Korea', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Korean War', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Park Geun-hye', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'International Relations', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2013-08-15T06:19:02+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Foreign', 'section_name': 'World', 'subsection_name': 'Asia Pacific', 'byline': {'original': 'By Choe Sang-Hun', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Choe', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Sang-Hun', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/59cc73f4-8160-5358-837c-03e2bd197309', 'word_count': 785, 'uri': 'nyt://article/59cc73f4-8160-5358-837c-03e2bd197309'}, {'abstract': 'The Dodgers tied the score in the ninth with a two-run homer and won in the 12th to extend their winning streak to eight games.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2013/08/15/sports/baseball/dodgers-rally-to-sweep-mets.html', 'snippet': 'The Dodgers tied the score in the ninth with a two-run homer and won in the 12th to extend their winning streak to eight games.', 'lead_paragraph': 'LOS ANGELES — Even while they were being held hitless through the first three innings, even as they entered the ninth inning facing a two-run deficit, it seemed inevitable: the Los Angeles Dodgers, baseball’s hottest team, would find some way to win.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/08/15/sports/baseball/15mets-2/15mets-2-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 400, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2013/08/15/sports/baseball/15mets-2/15mets-2-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 400}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'captio