ed to materialize.', 'print_section': 'A', 'print_page': '1', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/08/14/world/middleeast/16egypt-hp-am/15egypt_v2_337_ss-slide-SCC9-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 400, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2013/08/14/world/middleeast/16egypt-hp-am/15egypt_v2_337_ss-slide-SCC9-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 400}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/08/14/world/middleeast/16egypt-hp-am/15egypt_v2_337_ss-slide-SCC9-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 683, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/08/14/world/middleeast/16egypt-hp-am/15egypt_v2_337_ss-slide-SCC9-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 1365, 'width': 2048, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/08/14/world/middleeast/16egypt-hp-am/15egypt_v2_337_ss-slide-SCC9-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2013/08/14/world/middleeast/16egypt-hp-am/15egypt_v2_337_ss-slide-SCC9-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/08/14/world/middleeast/16egypt-hp-am/15egypt_v2_337_ss-slide-SCC9-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Islamists Debate Their Next Move in Tense Cairo', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'In Tense Cairo, Islamists Look To Next Move', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Kirkpatrick, David D', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Egypt', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Demonstrations, Protests, and Riots', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Deaths (Fatalities)', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Muslim Brotherhood (Egypt)', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Morsi, Mohamed', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Middle East and North Africa Unrest (2010- )', 'rank': 7, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Defense and Military Forces', 'rank': 8, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Cairo (Egypt)', 'rank': 9, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2013-08-15T08:49:34+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Foreign', 'section_name': 'World', 'subsection_name': 'Middle East', 'byline': {'original': 'By David D. Kirkpatrick', 'person': [{'firstname': 'David', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': '', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/5b56e065-b1a3-579a-9348-223ef6a2d13e', 'word_count': 1352, 'uri': 'nyt://article/5b56e065-b1a3-579a-9348-223ef6a2d13e'}, {'abstract': 'Why one person suffers and another doesn’t is the type of question that beleaguers Christopher Capozziello, whose twin brother, Nick, has cerebral palsy.', 'web_url': 'https://lens.blogs.nytimes.com/2013/08/15/five-minutes-older-a-lifetime-wiser/', 'snippet': 'Why one person suffers and another doesn’t is the type of question that beleaguers Christopher Capozziello, whose twin brother, Nick, has cerebral palsy.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Christopher Capozziello was born five minutes before his fraternal twin brother, Nick. While other twins might jokingly pull rank on each other, in the Capozziellos’ case those five minutes altered the courses of their lives.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Five Minutes That Split Lifetimes', 'kicker': 'Lens', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Cerebral Palsy', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Photography', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2013-08-15T09:00:11+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Multimedia/Photos', 'byline': {'original': 'By James Estrin', 'person': [{'firstname': 'James', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Estrin', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/95a88504-8011-5830-a2de-2b063f55b940', 'word_count': 812, 'uri': 'nyt://article/95a88504-8011-5830-a2de-2b063f55b940'}, {'abstract': 'A Riff Round Table on the high art of lowbrow pleasures.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2013/08/18/magazine/the-strange-ascent-of-strained-pulp.html', 'snippet': 'A Riff Round Table on the high art of lowbrow pleasures.', 'lead_paragraph': 'In his 2012 review of the Steven Soderbergh film “Haywire” in The New York Times, A. O. Scott identified and named a new phenomenon in popular culture: strained pulp. “Nowadays,” he wrote, “everyone must love (or at least pretend to love) pleasures that were supposedly once disdained or taken for granted: dive bars, street food, trashy films. But knowing, sophisticated attempts to replicate those things often traffic in their own kind of snobbery, confusing condescension with authenticity. Movies like ‘The American,’ ‘Drive’ and now ‘Haywire’ offer strained pulp, neither as dumb as we want them to be nor as smart as they think they are and not, in the end, all that much fun.”', 'print_section': 'MM', 'print_page': '44', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'captio