15T17:43:28+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'National', 'section_name': 'U.S.', 'subsection_name': 'Politics', 'byline': {'original': 'By John Harwood', 'person': [{'firstname': 'John', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Harwood', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/9747a45d-ca0e-5592-a820-8b935ceaa36b', 'word_count': 1027, 'uri': 'nyt://article/9747a45d-ca0e-5592-a820-8b935ceaa36b'}, {'abstract': 'Andy Cohen says he will not be a host of the 2013 Miss Universe pageant, scheduled to be held in Moscow in November.', 'web_url': 'https://artsbeat.blogs.nytimes.com/2013/08/15/citing-russian-anti-gay-law-miss-universe-co-host-pulls-out-of-pageant/', 'snippet': 'Andy Cohen says he will not be a host of the 2013 Miss Universe pageant, scheduled to be held in Moscow in November.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Andy Cohen, the host of Bravo’s late-night talk show “Watch What Happens Live,” has declined to continue his co-hosting duties for a different event, the Miss Universe pageant, because of the competition’s location in Russia and that country’s recent adoption of anti-gay laws.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Citing Russian Anti-Gay Law, Miss Universe Co-Host Pulls Out of Pageant', 'kicker': 'ArtsBeat', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Beauty Contests', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Homosexuality', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Cohen, Andy', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Moscow (Russia)', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Russia', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2013-08-15T17:48:19+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Arts', 'byline': {'original': 'By Adam W. Kepler', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Adam', 'middlename': 'W.', 'lastname': 'Kepler', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/5b18a968-dda1-52db-a33f-9aa9e7b8fa12', 'word_count': 207, 'uri': 'nyt://article/5b18a968-dda1-52db-a33f-9aa9e7b8fa12'}, {'abstract': 'Higher income parents spend more on nearly every item with a discretionary component. That adds up to Montessori instead of in-home day care, houses in stronger school districts, and more money spent on activities, travel, and other miscellany that the lower-income child either doesn’t “need” or just doesn’t get.', 'web_url': 'https://parenting.blogs.nytimes.com/2013/08/15/what-we-spend-to-raise-a-child-and-what-we-dont/', 'snippet': 'Higher income parents spend more on nearly every item with a discretionary component. That adds up to Montessori instead of in-home day care, houses in stronger school districts, and more money spent on activities, travel, and other miscellany tha...', 'lead_paragraph': 'The annual United States Department of Agriculture numbers on the cost of raising a child are out for children born in 2012. (I love that it’s the Agriculture Department, which suggests that we raise children like we do cattle.) Not surprisingly, nearly everything has gone up: parents of children born in 2012 are projected to spend more on child care, education, health care and clothing than parents of kids born in 2011, with an estimated total of $241,080 ($301,970 adjusted for projected inflation) for middle-income families in two-parent households. ', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'What We Spend to Raise a Child — and What We Don’t', 'kicker': 'Motherlode', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Children and Childhood', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Education (K-12)', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Income', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Personal Finances', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2013-08-15T17:57:44+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Style', 'byline': {'original': "By Kj Dell'Antonia", 'person': [{'firstname': 'Kj', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': "Dell'Antonia", 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/bf05e7b3-b1ff-5216-910e-6006802d39f0', 'word_count': 624, 'uri': 'nyt://article/bf05e7b3-b1ff-5216-910e-6006802d39f0'}, {'abstract': 'The aftermath of a deadly bombing in a southern suburb of Beirut on Thursday was captured in video broadcast by the satellite channel run by Hezbollah, the militant movement and political party based in the area.', 'web_url': 'https://thelede.blogs.nytimes.com/2013/08/15/hezbollah-tv-video-of-beirut-bomb-aftermath/', 'snippet': 'The aftermath of a deadly bombing in a southern suburb of Beirut on Thursday was captured in video broadcast by the satellite channel run by Hezbollah, the militant movement and political party based in the area.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Updated, 6:16 p.m. | The aftermath of a deadly bombing in a southern suburb of the Lebanese capital, Beirut, on Thursday was captured in video broadcast by the satellite channel run by Hezbollah, the militant movement and political party based in the area.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Hezbollah TV Video of Beirut Bomb Aftermath', 'kicker': 'The Lede', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Bombs and Explosives', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Hezbollah', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Beirut (Lebanon)', 'rank': 3, 