T23:19:08+0000', 'document_type': 'multimedia', 'news_desk': 'Culture', 'section_name': 'Movies', 'byline': {'original': 'Vijai Singh', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Vijai', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Singh', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Video', '_id': 'nyt://video/391c189f-a57b-5105-8430-d6f7baee3fcf', 'word_count': 0, 'uri': 'nyt://video/391c189f-a57b-5105-8430-d6f7baee3fcf'}, {'abstract': 'The contract dispute is approaching the two-week mark with no hint of an imminent settlement.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2013/08/16/business/media/cbs-blackout-on-time-warner-cable-may-last-until-nfl-season.html', 'snippet': 'The contract dispute is approaching the two-week mark with no hint of an imminent settlement.', 'lead_paragraph': 'More and more, it is looking like the blackout of CBS stations in more than three million homes subscribing to Time Warner Cable could last until the start of the National Football League season on Sept. 8, analysts who are monitoring the situation said this week.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'CBS Blackout on Time Warner Cable May Last Until N.F.L. Season', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Time Warner Cable Inc', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'CBS Corporation', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Cable Television', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Prices (Fares, Fees and Rates)', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2013-08-15T23:29:49+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Business', 'section_name': 'Business Day', 'subsection_name': 'Media', 'byline': {'original': 'By Bill Carter', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Bill', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Carter', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/69ea04c6-3bd9-5f4b-b2ef-7d628dbef37a', 'word_count': 482, 'uri': 'nyt://article/69ea04c6-3bd9-5f4b-b2ef-7d628dbef37a'}, {'abstract': 'An exhibition showcases photographs pledged by philanthropist Emily Fisher Landau at the Aldrich Contemporary Art Museum.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2013/08/18/nyregion/legacy-photographs-from-the-emily-fisher-landau-collection-at-the-aldrich.html', 'snippet': 'An exhibition showcases photographs pledged by philanthropist Emily Fisher Landau at the Aldrich Contemporary Art Museum.', 'lead_paragraph': '“Legacy: Photographs from the Emily Fisher Landau Collection,” at the Aldrich Contemporary Art Museum in Ridgefield, is a small show — 20 photographs in two galleries. But the roster of 17 artists is impressive, including Robert Mapplethorpe and Nan Goldin as well as names usually associated with practices other than photography, like Kiki Smith, Matthew Barney and Robert Longo (whose show of recent drawings is elsewhere in the museum). And the works themselves are important, not only as individual images, but as a representation of the transformative developments in artists’ uses of photography between 1980 and 2004, when all of the pieces were made. ', 'print_section': 'CT', 'print_page': '12', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/08/18/nyregion/18ARTSCTWE1/18ARTSCTWE1-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 701, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2013/08/18/nyregion/18ARTSCTWE1/18ARTSCTWE1-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 701}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/08/18/nyregion/18ARTSCTWE1/18ARTSCTWE1-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 1024, 'width': 877, 'subTyp