a-8fa3-b2ec9beeda22'}, {'abstract': 'Valerie June is trying to bring a little Memphis to the people.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2013/08/18/magazine/williamsburg-appalachia-country-blues-goes-pop.html', 'snippet': 'Valerie June is trying to bring a little Memphis to the people.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Valerie June, the country-and-blues-ish musician, was exploring the vintage stringed-instrument store and repair shop RetroFret — a sprawling series of rooms accessible to the public only through a secret-feeling rooftop garden, which was itself up a flight of stairs and behind a nondescript silver door on an otherwise abandoned-seeming block in Brooklyn’s Gowanus neighborhood. A friend had introduced her to a luthier there for the purposes of repairing a 1920s-era banjo ukulele that she played the previous night at a gig in the East Village.', 'print_section': 'MM', 'print_page': '30', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/08/18/magazine/18june/18june-articleLarge-v2.jpg', 'height': 720, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2013/08/18/magazine/18june/18june-articleLarge-v2.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 720}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/08/18/magazine/18june/18june-jumbo-v2.jpg', 'height': 1024, 'width': 854, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/08/18/magazine/18june/18june-superJumbo-v2.jpg', 'height': 2048, 'width': 1708, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/08/18/magazine/18june/18june-thumbStandard-v3.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2013/08/18/magazine/18june/18june-thumbStandard-v3.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/08/18/magazine/18june/18june-thumbLarge-v3.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Williamsburg-Appalachia- Country-Blues Goes Pop', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'No Country For Old Men', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Music', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Zimmerman, Edith', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'June, Valerie', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2013-08-16T14:33:42+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Magazine', 'section_name': 'Magazine', 'byline': {'original': 'By Edith Zimmerman', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Edith', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Zimmerman', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/ea25d27c-e746-54da-81e3-4c93479cbca1', 'word_count': 791, 'uri': 'nyt://article/ea25d27c-e746-54da-81e3-4c93479cbca1'}, {'abstract': 'How one groovy student made a mattress that hugs back.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2013/08/18/magazine/who-made-that-water-bed.html', 'snippet': 'How one groovy student made a mattress that hugs back.', 'lead_paragraph': 'In the late 1960s, a design student named Charlie Hall embarked on an independent project to explore the idea of comfort. He poured a cornstarch-based gel into an enormous vinyl bag, sealed it and climbed on top. He felt as if he were levitating, his body cradled in the gel that molded around him. But there were drawbacks: the chair began to stink after a few days, and it weighed 300 pounds. “Unless you had a forklift in your living room, it was an impossible piece of furniture,” he says. Hall switched to water as his filler. And he decided to build a mattress instead of a chair; since people rarely move their beds, the weight wouldn’t matter so much. Plus, he says,“It’s the piece of furniture you spend the most amount of time in.”', 'print_section': 'MM', 'print_page': '16', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/08/18/magazine/18wmt/18wmt-articleLarge-v2.jpg', 'height': 280, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2013/08/18/magazine/18wmt/18wmt-articleLarge-v2.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 280}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/08/18/magazine/18wmt/18wmt-jumbo-v2.jpg', 'height': 477, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/08/18/magazine/18wmt/18wmt-superJumbo-v2.jpg', 'height': 955, 'width': 2048, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/08/18/magazine/18wmt/18wmt-thumbStandard-v2.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2013/08/18/magazine/18wmt/18wmt-thumbStandard-v2.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/08/18/magazine/18wmt/18wmt-thumbLarge-v2.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Who Made That Water Bed?', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': No