rticle/ca714f33-3d8b-52a9-9ad7-3083de48719c'}, {'abstract': 'A poem about admitting you’re a poet.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2013/08/18/books/review/the-art-of-passing-for-them.html', 'snippet': 'A poem about admitting you’re a poet.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Please. Get back in your body. All I said was“I’m a poet.” I tried not to. We get along fineuntil then, talking about golf which I don’t play,and tennis which my friends pay menot to play, about how much you hate teachersbecause they ruined your life, left you hatingeverything, so all you can do is “make money,and know nothing. Nothing! Horrible people, teachers.Horrible! I hope you don’t know one,”and since I am one, I know I’m passing.It must be the pearls. The off-the-shoulder dress.', 'print_section': 'BR', 'print_page': '27', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/08/18/books/review/0818-bks-HAMMOND-sub/0818-bks-HAMMOND-sub-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 169, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2013/08/18/books/review/0818-bks-HAMMOND-sub/0818-bks-HAMMOND-sub-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 169}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/08/18/books/review/0818-bks-HAMMOND-sub/0818-bks-HAMMOND-sub-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 288, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/08/18/books/review/0818-bks-HAMMOND-sub/0818-bks-HAMMOND-sub-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 575, 'width': 2048, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/08/18/books/review/0818-bks-HAMMOND-sub/0818-bks-HAMMOND-sub-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2013/08/18/books/review/0818-bks-HAMMOND-sub/0818-bks-HAMMOND-sub-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/08/18/books/review/0818-bks-HAMMOND-sub/0818-bks-HAMMOND-sub-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'The Art of Passing for Them', 'kicker': 'Poem', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'The Art of Passing for Them', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Poetry and Poets', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Books and Literature', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2013-08-16T16:11:44+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'BookReview', 'section_name': 'Books', 'subsection_name': 'Book Review', 'byline': {'original': 'By Mary Stewart Hammond', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Mary', 'middlename': 'Stewart', 'lastname': 'Hammond', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/e0b525b2-2078-52c5-9e0c-90ff7dd6bd23', 'word_count': 1007, 'uri': 'nyt://article/e0b525b2-2078-52c5-9e0c-90ff7dd6bd23'}, {'abstract': 'Mitchell S. Jackson’s novel tackles poverty, addiction and the ravages of drugs.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2013/08/18/books/review/mitchell-s-jacksons-residue-years.html', 'snippet': 'Mitchell S. Jackson’s novel tackles poverty, addiction and the ravages of drugs.', 'lead_paragraph': 'In Mitchell S. Jackson’s powerful debut novel, “The Residue Years,” we know how things will end for Champ Thomas and his mother, Grace, before we know how they get there. In the prologue — one of the few I’ve read that is organic to the story that follows — a mother is visiting her son in prison. This imbues what follows with a sense of hopelessness that becomes ever sharper in contrast to the novel itself, which is, in its way, full of impossible hope.', 'print_section': 'BR', 'print_page': '9', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/08/18/books/review/18GAY/18GAY-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 616, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2013/08/18/books/review/18GAY/18GAY-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 616}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/08/18/books/review/18GAY/18GAY-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 1024, 'width': 998, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/08/18/books/review/18GAY/18GAY-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 2045, 'width': 1992, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/08/18/books/review/18GAY/18GAY-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2013/08/18/books/review/18GAY/18GAY-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/08/18/books/review/18GAY/18GAY-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Nickel and Dimed', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Nickel and Dimed', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Gay, Roxane', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Books and Literature', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Jackson, Mitchell S', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2013-08-16T16:11:56+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'BookReview', 'section_name': 'Books', 'subsection_name': 'Book Review', 'byline': {'original': 'By Roxane Gay', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Roxane', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Gay', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Review', '_id': 'nyt://article/17fe9179-4618-5115-abc6-b90bdff2879a', 'word_count': 879, 'uri': 'nyt://article/17fe9179-4618-5115-abc6-b90bdff2879a'}, {'abstract': 'A story of modern India, told through the ups and downs of one of its boldest enterprises: cricket’s Indian Premier League.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2013/08/18/books/review/the-great-tamasha-by-james-astill.html', 'snippet': 'A story of modern India, told through the ups and downs of one of its boldest enterprises: cricket’s Indian Premier League.', 'lead_paragraph': 'For two months in spring, the Indian Premier League is watched more than anything else on Indian television. Test cricket is played between nations over five days, without guarantee of a winner. I.P.L. matches last three hours and are played between Indian teams owned by businessmen and movie stars. Results are guaranteed. ', 'print_section': 'BR', 'print_page': '10', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/08/18/books/review/18BATTACHARYA/18BATTACHARYA-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 303, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2013/08/18/books/review/18BATTACHARYA/18BATTACHARYA-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 303}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/08/18/books/review/18BATTACHARYA/18BATTACHARYA-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 516, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/08/18/books/review/18BATTACHARYA/18BATTACHARYA-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 1033, 'width': 2048, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/08/18/books/review/18BATTACHARYA/18BATTACHARYA-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2013/08/18/books/review/18BATTACHARYA/18BATTACHARYA-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/08/18/books/review/18BATTACHARYA/18BATTACHARYA-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'A Sporting Chance', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'A Sporting Chance', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Books and Literature', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Cricket (Game)', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Astill, James', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2013-08-16T16:12:04+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'BookReview', 'section_name': 'Books', 'subsection_name': 'Book Review', 'byline': {'original': 'By Rahul Bhattacharya', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Rahul', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Bhattacharya', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Review', '_id': 'nyt://article/930d7f7e-dedc-5e7e-ac16-4afbe8b5b616', 'word_count': 1129, 'uri': 'nyt://article/930d7f7e-dedc-5e7e-ac16-4afbe8b5b616'}, {'abstract': 'A group of investigators pursues a horror auteur known for shunning the public eye.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2013/08/18/books/review/night-film-by-marisha-pessl.html', 'snippet': 'A group of investigators pursues a horror auteur known for shunning the public eye.', 'lead_paragraph': 'The true believers come out at night. They meet in condemned buildings marked by the symbol of a stylized eye to watch a “night film” — one of the psychologically punishing horror pictures of Stanislas Cordova, a master filmmaker who has shunned publicity with a zeal that makes Thomas Pynchon look like a shameless attention hog. Adding to the mystique is a sense that Cordova might have retreated because he has something unsavory to hide. Bad things happen to those who try to pick his locked closet for a look at the skeletons. Consider the star reporter Scott McGrath, who plunged into disgrace after a preliminary investigation into Cordova’s secrets blew up in his face. McGrath has a score to settle, so when Cordova’s achingly gifted daughter turns up dead in a Manhattan ruin, it’s Take 2 for the unemployed journalist. Nor is McGrath the only one who wants to know what’s been going on behind the camera; in short order he’s joined by a shabby but smoldering drug dealer named Hopper and a breathless ingénue, Nora Halliday. Together, the three make an adorably awkward family of misfits, who will be even more appealing should George Clooney, Ryan Gosling and Alison Brie be cast for the movie adaptation.', 'print_section': 'BR', 'print_page': '11', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/08/18/books/review/0818-bks-HILL/0818-bks-HILL-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 386, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2013/08/18/books/review/0818-bks-HILL/0818-bks-HILL-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 386}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/08/18/books/review/0818-bks-HILL/0818-bks-HILL-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 658, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/08/18/books/review/0818-bks-HILL/0818-bks-HILL-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 1317, 'width': 2048, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/08/18/books/review/0818-bks-HILL/0818-bks-HILL-thumbStandard-v2.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2013/08/18/books/review/0818-bks-HILL/0818-bks-HILL-thumbStandard-v2.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/08/18/books/review/0818-bks-HILL/0818-bks-HILL-thumbLarge-v2.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Underground Idol', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Underground Idol', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Hill, Joe', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Pessl, Marisha', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Books and Literature', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2013-08-16T16:12:27+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'BookReview', 'section_name': 'Books', 'subsection_name': 'Book Review', 'byline': {'original': 'By Joe Hill', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Joe', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Hill', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Review', '_id': 'nyt://article/2e26776d-808f-5538-8c3a-20b7d0895724', 'word_count': 1121, 'uri': 'nyt://article/2e26776d-808f-5538-8c3a-20b7d0895724'}, {'abstract': 'What started with curiosity and conversation ends with vivid discoveries on the Italian island.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2013/08/18/travel/digging-up-family-roots-in-sicily.html', 'snippet': 'What started with curiosity and conversation ends with vivid discoveries on the Italian island.', 'lead_paragraph': 'As a writer I’ve always tended to seek out origins. My first book, about the search for the historical Jesus, was an attempt to get at the “real” story behind my Catholic upbringing. After living in Manhattan for several years, I wrote “The Island at the Center of the World,” a book about the Dutch settlement of New Amsterdam, the seed from which New York City grew.', 'print_section': 'TR', 'print_page': '1', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/08/18/travel/18SICILY_SPAN-sf/18SICILY_SPAN-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 646, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2013/08/18/travel/18SICILY_SPAN-sf/18SICILY_SPAN-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 646}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/08/18/travel/18SICILY_SPAN-sf/18SICILY_SPAN-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 1024, 'width': 952, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/08/18/travel/18SICILY_SPAN-sf/18SICILY_SPAN-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 2048, 'width': 1904, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': 