d', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Video', '_id': 'nyt://video/dcccdccb-bbeb-5e24-a3bf-cff0ba6d7ff8', 'word_count': 0, 'uri': 'nyt://video/dcccdccb-bbeb-5e24-a3bf-cff0ba6d7ff8'}, {'abstract': 'In August 1863, the Union began to pierce through the Cumberland Plateau and into the Confederate heartland.', 'web_url': 'https://opinionator.blogs.nytimes.com/2013/08/16/across-the-cumberlands/', 'snippet': 'In August 1863, the Union began to pierce through the Cumberland Plateau and into the Confederate heartland.', 'lead_paragraph': 'On Aug. 16, 1863, Henry Campbell recorded in his diary how his unit, the 18th Indiana Battery, had just climbed the front range of the Cumberland Mountains, which separate the fertile Cumberland Basin of middle Tennessee from the eastern part of the state. A rainstorm impeded their ascent, making the road “exceedingly steep, slippery, and winding.” As they labored upward, the soldiers were enveloped by a dense fog. Suddenly, as they approached the top of the mountain, they found themselves entirely above the clouds, looking down, Campbell wrote, on “one of the grandest sights I ever witnessed.” Bright sunlight poured through the rain, creating “hundreds of rain bows on the sides of the mountains beneath us.” The 18-year-old Hoosier struggled to convey in words a sublime spectacle “that no pen could describe.”', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Across the Cumberlands', 'kicker': 'Disunion', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Civil War (US) (1861-65)', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Chattanooga (Tenn)', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2013-08-16T16:30:44+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Opinion', 'byline': {'original': 'By daniel w. crofts', 'person': [], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 