', 'url': 'images/2013/08/18/arts/18WORLDSEND1_SPAN/18WORLDSEND1-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 400, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2013/08/18/arts/18WORLDSEND1_SPAN/18WORLDSEND1-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 400}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/08/18/arts/18WORLDSEND1_SPAN/18WORLDSEND1-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 683, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/08/18/arts/18WORLDSEND1_SPAN/18WORLDSEND1-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 1365, 'width': 2048, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/08/18/arts/18WORLDSEND1_SPAN/18WORLDSEND1-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2013/08/18/arts/18WORLDSEND1_SPAN/18WORLDSEND1-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/08/18/arts/18WORLDSEND1_SPAN/18WORLDSEND1-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'They’ve Come Back for One Last Pint', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'They’ve Come Back for One Last Pint', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Movies', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Wright, Edgar', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Pegg, Simon', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Frost, Nick', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'creative_works', 'value': "The World's End (Movie)", 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2013-08-16T18:23:01+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Arts&Leisure', 'section_name': 'Movies', 'byline': {'original': 'By Dave Itzkoff', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Dave', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Itzkoff', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Interview', '_id': 'nyt://article/3bad8583-e195-5bf0-a99c-c152b1b1608f', 'word_count': 1252, 'uri': 'nyt://article/3bad8583-e195-5bf0-a99c-c152b1b1608f'}, {'abstract': 'A tale of two predictive successes.', 'web_url': 'https://krugman.blogs.nytimes.com/2013/08/16/stagflation-stagnation-and-intellectual-asymmetry/', 'snippet': 'A tale of two predictive successes.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Brad DeLong weighs in on the Friedman legacy, and notes that there have been two big successful sort of meta-predictions in macroeconomics over the past half-century. In the late 60s, Friedman and Phelps warned of the risk of stagflation, and were vindicated in the 1970s. In the late 1990s, some economists warned of the risk of Depression-type liquidity traps, and were vindicated in the Naughties. The first success led to Friedmam’s promotion to demigod status; the second success has not produced any comparable elevation.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Stagflation, Stagnation, and Intellectual Asymmetry', 'kicker': 'Paul Krugman', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [], 'pub_date': '2013-08-16T18:27:12+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Opinion', 'byline': {'original': 'By Paul Krugman', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Paul', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Krugman', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/005431e9-1afa-5975-9992-8cc2f0374b8a', 'word_count': 353, 'uri': 'nyt://article/005431e9-1afa-5975-9992-8cc2f0374b8a'}, {'abstract': 'A problem was resolved on Thursday night when a crew from the Park Service’s preservation unit smoothed the area where an inscription had been removed.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2013/08/17/us/politics/despite-fears-king-memorial-is-repaired-before-anniversary.html', 'snippet': 'A problem was resolved on Thursday night when a crew from the Park Service’s preservation unit smoothed the area where an inscription had been removed.', 'lead_paragraph': 'WASHINGTON — Repairs to the Martin Luther King Jr. Memorial have been completed, erasing doubts about whether the site would be ready for the 50th anniversary of the March on Washington and Dr. King’s “I Have a Dream” speech on Aug. 28.', 'print_section': 'A', 'print_page': '13', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/08/17/us/MEMORIAL/MEMORIAL-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 399, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2013/08/17/us/MEMORIAL/MEMORIAL-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 399}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/08/17/us/MEMORIAL/MEMORIAL-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 681, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/08/17/us/MEMORIAL/MEMORIAL-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 1361, 'width': 2048, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/08/17/us/MEMORIAL/MEMORIAL-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2013/08/17/us/MEMORIAL/MEMORIAL-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/08/17/us/MEMORIAL/MEMORIAL-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Despite Fears, King Memorial Is Ready for Anniversary', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Despite Fears, King Memorial Is Ready for Anniversary', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Monuments and Memorials (Structures)', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'King, Martin Luther Jr', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'National Park Service', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Lei Yixin', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Washington (DC)', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2013-08-16T18:30:11+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'National', 'section_name': 'U.S.', 'byline': {'original': 'By Ashley Southall', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Ashley', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Southall', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/439c180e-2a74-5533-a66a-18d74029bedd', 'word_count': 567, 'uri': 'nyt://article/439c180e-2a74-5533-a66a-18d74029bedd'}, {'abstract': 'When a vast, unsupervised organization makes a mistake, it can amount to a serious abuse of government authority.', 'web_url': 'https://takingnote.blogs.nytimes.com/2013/08/16/what-happens-when-the-n-s-a-gets-sloppy/', 'snippet': 'When a vast, unsupervised organization makes a mistake, it can amount to a serious abuse of government authority.', 'lead_paragraph': 'All institutions make mistakes, but when an organization as vast and unsupervised as the National Security Agency gets sloppy, the mistakes quickly amount to a serious abuse of government authority. Barton Gellman’s article today in the Washington Post was remarkable not just because it showed widespread incompetence by the agency, but because it showed the real dangers in allowing the N.S.A. to exercise overly broad power with almost no oversight.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'What Happens When the N.S.A. Gets Sloppy', 'kicker': 'Taking Note', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Surveillance of Citizens by Government', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Gellman, Barton', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'National Security Agency', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2013-08-16T18:31:00+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Opinion', 'byline': {'original': 'By David Firestone', 'person': [{'firstname': 'David', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Firestone', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/13a0b89b-777f-5a29-99aa-141df7d1a046', 'word_count': 710, 'uri': 'nyt://article/13a0b89b-777f-5a29-99aa-141df7d1a046'}, {'abstract': 'With his latest film, “The Grandmaster,” set for release on Friday, the director Wong Kar-wai reflects on his history with kung fu movies.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2013/08/18/movies/wong-kar-wai-on-his-new-film-the-grandmaster.html', 'snippet': 'With his latest film, “The Grandmaster,” set for release on Friday, the director Wong Kar-wai reflects on his history with kung fu movies.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Wong Kar-wai was recounting his research for “The Grandmaster,” the tale of the kung fu innovator who trained Bruce Lee. As he described crisscrossing the mainland to talk to aging martial artists, he could have been setting the scene for a movie.', 'print_section': 'AR', 'print_page': '9', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/08/18/arts/18WONG_SPAN/18WONG_SPAN-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 400, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2013/08/18/arts/18WONG_SPAN/18WONG_SPAN-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 400}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/08/18/arts/18WONG_SPAN/18WONG_SPAN-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 683, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/08/18/arts/18WONG_SPAN/18WONG_SPAN-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 1365, 'width': 2048, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/08/18/arts/18WONG_SPAN/18WONG_SPAN-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2013/08/18/arts/18WONG_SPAN/18WONG_SPAN-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/08/18/arts/18WONG_SPAN/18WONG_SPAN-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'A Kung Fu That Maims and Romances', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'A Kung Fu That Maims and Romances', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Rapold, Nicolas', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Movies', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Martial Arts', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Wong Kar-Wai', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'creative_works', 'value': 'The Grandmaster (Movie)', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2013-08-16T18:36:45+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Arts&Leisure', 'section_name': 'Movies', 'byline': {'original': 'By Nicolas Rapold', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Nicolas', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Rapold', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/3c015566-8bb2-5796-87f8-3edcc2997226', 'word_count': 1040, 'uri': 'nyt://article/3c015566-8bb2-5796-87f8-3edcc2997226'}, {'abstract': 'The Gap App Challenge was designed to help public schools through technological innovation.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2013/08/18/nyregion/closing-the-proficiency-gap-in-new-york-schools.html', 'snippet': 'The Gap App Challenge was designed to help public schools through technological innovation.', 'lead_paragraph': 'As befits a tech mogul, Mayor Michael R. Bloomberg has been a resolute supporter of digital initiatives in New York City, from hiring the city’s first chief digital officer, Rachel Sterne Haot, to strong patronage of the thriving Silicon Alley start-up community.', 'print_section': 'MB', 'print_page': '2', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/08/18/nyregion/18APPCITY/18APPCITY-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2013/08/18/nyregion/18APPCITY/18APPCITY-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Closing the Proficiency Gap in New York Schools', 'kicker': 'App City', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Closing the Proficiency Gap in New York Schools', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Mobile Applications', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Bromwich, Jonah', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Education (K-12)', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Bloomberg, Michael R', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'New York City', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Mathematics', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2013-08-16T18:37:36+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Metro', 'section_name': 'New York', 'byline': {'original': 'By Jonah Engel Bromwich', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Jonah', 'middlename': 'Engel', 'lastname': 'Bromwich', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/54923f0b-f077-5425-952e-1e97524790f8', 'word_count': 516, 'uri': 'nyt://article/54923f0b-f077-5425-952e-1e97524790f8'}, {'abstract': 'It’s both too high and too low.', 'web_url': 'https://krugman.blogs.nytimes.com/2013/08/16/schroedingers-price-index/', 'snippet': 'It’s both too high and too low.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Apparently Republicans are trying, once again, to extract chain-linking of Social Security benefits as the price of some kind of deal. I have no idea whether this will go anywhere; my guess, or maybe just my hope, is that Obama the Grand Bargainer has vanished from the scene.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Schroedinger’s Price Index', 'kicker': 'Paul Krugman', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [], 'pub_date': '2013-08-16T18:39:39+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Opinion', 'byline': {'original': 'By Paul Krugman', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Paul', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Krugman', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/90224dce-31cd-5aca-8c81-4785f1866c69', 'word_count': 247, 'uri': 'nyt://article/90224dce-31cd-5aca-8c81-4785f1866c69'}, {'abstract': 'A day in the life of armed America.', 'web_url': 'https://nocera.blogs.nytimes.com/2013/08/16/the-gun-report-august-16-2013/', 'snippet': 'A day in the life of armed America.', 'lead_paragraph': 'The Gun Report began seven months ago, after I wrote a column that simply grabbed examples of gun violence from the week’s news. Since then, we have tried to document the awful range and scope of daily gun violence, from accidents involving small children who get their hands on guns, to drive-by shootings, to killings that transpire in a flash of anger. Now, however, the Gun Report is going to take a short break. We will be back after Labor Day.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'The Gun Report: August 16, 2013', 'kicker': 'Joe Nocera', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [], 'pub_date': '2013-08-16T18:46:41+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Opinion', 'byline': {'original': 'By Joe Nocera', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Joe', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Nocera', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/339c9c75-3ede-5f98-880b-f3479282078f', 'word_count': 1565, 'uri': 'nyt://article/339c9c75-3ede-5f98-880b-f3479282078f'}, {'abstract': 'The Southern California skateboarder-turned-street artist praises the kinetic sculptures of his fellow West Coast innovator.', 'web_url': 'https://tmagazine.blogs.nytimes.com/2013/08/16/artists-on-artists-chris-johanson-on-the-apocalyptic-machines-of-kal-spelletich/', 'snippet': 'The Southern California skateboarder-turned-street artist praises the kinetic sculptures of his fellow West Coast innovator.', 'lead_paragraph': 'After a two-year hiatus, the skateboarder-turned-art Chris Johanson is back in a major way, with his first comprehensive museum solo show, “Within the River of Time Is My Mind,” at the Museum of Contemporary Art Los Angeles (through Oct. 13) and a self-titled new monograph from Phaidon.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Artists on Artists | Chris Johanson on the Apocalyptic Machines of Kal Spelletich', 'kicker': 'T Magazine', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Art', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Burning Man Festival', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Johanson, Chris', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'creative_works', 'value': 'Slacker (Movie)', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2013-08-16T18:48:15+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'T Magazine', 'byline': {'original': 'By Carol Kino', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Carol', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Kino', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/b3b3aa2b-6890-56a0-9dc8-c2ef6d4a6eab', 'word_count': 443, 'uri': 'nyt://article/b3b3aa2b-6890-56a0-9dc8-c2ef6d4a6eab'}, {'abstract': 'Sherone Lewis of Crown Heights, a call center manager for the city’s 311 line, seeks spiritual sustenance before reporting to a job that can be rather challenging.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2013/08/18/nyregion/a-prayer-before-the-complaints-roll-in.html', 'snippet': 'Sherone Lewis of Crown Heights, a call center manager for the city’s 311 line, seeks spiritual sustenance before reporting to a job that can be rather challenging.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Sherone Lewis works for the City of New York and loves her job — a common enough sentiment, you might say, but consider this: For the last 11 years, Ms. Lewis, 46, has worked for the city’s 311 line, managing the complaints and dissatisfactions of a populace that approaches them as Olympic events. Ms. Lewis started as a call center representative, or C.C.R., in December 2002, just as the Bloomberg administration was consolidating the city’s help lines into 311, and is now a call center manager. Sundays often find her working the evening shift downtown, commuting from her apartment in Crown Heights, Brooklyn, which she shares with her 25-year-old daughter.', 'print_section': 'MB', 'print_page': '2', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/08/18/nyregion/18ROUTINE3_SPAN/18ROUTINE3-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 400, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2013/08/18/nyregion/18ROUTINE3_SPAN/18ROUTINE3-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 400}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/08/18/nyregion/18ROUTINE3_SPAN/18ROUTINE3-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 683, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/08/18/nyregion/18ROUTINE3_SPAN/18ROUTINE3-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 1365, 'width': 2048, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/08/18/nyregion/18ROUTINE3_SPAN/18ROUTINE3-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2013/08/18/nyregion/18ROUTINE3_SPAN/18ROUTINE3-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/08/18/nyregion/18ROUTINE3_SPAN/18ROUTINE3-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'A Prayer Before the Complaints Roll In', 'kicker': 'Sunday Routine', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'A Prayer Before the Complaints Roll In', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Three-One-One (311) (NYC Hot Line)', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'New York City', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2013-08-16T18:51:28+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Metro', 'section_name': 'New York', 'byline': {'original': 'By John Leland', 'person': [{'firstname': 'John', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Leland', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/489a865c-13b6-5c30-b3aa-6865029f9413', 'word_count': 778, 'uri': 'nyt://article/489a865c-13b6-5c30-b3aa-6865029f9413'}, {'abstract': 'If you divorced with very young children, what do you know now that you wish you had known then?', 'web_url': 'https://parenting.blogs.nytimes.com/2013/08/16/can-a-child-be-too-young-for-shared-custody/', 'snippet': 'If you divorced with very young children, what do you know now that you wish you had known then?', 'lead_paragraph': 'A reader finds herself “abruptly” getting divorced, with two small children: 15 months and 5½ years old. The circumstances of the divorce aren’t happy ones, and she is angry and upset, but writes that she is trying “to protect the relationship my children have with their father while still providing them with the necessary stability for them to thrive.”', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Can a Child Be Too Young for Shared Custody?', 'kicker': 'Motherlode', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Children and Childhood', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Divorce, Separations and Annulments', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Parenting', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2013-08-16T18:52:37+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Style', 'byline': {'original': "By Kj Dell'Antonia", 'person': [{'firstname': 'Kj', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': "Dell'Antonia", 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/3cfee22f-b6f4-5383-ae27-61ab7e0bbb18', 'word_count': 175, 'uri': 'nyt://article/3cfee22f-b6f4-5383-ae27-61ab7e0bbb18'}, {'abstract': '“Betty Boop: The Essential Collection” features the Fleischer Brothers’ immortal animated song-and-dance star in remastered cartoons.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2013/08/18/movies/homevideo/betty-boop-the-essential-collection-comes-to-blu-ray.html', 'snippet': '“Betty Boop: The Essential Collection” features the Fleischer Brothers’ immortal animated song-and-dance star in remastered cartoons.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Betty Boop began her life as a floppy-eared dog and ended it as a happy homemaker dressed in an apron. But at her peak — roughly from 1931 to 1934 — she was a major figure in American movies, despite being, as her theme song put it, “made of pen and ink.”', 'print_section': 'AR', 'print_page': '10', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/08/18/arts/18KEHR/18KEHR-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 400, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2013/08/18/arts/18KEHR/18KEHR-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 400}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/08/18/arts/18KEHR/18KEHR-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 683, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/08/18/arts/18KEHR/18KEHR-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 787, 'width': 1180, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/08/18/arts/18KEHR/18KEHR-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2013/08/18/arts/18KEHR/18KEHR-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/08/18/arts/18KEHR/18KEHR-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Boop-Boop-a-Doo on Blu-ray', 'kicker': 'On Video', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Boop-Boop-a-Doo on Blu-ray', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Kehr, Dave', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'DVD (Digital Versatile Disc)', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Cartoons and Cartoonists', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Video Recordings and Downloads', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Movies', 'rank': 8, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2013-08-16T18:55:37+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Arts&Leisure', 'section_name': 'Movies', 'subsection_name': 'DVD', 'byline': {'original': 'By Dave Kehr', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Dave', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Kehr', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/23266e84-0660-59dd-a7f5-4700af6aa27d', 'word_count': 1400, 'uri': 'nyt://article/23266e84-0660-59dd-a7f5-4700af6aa27d'}, {'abstract': '“It’s the dream of most of the chefs to open their own place someday and for me the moment is now,” the chef said.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2013/08/17/dining/gabriel-kreuther-will-leave-the-modern.html', 'snippet': '“It’s the dream of most of the chefs to open their own place someday and for me the moment is now,” the chef said.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Gabriel Kreuther is stepping down as executive chef at the Modern, where he has been since 2004. “It’s the dream of most of the chefs to open their own place someday and for me the moment is now,” he said. He probably will not leave before the end of the year, he said. In announcing the news, Union Square Hospitality Group, which runs the high-end restaurant at the Museum of Modern Art along with its cafe and private dining room, said it wished him the best and was “beginning a search for out next executive chef.”', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/08/16/dining/momachef-16/momachef-16-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 400, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2013/08/16/dining/momachef-16/momachef-16-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 400}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/08/16/dining/momachef-16/momachef-16-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 683, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/08/16/dining/momachef-16/momachef-16-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 1365, 'width': 2048, 'subType': 'supe