document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Arts&Leisure', 'section_name': 'Arts', 'subsection_name': 'Music', 'byline': {'original': 'By Corinna da Fonseca-Wollheim', 'person': [{'firstname': '', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': '', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/426b9638-a521-5e9d-8ab2-5e849dc44e9d', 'word_count': 1532, 'uri': 'nyt://article/426b9638-a521-5e9d-8ab2-5e849dc44e9d'}, {'abstract': 'Tuesday’s episode of “Rizzoli & Isles” on TNT gives fans of this reliable series another look at Charles Hoyt, one of the most memorable recurring characters the show has had.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2013/08/18/arts/television/no-rest-in-peace-for-the-wicked.html', 'snippet': 'Tuesday’s episode of “Rizzoli & Isles” on TNT gives fans of this reliable series another look at Charles Hoyt, one of the most memorable recurring characters the show has had.', 'lead_paragraph': '“America loves a serial killer,” the detective Jane Rizzoli (Angie Harmon) says in Tuesday’s episode of “Rizzoli & Isles,” at 9 p.m. on TNT. So why not bring one back from the dead? The episode, “No One Mourns the Wicked,” gives fans of this reliable series another look at Charles Hoyt, one of the most memorable recurring characters the show has had since its debut in 2010. Hoyt was Rizzoli’s archnemesis until he was killed off in Season 2. Or was he? ', 'print_section': 'AR', 'print_page': '2', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/08/18/arts/18GUIDE10/18GUIDE10-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 400, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2013/08/18/arts/18GUIDE10/18GUIDE10-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 400}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/08/18/arts/18GUIDE10/18GUIDE10-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 683, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/08/18/arts/18GUIDE10/18GUIDE10-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 1365, 'width': 2048, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/08/18/arts/18GUIDE10/18GUIDE10-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2013/08/18/arts/18GUIDE10/18GUIDE10-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/08/18/arts/18GUIDE10/18GUIDE10-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'No Rest in Peace for the Wicked', 'kicker': 'The Week Ahead | Television', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'No Rest in Peace For the Wicked', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Television', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2013-08-16T19:05:50+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Arts&Leisure', 'section_name': 'Arts', 'subsection_name': 'Television', 'byline': {'original': 'By Neil Genzlinger', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Neil', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Genzlinger', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/ee43d3c1-f2a0-5c8f-aa09-7c99ddbaf750', 'word_count': 187, 'uri': 'nyt://article/ee43d3c1-f2a0-5c8f-aa09-7c99ddbaf750'}, {'abstract': '“Mr. Burns, a Post-Electric Play,” by Anne Washburn, tackles global disaster, adaptation and the peculiar power of stories passed down over generations.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2013/08/18/theater/release-the-hounds-of-memory.html', 'snippet': '“Mr. Burns, a Post-Electric Play,” by Anne Washburn, tackles global disaster, adaptation and the peculiar power of stories passed down over generations.', 'lead_paragraph': '\xa0This is not a comparison that any playwright wants to have to live up to, but Anne Washburn has always struck me as the American author most like Caryl Churchill. Like that justly hailed British playwright, Ms. Washburn (“The Internationalist”) writes formally daring, ominous and prickly dramas that urgently address topical issues while creating worlds that seem very foreign. In her new work, “Mr. Burns, a Post-Electric Play,” which drew raves in its run in Washington, she tackles global disaster, adaptation and the peculiar power of stories passed down over generations. ', 'print_section': 'AR', 'print_page': '2', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/08/18/arts/18GUIDE1_SPAN/18GUIDE1-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 400, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2013/08/18/arts/18GUIDE1_SPAN/18GUIDE1-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 400}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/08/18/arts/18GUIDE1_SPAN/18GUIDE1-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 683, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/08/18/arts/18GUIDE1_SPAN/18GUIDE1-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 1365, 'width': 2048, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/08/18/arts/18GUIDE1_SPAN/18GUID