 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/fce985bb-905e-5cd7-8d27-2d76013af98d', 'word_count': 404, 'uri': 'nyt://article/fce985bb-905e-5cd7-8d27-2d76013af98d'}, {'abstract': 'India’s currency fell sharply against the dollar. Inflation in the country remains high even as growth is slowing.', 'web_url': 'https://takingnote.blogs.nytimes.com/2013/08/16/the-rupees-decline/', 'snippet': 'India’s currency fell sharply against the dollar. Inflation in the country remains high even as growth is slowing.', 'lead_paragraph': 'The economies of many emerging markets are struggling, as Nathaniel Popper pointed out in The Times earlier this week. In China, for example, credit has dried up so much that all but one of the car dealers in the once booming city of Shenmu in the northwest have failed. Perhaps the country the world should be most worried about is India. The country’s currency, the rupee, fell sharply against the dollar on Friday, briefly passing 62 to the dollar; it was at 55 to the dollar as recently as May. Inflation remains high even as growth is slowing.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'The Rupee’s Decline', 'kicker': 'Taking Note', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Indian Rupee (Currency)', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'India', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2013-08-16T20:01:00+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Opinion', 'byline': {'original': 'By Vikas Bajaj', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Vikas', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Bajaj', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/5931a796-a32e-5d1b-862d-38a0f7cc85d2', 'word_count