ar you-know-where in the late Giuliani years, sometimes the challenge is keeping the planets out of one another’s orbits, its chef and owner said.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2013/08/18/nyregion/near-seat-of-government-seats-of-power.html', 'snippet': 'At City Hall, the power-lunch spot that opened near you-know-where in the late Giuliani years, sometimes the challenge is keeping the planets out of one another’s orbits, its chef and owner said.', 'lead_paragraph': 'At City Hall, the power-lunch spot that opened near you-know-where in the late Giuliani years, sometimes the challenge is keeping the planets out of one another’s orbits, said Henry Meer, the chef and owner. Mayor Michael R. Bloomberg favors Table 41, a booth along the western wall — as do Raymond W. Kelly, the police commissioner, and Cyrus R. Vance Jr., the Manhattan district attorney. Representative Gregory W. Meeks typically takes Table 21, and the mayoral candidate Joseph J. Lhota’s regular spot is Table 23. Judges, lobbyists and officials have their turf. Though Mr. Meer tries to keep regulars at their regular tables, sometimes the powers’ lunch needs overlap. “In that case, I usually give the table to whoever called first,” Mr. Meer said.', 'print_section': 'MB', 'print_page': '3', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/08/18/nyregion/18TABLE_SPAN/18TABLE-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 400, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2013/08/18/nyregion/18TABLE_SPAN/18TABLE-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 400}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/08/18/nyregion/18TABLE_SPAN/18TABLE-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 683, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': Non