mage', 'url': 'images/2013/08/18/sunday-review/18download/18download-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 2048, 'width': 581, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/08/18/sunday-review/18download/18download-thumbStandard-v2.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2013/08/18/sunday-review/18download/18download-thumbStandard-v2.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/08/18/sunday-review/18download/18download-thumbLarge-v2.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Wendy Davis', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Wendy Davis', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Murphy, Kate', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Davis, Wendy', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Texas', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'ABORTION', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Elections, State Legislature', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2013-08-17T18:15:12+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'OpEd', 'section_name': 'Opinion', 'subsection_name': 'Sunday Review', 'byline': {'original': 'By Kate Murphy', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Kate', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Murphy', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Op-Ed', '_id': 'nyt://article/31dc2d5b-3a94-5bf5-851f-4f1100b6cee0', 'word_count': 421, 'uri': 'nyt://article/31dc2d5b-3a94-5bf5-851f-4f1100b6cee0'}, {'abstract': 'How the War of Rebellion became the Civil War.', 'web_url': 'https://opinionator.blogs.nytimes.com/2013/08/17/the-name-of-war/', 'snippet': 'How the War of Rebellion became the Civil War.', 'lead_paragraph': 'In 1881 the United States government published the first of many volumes of the official records of its war with the Confederate States of America. That massive resource has been a first port of call for historians, amateur and professional, since the moment of its publication; today digitization has made it even more widely accessible. Its shorthand nickname is the O.R., for Official Records. It can come as a surprise, then, to see that its full title is The War of the Rebellion: A Compilation of the Official Records of the Union and Confederate Armies. It was called that because the rebellion was what the people who actually fought the war, especially but not only on the Union side, were most likely to call it. Understanding why helps us to better understand how the war’s participants understood the conflict, and how they remembered it. ', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/04/25/opinion/opinionator-pog-disunion/opinionator-pog-disunion-thumbStandard.gif', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2014/04/25/opinion/opinionator-pog-disunion/opinionator-pog-disunion-thumbStandard.gif', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/04/25/opinion/opinionator-pog-disunion/opinionator-pog-disunion-thumbLarge.png', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'The Name of War', 'kicker': 'Disunion', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Civil War (US) (1861-65)', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Lincoln, Abraham', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2013-08-17T18:20:04+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'OpEd', 'section_name': 'Opinion', 'byline': {'original': 'By Chandra Manning and Adam Rothman', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Chandra', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Manning', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}, {'firstname': 'Adam', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Rothman', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 2}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/f9af7760-a625-570a-a302-8a0b821fb8a6', 'word_count': 1980, 'uri': 'nyt://article/f9af7760-a625-570a-a302-8a0b821fb8a6'}, {'abstract': 'Tomato farmers and canners move toward sustainability — and fairness.', 'web_url': 'https://opinionator.blogs.nytimes.com/2013/08/17/not-all-industrial-food-is-evil/', 'snippet': 'Tomato farmers and canners move toward sustainability — and fairness.', 'lead_paragraph': 'I’VE long wondered how producing a decent ingredient, one that you can buy in any supermarket, really happens. Take canned tomatoes, of which I probably use 100 pounds a year. It costs $2 to $3 a pound to buy hard, tasteless, “fresh” plum tomatoes, but only half that for almost two pounds of canned tomatoes that taste much better. How is that possible?', 'print_section': 'SR', 'print_page': '4', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Not All Industrial Food Is Evil', 'kicker': 'Mark Bittman', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Not All Industrial Food Is Evil', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Agriculture and Farming', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Factory Farming', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Tomatoes', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Pacific Coast Producers', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Rominger Brothers Farms', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'California', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2013-08-17T18:25:17+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Opinion', 'byline': {'original': 'By Mark Bittman', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Mark', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Bittman', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/d060cda9-b55d-53ca-a1f2-996662492f76', 'word_count': 1200, 'uri': 'nyt://article/d060cda9-b55d-53ca-a1f2-996662492f76'}, {'abstract': 'Plans to expand the number of top-level Internet domains beyond familiar ones like .com and .net have generated a rush of activity — as well as opposition.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2013/08/18/technology/when-you-cant-tell-web-suffixes-without-a-scorecard.html', 'snippet': 'Plans to expand the number of top-level Internet domains beyond familiar ones like .com and .net have generated a rush of activity — as well as opposition.', 'lead_paragraph': 'ON the Web, there’s no place like .home. ', 'print_section': 'BU', 'print_page': '3', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/08/18/business/18-TECHNO/18-TECHNO-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 1086, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2013/08/18/business/18-TECHNO/18-TECHNO-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 1086}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/08/18/business/18-TECHNO/18-TECHNO-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 1024, 'width': 566, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 