ginal': 'By Frank Bruni', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Frank', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Bruni', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Op-Ed', '_id': 'nyt://article/2187649e-948d-57a3-becf-577d63b724f4', 'word_count': 1135, 'uri': 'nyt://article/2187649e-948d-57a3-becf-577d63b724f4'}, {'abstract': 'Day care has become prohibitively expensive not just for the working poor, but for the middle class and upper middle class as well.', 'web_url': 'https://opinionator.blogs.nytimes.com/2013/08/17/crushed-by-the-cost-of-child-care/', 'snippet': 'Day care has become prohibitively expensive not just for the working poor, but for the middle class and upper middle class as well.', 'lead_paragraph': 'The difficulty of obtaining good, affordable day care is well known as a problem afflicting the working poor. But increasingly, middle- and upper-middle-class parents are finding that day care is hard to find or access and that even when it is available it is startlingly costly. Among the mothers I spoke to, one sent her daughter to a day care proprietor where the owner secretly had another woman mind all eight babies all day long; another signed up for a slot at a local day care when she was newly pregnant. Her daughter is now 5, and she is still on the wait list.', 'print_section': 'SR', 'print_page': '4', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Crushed by the Cost of Child Care', 'kicker': 'The Great Divide', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Crushed By the Cost of Child Care', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Child Care', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Day Care Centers', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Labor and Jobs', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Women and Girls', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'valu