5aa0-a1bb-fcbe92c8c952'}, {'abstract': 'Television highlights.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2013/08/18/arts/whats-on-sunday.html', 'snippet': 'Television highlights.', 'lead_paragraph': '9 P.M. (13, 49) THE LADY VANISHES In this “Masterpiece Mystery!” movie, based on Ethel Lina White’s 1936 novel, “The Wheel Spins,” from which Alfred Hitchcock adapted his 1938 thriller, “The Lady Vanishes,” Iris Carr (Tuppence Middleton, above, with Tom Hughes), a beautiful young socialite, is befriended on a train by an older Englishwoman named Miss Froy (Selina Cadell), who then disappears — after which Iris’s fellow passengers deny she ever existed. Only Max Hare (Mr. Hughes), the infatuated engineer, pays heed to Iris’s conviction. Is it fair to compare this film with Hitchcock’s? Mike Hale asked in The New York Times. “It doesn’t really matter, because if you’ve seen the original, it’s impossible to do otherwise,” he decided. “This new ‘Lady Vanishes,’ directed by Diarmuid Lawrence (‘South Riding’) from a screenplay by Fiona Seres, is a perfectly adequate television mystery of the week. But it forgoes the crackling pace, light touch and surprisingly sophisticated sexual banter of the original, opting for melancholy, ominousness and sentimentality.”', 'print_section': 'SP', 'print_page': '16', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'What’s On Sunday', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'What’s On Sunday', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [], 'pub_date': '2013-08-18T05:23:55+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Culture', 'section_name': 'Arts', 'byline': {'original': 'By Kathryn Shattuck', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Kathryn', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Shattuck', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Schedule', '_id': 'nyt://article/50afa2a8-9706-5365-ab71-21e57c695d71', 'word_count': 673,