': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/76844644-86c5-511a-ac2b-2f821da6e420', 'word_count': 516, 'uri': 'nyt://article/76844644-86c5-511a-ac2b-2f821da6e420'}, {'abstract': 'The string of options-backdating cases showed how difficult it was to prosecute senior executives for corporate misconduct that involves arcane accounting issues.', 'web_url': 'https://dealbook.nytimes.com/2013/08/19/end-of-the-options-backdating-era/', 'snippet': 'The string of options-backdating cases showed how difficult it was to prosecute senior executives for corporate misconduct that involves arcane accounting issues.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Before the clamor about the lack of prosecutions from the financial crisis and the current crackdown on insider trading, the practice of backdating stock options came to light seven years ago and prompted a flurry of prosecutions. The practice is now long forgotten, but it looks as if the last of those cases has finally concluded, and as the Grateful Dead put it so well, “What a long, strange trip it’s been.”', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'End of the Options Backdating Era', 'kicker': 'DealBook', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Executive Compensation', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Securities and Commodities Violations', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Stock Options and Purchase Plans', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'United States Attorneys', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Justice Department', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2013-08-19T17:03:16+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Business Day', 'byline': {'original': 'By Peter J. Henning', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Peter', 'middlename': 'J.', 'lastname': 'Henning', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/0baa1211-0c48-5430-85a1-6926340d1724', 'word_count': 949, 'uri': 'nyt://article/0baa1211-0c48-5430-85a1-6926340d1724'}, {'abstract': 'Booms are alike, busts differ.', 'web_url': 'https://krugman.blogs.nytimes.com/2013/08/19/anna-karenina-and-the-business-cycle/', 'snippet': 'Booms are alike, busts differ.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Antonio Fatas and Ilan Mihov have a new paper in which they propose rethinking the way we classify business cycles. I’m still trying to decide what I think of their methodology, and I’m troubled by their dates – for example, I don’t believe that the U.S. economy was operating at capacity in late 1992. But I’m very much in sympathy with their underlying view about the asymmetry between booms and busts; the question is why, exactly, it feels right.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Anna Karenina and the Business Cycle', 'kicker': 'Paul Krugman', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [], 'pub_date': '2013-08-19T17:03:21+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Opinion', 'byline': {'original': 'By Paul Krugman', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Paul', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Krugman', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/443d6646-dd8b-5f40-b4c7-bd34e05731bb', 'word_count': 597, 'uri': 'nyt://article/443d6646-dd8b-5f40-b4c7-bd34e05731bb'}, {'abstract': 'Brian Bennett joined PPC Associates, San Mateo, Calif., in a new post, vice president for sales and customer development. He had most recently been at Conductor, New York.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2013/08/19/business/media/accounts-and-people-of-note-in-the-advertising-industry.html', 'snippet': 'Brian Bennett joined PPC Associates, San Mateo, Calif., in a new post, vice president for sales and customer development. He had most recently been at Conductor, New York.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Brian Bennett joined PPC Associates, San Mateo, Calif., in a new post, vice president for sales and customer development. He had most recently been vice president for channels and partners at Conductor, New York.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Accounts and People of Note in the Advertising Industry', 'kicker': 'Webdenda', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Appointments and Executive Changes', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Public Relations and Publicity', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'ADVERTISING AND MARKETING', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2013-08-19T17:22:10+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Business', 'section_name': 'Business Day', 'subsection_name': 'Media', 'byline': {'original': 'By Stuart Elliott', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Stuart', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Elliott', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/f9993947-3ad6-5e9a-9725-bfe225696e8d', 'word_count': 1018, 'uri': 'nyt://article/f9993947-3ad6-5e9a-9725-bfe225696e8d'}, {'abstract': 'A counterintuitive — yet crucial — lesson about writing.', 'web_url': 'https://6thfloor.blogs.nytimes.com/2013/08/19/dont-write-what-you-know/', 'snippet': 'A counterintuitive — yet crucial — lesson about writing.', 'lead_paragraph': 'In another installment in which we ask writers to reflect on their own work, we had B.C. Edwards, author of this past Sunday’s Lives essay, share with us the most helpful piece of writing advice he ever received.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'he