N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Pebble Beach (Calif)', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2013-08-19T22:17:01+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Automobiles', 'byline': {'original': 'By Ronald Ahrens', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Ronald', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Ahrens', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/76523b48-e01b-5ab8-896c-ce4399727915', 'word_count': 278, 'uri': 'nyt://article/76523b48-e01b-5ab8-896c-ce4399727915'}, {'abstract': 'In “Old Cats,” a troubled daughter’s strained relationship with her elderly mother plays out in a Chilean apartment.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2013/08/20/movies/in-old-cats-bad-relatives-take-over-a-small-space.html', 'snippet': 'In “Old Cats,” a troubled daughter’s strained relationship with her elderly mother plays out in a Chilean apartment.', 'lead_paragraph': '“Old Cats” takes place almost entirely in an apartment in Santiago, Chile, occupied by Isadora (Bélgica Castro); her second husband, Enrique (Alejandro Sieveking); and the felines of the title, a pair of roly-poly tabbies described by Isadora as “the kings of the house.” The enclosed space and the age of the couple — as well as Isadora’s increasingly frequent lapses of lucidity — may remind you, at first, of “Amour,” Michael Haneke’s unsparing end-of-life excursion. But “Old Cats,” written and directed by Sebastián Silva and Pedro Peirano (originally released in Chile in 2010, long before “Amour”), is less concerned with the austere reality of death than with the stress and mess of living.', 'print_section': 'C', 'print_page': '3', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/08/20/arts/20OLDCATS/20OLDCATS-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 359, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name