ajor': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Company Reports', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Shopping and Retail', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Real Estate and Housing (Residential)', 'rank': 7, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2013-08-20T18:39:21+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Business', 'section_name': 'Business Day', 'byline': {'original': 'By Stephanie Clifford', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Stephanie', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Clifford', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/167c1879-74cc-5c59-904a-26ba516423d3', 'word_count': 905, 'uri': 'nyt://article/167c1879-74cc-5c59-904a-26ba516423d3'}, {'abstract': 'Youth and health are fleeting things.', 'web_url': 'https://krugman.blogs.nytimes.com/2013/08/20/they-cant-handle-the-health-care-truth/', 'snippet': 'Youth and health are fleeting things.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Aaron Carroll talks about the Republican health care dilemma, and makes a good point: it runs deeper than the specific fact that Obamacare looks the way it does because it has to. At the most fundamental level, you can’t guarantee adequate health care to everyone unless the people who don’t need help right now — the young, healthy, and affluent — are induced, one way or another, to contribute to the care of those who do need help. You can do this purely with taxes, via a single-payer system (and maybe even by having the government act as provider), or you can do it, Swiss or Massachusetts style, via a combination of regulation, taxes, and subsidies. But some way of corralling the lucky healthy into contributing is necessary.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'They Can’t Handle The Health Care Truth', 'kicker': 'Paul Krugman', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [], 'pub_date': '2013-08-20T18:40:42+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Opinion', 'byline': {'original': 'By Paul Krugman', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Paul', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Krugman', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/e02d5a57-7e4a-576c-946d-7412cd9f8b03', 'word_count': 281, 'uri': 'nyt://article/e02d5a57-7e4a-576c-946d-7412cd9f8b03'}, {'abstract': 'A new work from the playwright Chiori Miyagawa and a stage version of Flann O’Brien’s surreal crime novel “The Third Policeman” are part of La MaMa’s new season.', 'web_url': 'https://artsbeat.blogs.nytimes.com/2013/08/20/la-mama-announces-its-fall-season/', 'snippet': 'A new work from the playwright Chiori Miyagawa and a stage version of Flann O’Brien’s surreal crime novel “The Third Policeman” are part of La MaMa’s new season.', 'lead_paragraph': 'An adaptation of the Flann O’Brien novel “The Third Policeman” and new works from the director Irina Brook and the playwright Chiori Miyagawa are part of the fall season at La MaMa, the downtown theater company announced on Tuesday.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'La MaMa Announces Its Fall Season', 'kicker': 'ArtsBeat', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Theater', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'La MaMa ETC', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2013-08-20T18:44:09+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Arts', 'byline': {'original': 'By Erik Piepenburg', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Erik', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Piepenburg', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/adeb8d40-9ba1-5cdf-b