20130825_SNL-slide-ZA1W/20130825_SNL-slide-ZA1W-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': '‘SNL’ Through the Years', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [], 'pub_date': '2013-08-21T21:08:45+0000', 'document_type': 'multimedia', 'news_desk': 'Arts', 'section_name': 'Arts', 'subsection_name': 'Television', 'byline': {'original': '', 'person': [], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Slideshow', '_id': 'nyt://slideshow/cd84b9d1-43b6-57b2-bcc3-853e09a46050', 'word_count': 0, 'uri': 'nyt://slideshow/cd84b9d1-43b6-57b2-bcc3-853e09a46050'}, {'abstract': 'Television highlights.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2013/08/22/arts/television/whats-on-thursday.html', 'snippet': 'Television highlights.', 'lead_paragraph': ' 1 P.M. (IFC) THE CABLE GUY (1996) Jim Carrey plays Chip Douglas, a sociopathic cable television installer who first invades the home and then the personal and professional life of Steven M. Kovacs (Matthew Broderick, above left, with Mr. Carrey) in this dark comedy directed by Ben Stiller. Jack Black is the friend who warns Steven that Chip is not as harmless he initially appears. “Mr. Carrey, who won legions of fans just by speaking from his buttocks, now tries the creepy gambit of talking with a lisp, sneering at strangers, behaving like a deranged stalker and wallowing in sad, stale references to ancient television shows,” Janet Maslin wrote in The New York Times. His character “is a pure creature of the television age, and the film suggests he is to be both pitied and tuned out.”', 'print_section': 'C', 'print_page': '8', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'What’s On Thursday', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'What’s On Thursday', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [], 'pub_date': '2013-08-21T21:08