ion': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/86e9964c-6e79-56c8-83c3-8486dddef5b0', 'word_count': 419, 'uri': 'nyt://article/86e9964c-6e79-56c8-83c3-8486dddef5b0'}, {'abstract': 'The conflicted history of one of the Civil War’s worst atrocities.', 'web_url': 'https://opinionator.blogs.nytimes.com/2013/08/22/the-lawrence-massacre/', 'snippet': 'The conflicted history of one of the Civil War’s worst atrocities.', 'lead_paragraph': 'On April 20, 1866, a letter addressed to James Knox arrived in Chesterfield, S.C. Robert E. Lee had surrendered his once-mighty Army of Northern Virginia to Ulysses S. Grant more than a year prior — but the blood of R. H. Miller, the man who had sent the letter from his home in Lawrence, Kans., was still up. Miller outlined a general list of grievances against the ex-Confederacy and then quickly turned to the real subject of his letter: the Lawrence Massacre of Aug. 21, 1863.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'The Lawrence Massacre', 'kicker': 'Disunion', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Civil War (US) (1861-65)', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Kansas', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Missouri', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2013-08-22T17:47:33+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Opinion', 'byline': {'original': 'By Matthew C. Hulbert', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Matthew', 'middlename': 'C.', 'lastname': 'Hulbert', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/f3e111f6-364a-555b-b7a1-66b814b8f740', 'word_count': 1721, 'uri': 'nyt://article/f3e111f6-364a-555b-b7a1-66b814b8f740'}, {'abstract': 'Some polls show the Democratic candidate for governor, Ter