N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Police Department (NYC)', 'rank': 8, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'New York City', 'rank': 9, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'United States', 'rank': 10, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2013-08-22T19:27:22+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Opinion', 'byline': {'original': 'By Ross Douthat', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Ross', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Douthat', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/b2ef80da-76f3-5d9b-90c6-f7f5af1c1e2b', 'word_count': 1300, 'uri': 'nyt://article/b2ef80da-76f3-5d9b-90c6-f7f5af1c1e2b'}, {'abstract': 'Amanda Ripley follows three Americans abroad to examine how and why other nations educate their students so much more effectively than we do.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2013/08/25/books/review/amanda-ripleys-smartest-kids-in-the-world.html', 'snippet': 'Amanda Ripley follows three Americans abroad to examine how and why other nations educate their students so much more effectively than we do.', 'lead_paragraph': '“If you want the American dream, go to Finland.” These blunt words from a British politician, quoted by Amanda Ripley in “The Smartest Kids in the World,” may lead readers to imagine that her book belongs to a very particular and popular genre. We love to read about how other cultures do it better (stay slim, have sex, raise children). In this case, Ripley is offering to show how other nations educate students so much more effectively than we do, and her opening pages hold out a promising suggestion of masochistic satisfaction. “American educators described Finland as a silky paradise,” she writes, “a place where all the teachers were admired and all the children beloved.”', 'print_section': 'BR', 'print_page': '1', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/08/25/books/review/0825-bks-COVER/0825-bks-COVER-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 454, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2013/08/25/books/review/0825-bks-COVER/0825-bks-COVER-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 454}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/08/25/books/review/0825-bks-COVER/0825-bks-COVER-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 774, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/08/25/books/review/0825-bks-COVER/0825-bks-COVER-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 1549, 'width': 2048, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/08/25/books/review/0825-bks-COVER/0825-bks-COVER-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2013/08/25/books/review/0825-bks-COVER/0825-bks-COVER-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/08/25/books/review/0825-bks-COVER/0825-bks-COVER-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Likely to Succeed', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Likely to Succeed', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Paul, Annie Murphy', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Ripley, Amanda', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Books and Literature', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Education', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2013-08-22T19:32:24+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'BookReview', 'section_name': 'Books', 'subsection_name': 'Book Review', 'byline': {'original': 'By Annie Murphy Paul', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Annie', 'middlename': 'Murphy', 'lastname': 'Paul', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Review', '_id': 'nyt://article/0f9a03b1-7abb-548c-b750-988c355235d5', 'word_count': 1191, 'uri': 'nyt://article/0f9a03b1-7abb-548c-b750-988c355235d5'}, {'abstract': 'A son’s seemingly precocious love of words is identified as a rare disorder.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2013/08/25/books/review/early-reader.html', 'snippet': 'A son’s seemingly precocious love of words is identified as a rare disorder.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Dreams, books, are each a world; and books, we know,Are a substantial world, both pure and good:Round these, with tendrils strong as flesh and blood,Our pastime and our happiness will grow. — William Wordsworth', 'print_section': 'BR', 'print_page': '39', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/08/25/books/review/0825-bks-Gilman/0825-bks-Gilman-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 849, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2013/08/25/books/review/0825-bks-Gilman/0825-bks-Gilman-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 849}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/08/25/books/review/0825-bks-Gilman/0825-bks-Gilman-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 1024, 'width': 724, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/08/25/books/review/0825-bks-Gilman/0825-bks-Gilman-supe