value': 'City Council (NYC)', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Bloomberg, Michael R', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Police Brutality, Misconduct and Shootings', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Kelly, Raymond W', 'rank': 7, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2013-08-22T20:58:49+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Metro', 'section_name': 'New York', 'byline': {'original': 'By J. David Goodman', 'person': [{'firstname': 'J.', 'middlename': 'David', 'lastname': 'Goodman', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/98c0cc78-fcca-57e8-9d9a-713e4185c484', 'word_count': 1230, 'uri': 'nyt://article/98c0cc78-fcca-57e8-9d9a-713e4185c484'}, {'abstract': 'Put your predictive skills to the test each week of the baseball season.', 'web_url': 'https://bats.blogs.nytimes.com/2013/08/22/triple-play-make-your-picks-for-week-22/', 'snippet': 'Put your predictive skills to the test each week of the baseball season.', 'lead_paragraph': 'The Bats blog again tests our readers’ predictive skills with three questions about the coming week. Feel free to lean on research, tea leaves, your gut instinct or maybe just dumb luck.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Triple Play: Make Your Picks for Week 22', 'kicker': 'Bats', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Baseball', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2013-08-22T21:00:30+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Sports', 'subsection_name': 'Baseball', 'byline': {'original': 'By The New York Times', 'person': [], 'organization': 'The New York Times'}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/23131e4b-362d-56f7-a91b-8606cc7192d5', 'word_count': 75, 'uri': 'nyt://article/23131e4b-362d-56f7-a91b-8606cc7192d5'}, {'abstract': '“Thérèse,” set in 1920s France, revolves around an upper-class woman for whom happiness is elusive.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2013/08/23/movies/in-therese-a-woman-struggles-in-1920s-france.html', 'snippet': '“Thérèse,” set in 1920s France, revolves around an upper-class woman for whom happiness is elusive.', 'lead_paragraph': 'The rich are different from you and me, as F. Scott Fitzgerald wrote, sometimes nowhere more so than in French movies. In the United States, a Viking stove in the kitchen and a BMW in the driveway may be mere background noise in a sleek thriller; in France, such emblems of class privilege can be grounds for a date with the Widow (a k a the guillotine), metaphorically speaking. In “Thérèse,” a 1920s woman is driven to commit a terrible crime, for all sorts of nominal reasons, though, in truth, her paramount motive is the accident of her birth: she’s bourgeois.', 'print_section': 'C', 'print_page': '8', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/08/23/arts/23THERESE_SPAN/0823THERESE-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 400, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2013/08/23/arts/23THERESE_SPAN/0823THERESE-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 400}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/08/23/arts/23THERESE_SPAN/0823THERESE-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 682, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/08/23/arts/23THERESE_SPAN/0823THERESE-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 1365, 'width': 2048, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/08/23/arts/23THERESE_SPAN/0823THERESE-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2013/08/23/arts/23THERESE_SPAN/0823THERESE-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/08/23/arts/23THERESE_SPAN/0823THERESE-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Privileged, but Wired Not to Find Comfort', 'kicker': 'Movie Review', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Privileged, But Wired Not to Find Comfort', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Movies', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Miller, Claude', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'creative_works', 'value': 'Therese (Movie)', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Tautou, Audrey', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2013-08-22T21:01:37+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Weekend', 'section_name': 'Movies', 'byline': {'original': 'By Manohla Dargis', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Manohla', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Dargis', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Review', '_id': 'nyt://article/1681548c-2cc8-536a-8569-05200b2513f4', 'word_count': 599, 'uri': 'nyt://article/1681548c-2cc8-536a-8569-05200b2513f4'}, {'abstract': 'His newest film, “The World’s End,” is centered on an epic pub crawl, complete with an apocalyptic climax, and hits theaters tomorrow.', 'web_url': 'https://tmagazine.blogs.nytimes.com/2013/08/22/q-a-edgar-wright-on-the-autobiographical-inspirations-behind-his-boozy-film/', 'snippet': 'His newest film, “The World’s End,” is centered on an epic pub crawl, complete with an apocalyptic climax, and hits theaters tomorrow.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Edgar Wright’s latest gleefully genre-fusing meditation on English small-town life, “The World’s End,” in theaters tomorrow, charts an epic pub crawl led by the character of Gary King (played by Wright’s co-writer Simon Pegg), a roguish, charismatic drunk. King reunites a disbanded group of friends from his teen years to finally complete “The Golden Mile,” in which a stop is made at all 12 pubs in their hometown, a task they had conspicuously failed at 20 years before. As the group makes its way toward an apocalyptic climax, old wounds are reopened, lost loves are reunited, an alien invasion is uncovered, and many, many pints are consumed. Here, the writer and director talks about real-life inspiration for the film.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Q. & A. | Edgar Wright on the Autobiographical Inspirations Behind His Boozy Film', 'kicker': 'T Magazine', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Wright, Edgar', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'creative_works', 'value': "The World's End (Movie)", 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2013-08-22T21:05:22+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'T Magazine', 'byline': {'original': 'By Jason Rowan', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Jason', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Rowan', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/d84043d0-b597-5322-a818-6ab709d72490', 'word_count': 917, 'uri': 'nyt://article/d84043d0-b597-5322-a818-6ab709d72490'}, {'abstract': 'In “World’s End,” five British men reprise a pub crawl they had tried but failed to finish more than 20 years earlier as teenagers.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2013/08/23/movies/worlds-end-continues-shaun-of-the-dead-and-hot-fuzz.html', 'snippet': 'In “World’s End,” five British men reprise a pub crawl they had tried but failed to finish more than 20 years earlier as teenagers.', 'lead_paragraph': 'On a summer evening in 1990, five British teenagers attempted a heroic pub crawl in the town of Newton Haven, a feat of herculean imbibing known as the Golden Mile. Along the way, there were vomiting, fighting and drunken sex, but amid all that fun, the group failed to make it through the requisite 12 watering holes, the last of which was called the World’s End. Their second try, more than 20 years later, is the subject of Edgar Wright’s new movie. ', 'print_section': 'C', 'print_page': '8', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/08/23/arts/23WORLDS_SPAN/0823WORLDS-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 400, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2013/08/23/arts/23WORLDS_SPAN/0823WORLDS-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 400}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/08/23/arts/23WORLDS_SPAN/0823WORLDS-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 683, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/08/23/arts/23WORLDS_SPAN/0823WORLDS-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 1366, 'width': 2048, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/08/23/arts/23WORLDS_SPAN/0823WORLDS-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2013/08/23/arts/23WORLDS_SPAN/0823WORLDS-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/08/23/arts/23WORLDS_SPAN/0823WORLDS-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Last Call for Friends to Grow Up', 'kicker': 'Movie Review', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Last Call For Friends To Grow Up', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Movies', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Wright, Edgar', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Pegg, Simon', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Frost, Nick', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'creative_works', 'value': "The World's End (Movie)", 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2013-08-22T21:06:45+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Weekend', 'section_name': 'Movies', 'byline': {'original': 'By A.O. Scott', 'person': [{'firstname': 'A.', 'middlename': 'O.', 'lastname': 'Scott', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Review', '_id': 'nyt://article/cf82cb79-9974-52ef-82fc-3605047460a2', 'word_count': 720, 'uri': 'nyt://article/cf82cb79-9974-52ef-82fc-3605047460a2'}, {'abstract': 'The drummer Jimmy Cobb, 84, the last remaining musician to have played on “Kind of Blue,” led a quartet on Wednesday at Village Vanguard.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2013/08/23/arts/music/jimmy-cobb-expands-his-act-at-village-vanguard.html', 'snippet': 'The drummer Jimmy Cobb, 84, the last remaining musician to have played on “Kind of Blue,” led a quartet on Wednesday at Village Vanguard.', 'lead_paragraph': 'The urge toward retrospection runs strong among jazz musicians of a certain age, and to resist it requires stern commitment. But there’s also a way to be stubborn about honoring the past and its pull, making no disclaimers and leaving nothing to conjec