': 'Buettner', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/5b2c84c2-fe1a-5e47-a129-77954ae7da73', 'word_count': 728, 'uri': 'nyt://article/5b2c84c2-fe1a-5e47-a129-77954ae7da73'}, {'abstract': 'Environment trumped event as Julianna Barwick performed music from her third album, “Nepenthe,” at the Judson Memorial Church on Tuesday.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2013/08/23/arts/music/environment-reigns-supreme-in-julianna-barwicks-nepenthe.html', 'snippet': 'Environment trumped event as Julianna Barwick performed music from her third album, “Nepenthe,” at the Judson Memorial Church on Tuesday.', 'lead_paragraph': 'There is an elusive quality to the natural light in Iceland, where long, white days of hazy luminosity don’t start and end so much as they seem to surreptitiously appear and, eventually, disappear. A similarly evanescent quality can be found in the music of Julianna Barwick, a singer and composer whose third album, “Nepenthe,” was issued by the Dead Oceans label on Tuesday. Ms. Barwick’s songs — chiefly wordless vocals, electronically looped — materialize out of ether, saturate a sonorous atmosphere, then dissolve. Environment trumps event.', 'print_section': 'C', 'print_page': '3', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/08/23/arts/23barwick-1/23barwick-1-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 393, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2013/08/23/arts/23barwick-1/23barwick-1-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 393}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/08/23/arts/23barwick-1/23barwick-1-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 672, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subty