ry “The United States of Football” follows retired players dealing with dementia and depression after a career of hard knocks.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2013/08/23/movies/the-united-states-of-football-looks-at-concussions.html', 'snippet': 'The documentary “The United States of Football” follows retired players dealing with dementia and depression after a career of hard knocks.', 'lead_paragraph': '“Would I allow my son to play football?” is the question that the sports journalist Sean Pamphilon positions at the center of “The United States of Football,” his documentary on the dangers of head injuries in that sport. He follows the stories of several retired N.F.L. players, living and dead, who had the degenerative brain disease chronic traumatic encephalopathy, which has been linked to repetitive blows to the head. Extreme dementia and depression set in before the disease slowly destroys the body. “What do you do with a 300-pound, 45-year-old person who’s out of control and is only going to get worse?” asks Lisa McHale, the widow of the Tampa Bay lineman Tom McHale.', 'print_section': 'C', 'print_page': '10', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/08/23/arts/23football-1/23football-1-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 400, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2013/08/23/arts/23football-1/23football-1-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 400}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/08/23/arts/23football-1/23football-1-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 683, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/08/23/arts/23football-1/23football-1-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 1367, 'width': 2048, 'subType': 's