, 'person': [], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'List', '_id': 'nyt://article/56b7af79-7d65-556b-8faa-bd5812b32c0d', 'word_count': 177, 'uri': 'nyt://article/56b7af79-7d65-556b-8faa-bd5812b32c0d'}, {'abstract': 'A lawsuit aims to end the charade that gives tax exemptions to partisan political groups posing as social welfare organizations.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2013/08/23/opinion/suing-the-irs-back-to-basics.html', 'snippet': 'A lawsuit aims to end the charade that gives tax exemptions to partisan political groups posing as social welfare organizations.', 'lead_paragraph': 'The ease with which big-money donors are able to influence the nation’s politics is nowhere better illustrated than by the charade that gives tax exemptions to obviously partisan political groups posing as “social welfare” organizations. Representative Chris Van Hollen, a Maryland Democrat, thus merits thanks for his decision to sue the Internal Revenue Service to force it to end its controversial muddying of tax law that has allowed these groups to flourish. ', 'print_section': 'A', 'print_page': '26', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Suing the I.R.S. Back to Basics', 'kicker': 'Editorial', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Suing the I.R.S. Back to Basics', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Nonprofit Organizations', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Internal Revenue Service Political Profiling', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Democratic Party', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Tax Credits, Deductions and Exemptions', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'United States Politics and Government', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Editorials', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Internal Revenue Service', 'rank': 7, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Campaign Finance', 'rank': 8, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2013-08-23T01:33:15+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Editorial', 'section_name': 'Opinion', 'byline': {'original': 'By The Editorial Board', 'person': [], 'organization': 'The Editorial Board'}, 'type_of_material': 'Editorial', '_id': 'nyt://article/e0df55b3-4b13-56c2-95f2-23d96d6fa330', 'word_count': 355, 'uri': 'nyt://article/e0df55b3-4b13-56c2-95f2-23d96d6fa330'}, {'abstract': 'A chemical attack, if proved, will require an aggressive response from the West.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2013/08/23/opinion/the-corpses-in-syria.html', 'snippet': 'A chemical attack, if proved, will require an aggressive response from the West.', 'lead_paragraph': 'The lifeless bodies depicted in photos and videos from Syria over the past two days suggest another chemical attack, quite possibly the deadliest single attack in more than two years of civil war. If the killings of an estimated 500 to 1,000 men, women and children outside of Damascus prove to be the work of President Bashar al-Assad’s cutthroat regime, as many suspect, the United States and other major powers will almost certainly have to respond much more aggressively than they have so far. ', 'print_section': 'A', 'print_page': '26', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'The Corpses in Syria', 'kicker': 'Editorial', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'The Corpses in Syria', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Biological and Chemical Warfare', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Free Syrian Army', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Assad, Bashar al-', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Syria', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Middle East and North Africa Unrest (2010- )', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Obama, Barack', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'United States International Relations', 'rank': 7, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Editorials', 'rank': 8, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2013-08-23T01:36:03+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Editorial', 'section_name': 'Opinion', 'byline': {'original': 'By The Editorial Board', 'person': [], 'organization': 'The Editorial Board'}, 'type_of_material': 'Editorial', '_id': 'nyt://article/fe3ec1da-e240-5e4e-ac14-33992174caec', 'word_count': 539, 'uri': 'nyt://article/fe3ec1da-e240-5e4e-ac14-33992174caec'}, {'abstract': 'President Obama’s proposal for a rating system, if properly designed, could make the cost of college more affordable.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2013/08/23/opinion/a-federal-prod-to-lower-college-costs.html', 'snippet': 'President Obama’s proposal for a rating system, if properly designed, could make the cost of college more affordable.', 'lead_paragraph': 'President Obama has been accused of promoting small-ball ideas in his second term, but the proposal he unveiled on Thursday is a big one: using sharp federal pressure to make college more affordable, potentially opening the gates of higher education to more families scared off by rising tuitions. While there are questions to be answered about his plan, his approach — tying federal student aid to the value of individual colleges — is a bold and important way to leverage the government’s power and get Washington off the sidelines.', 'print_section': 'A', 'print_page': '26', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'A Federal Prod to Lower College Costs', 'kicker': 'Editorial', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'A Federal Prod to Lower College Costs', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Obama, Barack', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Financial Aid (Education)', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Colleges and Universities', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Tuition', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Editorials', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2013-08-23T01:36:10+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Editorial', 'section_name': 'Opinion', 'byline': {'original': 'By The Editorial Board', 'person': [], 'organization': 'The Editorial Board'}, 'type_of_material': 'Editorial', '_id': 'nyt://article/5bfec81c-a5be-515a-a9c4-d238c6f3ef23', 'word_count': 652, 'uri': 'nyt://article/5bfec81c-a5be-515a-a9c4-d238c6f3ef23'}, {'abstract': 'Citing a lack of editorial control, ESPN ended its collaboration with the public affairs series “Frontline” on a two-part documentary set to run in October.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2013/08/23/sports/football/espn-exits-film-project-on-concussions.html', 'snippet': 'Citing a lack of editorial control, ESPN ended its collaboration with the public affairs series “Frontline” on a two-part documentary set to run in October.', 'lead_paragraph': 'ESPN on Thursday ended its official association with “Frontline,” the public television public affairs series, on a two-part documentary about concussions in the N.F.L. that is scheduled to be televised in October. After 15 months on the venture, ESPN chose to strip its name, logo and credit from the films, “League of Denial: The NFL’s Concussion Crisis.”', 'print_section': 'B', 'print_page': '13', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/08/23/sports/Y-ESPN/Y-ESPN-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 833, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2013/08/23/sports/Y-ESPN/Y-ESPN-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 833}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/08/23/sports/Y-ESP