7+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'BookReview', 'section_name': 'Books', 'subsection_name': 'Book Review', 'byline': {'original': '', 'person': [], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Letter', '_id': 'nyt://article/80780eb3-bcb5-5aac-9734-36b6b7f0a9dd', 'word_count': 278, 'uri': 'nyt://article/80780eb3-bcb5-5aac-9734-36b6b7f0a9dd'}, {'abstract': 'An 18-year-old plans a murder-suicide in Matthew Quick’s young adult novel.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2013/08/25/books/review/forgive-me-leonard-peacock-by-matthew-quick.html', 'snippet': 'An 18-year-old plans a murder-suicide in Matthew Quick’s young adult novel.', 'lead_paragraph': 'On the morning Leonard Peacock, the high school narrator of Matthew Quick’s young adult novel, prepares a murder-suicide, he packs the Nazi pistol his grandfather brought back from the war and starts his school day with a plan. It is his 18th birthday. He is targeting his onetime best friend. Creeping through the morning with this secret knowledge, he attempts to reconcile the lives that have gone wrong around him, and to savor the freedom that comes from his imminent demise. ', 'print_section': 'BR', 'print_page': '13', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Trouble in Mind', 'kicker': "Children's Books", 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Trouble in Mind', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Heller, Nathan', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Quick, Matthew', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Books and Literature', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2013-08-23T15:19:18+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'BookReview', 'section_name': 'Books', 'subsection_name': 'Book Review', 'byline': {'original': 'By Nathan Heller', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Nathan', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Heller', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Review', '_id': 'nyt://article/678c9187-49df-5ed9-bdff-5b076eaa7632', 'word_count': 877, 'uri': 'nyt://article/678c9187-49df-5ed9-bdff-5b076eaa7632'}, {'abstract': 'Two books consider the lasting legacy of Leo Strauss.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2013/08/25/books/review/two-books-about-the-legacy-of-leo-strauss.html', 'snippet': 'Two books consider the lasting legacy of Leo Strauss.', 'lead_paragraph': 'When Leo Strauss died in 1973 he was virtually unknown outside the tiny academic circle that he inhabited. Forty years later his name and influence are known almost everywhere. His books and articles have been translated into nearly every European language and several Asian ones. Web sites — both pro and con — are devoted to him. International conferences debate his thought and legacy.', 'print_section': 'BR', 'print_page': '29', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/08/25/books/review/25SMITH/25SMITH-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 580, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2013/08/25/books/review/25SMITH/25SMITH-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 580}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/08/25/books/review/25SMITH/25SMITH-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 902, 'width': 933, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/08/25/books/review/25SMITH/25SMITH-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 902, 'width': 933, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/08/25/books/review/25SMITH/25SMITH-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2013/08/25/books/review/25SMITH/25SMITH-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/08/25/books/review/25SMITH/25SMITH-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Hidden Truths', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Hidden Truths', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Books and Literature', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Strauss, Leo', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2013-08-23T15:19:22+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'BookReview', 'section_name': 'Books', 'subsection_name': 'Book Review', 'byline': {'original': 'By Steven B. Smith', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Steven', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': '', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Review', '_id': 'nyt://article/0bbe37d5-6b8a-5a08-afae-ec2edb1dd3a9', 'word_count': 1152, 'uri': 'nyt://article/0bbe37d5-6b8a-5a08-afae-ec2edb1dd3a9'}, {'abstract': 'Two young women become entangled in cold war intrigue.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2013/08/25/books/review/dancer-daughter-traitor-spy-and-boy-on-the-bridge.html', 'snippet': 'Two young women become entangled in cold war intrigue.', 'lead_paragraph': 'When I lived in Moscow in my 20s, I’d often pass nervous young men standing in the metro station, holding flowers and waiting for their dates to show up. Meeting in the metro was a Russian tradition, and like Russia itself it seemed exotic, romantic and rife with drama. I arrived in tears to several of my own rendezvous because of confusion over the Cyrillic-only metro signs, and I missed others entirely. ', 'print_section': 'BR', 'print_page': '20', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Russia, With Love', 'kicker': "Children's Books", 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Russia, With Love', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Books and Literature', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Standiford, Natalie', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Kiem, Elizabeth', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2013-08-23T15:19:22+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'BookReview', 'section_name': 'Books', 'subsection_name': 'Book Review', 'byline': {'original': 'By Valerie Stivers', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Valerie', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Stivers', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Review', '_id': 'nyt://article/0c31b1dd-7066-5198-a948-6612ab173a93', 'word_count': 791, 'uri': 'nyt://article/0c31b1dd-7066-5198-a948-6612ab173a93'}, {'abstract': 'Two novels chart the difficult terrain known as high school.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2013/08/25/books/review/confessions-of-a-hater-and-snap-decision-maybe-tonight.html', 'snippet': 'Two novels chart the difficult terrain known as high school.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Like the lead characters in two new books about navigating the dynamics of high school society — Caprice Crane’s “Confessions of a Hater” and Bridie Clark’s “Snap Decision: Maybe Tonight?” — I was a bit of a fish out of water as a teenager. But there’s something about those uncomfortable years that draws me, like many adults, back to them — at least as a reader. The perspective of these novels is subtle enough to be engrossing for both teenagers and adults for whom high school’s intense friendships and antipathies still resonate. And how could they not? ', 'print_section': 'BR', 'print_page': '19', 'source': 'T