7-0382-5b4b-83a8-6d407b5cd6e9'}, {'abstract': 'In two middle-grade novels, social position is a powerful weapon.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2013/08/25/books/review/runt-and-trash-can-days.html', 'snippet': 'In two middle-grade novels, social position is a powerful weapon.', 'lead_paragraph': 'The movie “Mean Girls” is nearly a decade old, but the “burn book” at the center of Tina Fey’s screenplay remains an evergreen plot device. Mostly written by the popular girls, these burn books are public logs of carefully calculated insults lobbed at social rivals and undesirables. Like everything else in the past 10 years, the burn book has moved online, and Facebook and Tumblr are littered with hateful messages written by aggressive tweens. ', 'print_section': 'BR', 'print_page': '18', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Alpha Dogs, Underdogs', 'kicker': "Children's Books", 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Alpha Dogs, Underdogs', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Books and Literature', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Steinkellner, Teddy', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Baskin, Nora Raleigh', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2013-08-23T15:20:05+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'BookReview', 'section_name': 'Books', 'subsection_name': 'Book Review', 'byline': {'original': 'By Jessica Grose', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Jessica', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Grose', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Review', '_id': 'nyt://article/18fa5694-05b0-5588-b2f4-8eca92e1cc6d', 'word_count': 731, 'uri': 'nyt://article/18fa5694-05b0-5588-b2f4-8eca92e1cc6d'}, {'abstract': 'Meryl Streep narrates the audiobook of Colm Toibin’s “Testament of Mary.”', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2013/08/25/books/review/meryl-as-mary.html', 'snippet': 'Meryl Streep narrates the audiobook of Colm Toibin’s “Testament of Mary.”', 'lead_paragraph': '“The Testament of Mary,” Colm Toibin’s novel and play about the mother of Jesus, has been blessed in its interpreters. When Fiona Shaw played the role on Broadway, Toibin praised her “commitment to theater at its most pure and risky and dangerous.” Now Meryl Streep has narrated the audiobook, due out Sept. 10. No word yet on whether Toibin’s work will also make it to the big screen or whether Shaw and Streep will compete for the part.', 'print_section': 'BR', 'print_page': '4', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/08/25/books/review/0825-bks-openbook/0825-bks-openbook-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 213, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2013/08/25/books/review/0825-bks-openbook/0825-bks-openbook-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 213}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/08/25/books/review/0825-bks-openbook/0825-bks-openbook-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 363, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/08/25/books/review/0825-bks-openbook/0825-bks-openbook-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 372, 'width': 1048, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/08/25/books/review/0825-bks-openbook/0825-bks-openbook-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2013/08/25/books/review/0825-bks-openbook/0825-bks-openbook-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/08/25/books/review/0825-bks-openbook/0825-bks-openbook-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Meryl as Mary', 'kicker': 'Open Book', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Meryl as Mary', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Books and Literature', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2013-08-23T15:20:08+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'BookReview', 'section_name': 'Books', 'subsection_name': 'Book Review', 'byline': {'original': 'By John Williams', 'person': [{'firstname': 'John', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Williams', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/96ca5941-e389-5f92-b02a-f152a3774a78', 'word_count': 341, 'uri': 'nyt://article/96ca5941-e389-5f92-b02a-f152a3774a78'}, {'abstract': 'Six middle schoolers participate in a secret training program to fight evil.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2013/08/25/books/review/sidekicked-by-john-david-anderson.html', 'snippet': 'Six middle schoolers participate in a secret training program to fight evil.', 'lead_paragraph': 'I wanted to hate this book. After all, this was a book for children, and I don’t do children’s books. I was totally lost by Page 2 of the preface — wondering what a Zilchbot was (12 dozen of them were attacking Captain Marvelous and his friends) and why I should care about Kid Caliber or the Diamond Dame (heroes trapped in the Nulzone). As for Captain Marvelous, he seemed like a vanilla remake — a rip-off, really — of Superman, from the big red “M” on his chest to his ruby red cape and captaincy of the Legion of Justice. I am an authority on the Man of Steel, having written a full-fledged biography of him. “I know Superman, Superman is a friend of mine,” I was tempted to write to the author, John David Anderson, “and Mr. Anderson, Captain Marvelous is no Superman!”', 'print_section': 'BR', 'print_page': '11', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/08/25/books/review/25tye/25tye-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 363, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2013/08/25/books/review/25tye/25tye-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 363}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/08/25/books/review/25tye/25tye-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 620, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/08/25/books/review/25tye/25tye-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 1240, 'width': 2048, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/08/25/books/review/25tye/25tye-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2013/08/25/books/review/25tye/25tye-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/08/25/books/review/25tye/25tye-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Gifted and Talented', 'kicker': "Children's Books", 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Gifted and Talented', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Books and Literature', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Anderson, John David (1975- )', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2013-08-23T15:20:09+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'BookReview', 'section_name': 'Books', 'subsection_name': 'Book Review', 'byline': {'original': 'By Larry Tye', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Larry', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Tye', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Review', '_id': 'nyt://article/cd47eca5-d4d6-5e12-bcfd-2dd499aab7cb', 'word_count': 673, 'uri': 'nyt://article/cd47eca5-d4d6-5e12-bcfd-2dd499aab7cb'}, {'abstract': 'Three picture books capture the objects and incidents that form a child’s heritage.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2013/08/25/books/review/this-is-the-rope-by-jacqueline-woodson-and-more.html', 'snippet': 'Three picture books capture the objects and incidents that form a child’s heritage.', 'lead_paragraph': 'What parent wasn’t thrilled to hear about the Emory University study suggesting that children who are told stories about their families’ pasts may develop a greater ability to cope with the vicissitudes of life? But most children’s books that convey a sense of family history and tradition — “All-of-a-Kind Family,” “Little House in the Big Woods” — are written with the older child in mind. Younger children are more likely to learn the day-to-day habits of rabbits, monkeys and bears. So it’s refreshing to find three new picture books that take as their subject the stories of human families, illuminating the ways certain rituals — along with sayings, jokes, pastimes and world views — get passed down through the generations.', 'print_section': 'BR', 'print_page': '14', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/08/25/books/review/0825-bks-Steiker/0825-bks-Steiker-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 567, 'width': 600, 