{'original': 'By Valerie Steiker', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Valerie', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Steiker', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Review', '_id': 'nyt://article/4a6406af-b310-58e8-8596-1a8b11e59e56', 'word_count': 991, 'uri': 'nyt://article/4a6406af-b310-58e8-8596-1a8b11e59e56'}, {'abstract': 'Fleeing her troubled homeland, a Liberian woman seeks refuge on an Aegean island.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2013/08/25/books/review/a-marker-to-measure-drift-by-alexander-maksik.html', 'snippet': 'Fleeing her troubled homeland, a Liberian woman seeks refuge on an Aegean island.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Can the literary novel ever really get its arms around the problem of human evil? It keeps trying — a difficult assignment for the poor beast. In any case, an undaunted Alexander Maksik has brought his skills to this very problem. His second novel, “A Marker to Measure Drift,” recounts a season of homeless exile in the life of a 24-year-old Liberian woman fleeing an episode of gruesome violence incidental to the overthrow of the tyrant Charles Ghankay Taylor, in 2003. Maksik has produced a bold book, and an instructive one.', 'print_section': 'BR', 'print_page': '24', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/08/25/books/review/0825-bks-Rush/0825-bks-Rush-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 728, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2013/08/25/books/review/0825-bks-Rush/0825-bks-Rush-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 728}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/08/25/books/review/0825-bks-Rush/0825-bks-Rush-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 1024, 'width': 845, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subt