 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}, {'firstname': 'Justin', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Sablich', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 2}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/88938200-32de-5fa7-b7cd-eda98f76c337', 'word_count': 1210, 'uri': 'nyt://article/88938200-32de-5fa7-b7cd-eda98f76c337'}, {'abstract': 'The final batch of Richard Nixon’s secret and self-incriminating White House tape recordings have been released.', 'web_url': 'https://takingnote.blogs.nytimes.com/2013/08/23/nixons-political-wisdom-caught-on-tape/', 'snippet': 'The final batch of Richard Nixon’s secret and self-incriminating White House tape recordings have been released.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Our long audio nightmare is over. The final batch of Richard Nixon’s secret and self-incriminating White House tape recordings have been released. As in the 3,700 hours previously made public across the past 17 years, Nixon as president is there in all his cunning and self-pity —“Hamlet without the Prince,” in the description of Stanley Kutler, the historian who fought mightily to block Nixon and his loyalists from burying the tapes forever. They can be tedious listening, and it’s hard to judge what younger generations addicted to on-screen bursts of brevity will make of the 37th president’s schemes and maunderings amid the scandal known as Watergate. But they still offer an unparalleled voyeuristic fix, which had citizens lining up for hours to hear the initial batches played endlessly on 32 audio machines in Washington.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Nixon’s Political Wisdom, Caught on Tape', 'kicker': 'Taking Note', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Watergate Affair', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Nixon, Richard Milhous', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2013-08-23T17:30:38+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Opinion', 'byline': {'original': 'By Francis X. Clines', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Francis', 'middlename': 'X.', 'lastname': 'Clines', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/303ae301-b235-5854-a7e6-61103489c007', 'word_count': 465, 'uri': 'nyt://article/303ae301-b235-5854-a7e6-61103489c007'}, {'abstract': 'The decline in overall applications from 2008-12 was 17 percent, but among graduates of 20 top universities it was 28 percent.', 'web_url': 'https://economix.blogs.nytimes.com/2013/08/23/law-school-applicants-decline-especially-among-grads-of-elite-colleges/', 'snippet': 'The decline in overall applications from 2008-12 was 17 percent, but among graduates of 20 top universities it was 28 percent.', 'lead_paragraph': 'We’ve written before about the declining interest in law school, as evidenced by the number of people taking the Law School Admission Test and the number applying to law schools. Over at the Associate’s Mind blog, Keith Lee notes that the number of applicants is down across the board, but the drop-off is particularly sharp among people who went to elite schools for their bachelor’s degrees.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/08/23/business/23economix-law-school/23economix-law-school-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 400, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2013/08/23/business/23economix-law-school/23economix-law-school-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 400}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/08/23/business/23economix-law-school/23economix-law-school-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 683, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/08/23/business/23economix-law-school/23economix-law-school-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 1365, 'width': 2048, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/08/23/business/23economix-law-school/23economix-law-school-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2013/08/23/business/23economix-law-school/23economix-law-school-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/08/23/business/23economix-law-school/23economix-law-school-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Law School Applications Decline, Especially From Graduates of Elite Colleges', 'kicker': 'Economix', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Colleges and Universities', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Education', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Law Schools', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2013-08-23T17:30:47+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Business Day', 'subsection_name': 'Economy', 'byline': {'original': 'By Catherine Rampell', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Catherine', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Rampell', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/9d1d5ee4-f1e4-51e2-9bec-d2eab073a5fb', 'word_count': 460, 'uri': 'nyt://article/9d1d5ee4-f1e4-51e2-9bec-d2eab073a5fb'}, {'abstract': 'Ben Ratliff and Nate Chinen discuss the Grammy-winning jazz vibraphonist, who has a new autobiography and a new album.', 'web_url': 'https://artsbeat.blogs.nytimes.com/2013/08/23/popcast-gary-burton-resurgent-jazz-outsider/', 'snippet': 'Ben Ratliff and Nate Chinen discuss the Grammy-winning jazz vibraphonist, who has a new autobiography and a new album.', 'lead_paragraph': 'If you’re only a casual listener to jazz, and not a vibraphonist, Gary Burton may not be a big name to you. But he’s probably had more to do with the development of jazz over the last 50 years than you think as a player (he developed the widely used four-mallet Burton Grip for the vibraphone); a mentor (to Pat Metheny, for starters); a conceptual inspiration (to Bill Frisell, among others, through the way he incorporated country and rock into his music during the late ’60s); and a longtime teacher and administrator at Berklee College of Music.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/08/25/arts/25BURTON2/jpBurton-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 345, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2013/08/25/arts/25BURTON2/jpBurton-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 345}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/08/25/arts/25BURTON2/jpBurton-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 588, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/08/25/arts/25BURTON2/jpBurton-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 1176, 'width': 2048, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/08/25/arts/25BURTON2/jpBurton-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2013/08/25/arts/25BURTON2/jpBurton-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/08/25/arts/25BURTON2/jpBurton-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Popcast: Gary Burton, Resurgent Jazz Outsider', 'kicker': 'ArtsBeat', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [], 'pub_date': '2013-08-23T17:39:25+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Arts', 'byline': {'original': 'By The New York Times', 'person': [], 'organization': 'The New York Times'}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/31815bce-9bb3-53df-8e78-035563b1e030', 'word_count': 200, 'uri': 'nyt://article/31815bce-9bb3-53df-8e78-035563b1e030'}, {'abstract': 'Great tennis writing from the magazine’s archive.', 'web_url': 'https://6thfloor.blogs.nytimes.com/2013/08/23/required-u-s-open-reading-old-and-new/', 'snippet': 'Great tennis writing from the magazine’s archive.', 'lead_paragraph': 'There is much to dread about late August: the heat, the humidity, those people who leave work at like 1 every Friday while you work normal hours. But there is at least one thing to look forward to: the magazine’s annual U.S. Open issue. You probably remember previous Open coverage from the magazine. There was John Jeremiah Sullivan’s article on Venus and Serena Williams, Andy Samberg’s re-enactments of iconic men’s championships and Dewey Nicks’s video gallery of women’s tennis stars. And then there’s the older stuff. Who could forget Harry Gordon on Arthur Ashe; Hal Higdon on Billie Jean King; or Allison Danzig on Helen Wills? Wait, did you forget them? Did you never know about them? Maybe that’s because those pieces, dating to 1928, do not exist on the Internet.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/08/18/magazine/18archives-thumb/18archives-thumb-jumbo.png', 'height': 255, 'width': 300, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/08/18/magazine/18archives-thumb/18archives-thumb-superJumbo.png', 'height': 255, 'width': 300, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/08/18/magazine/18archives-thumb/18archives-thumb-thumbStandard.png', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2013/08/18/magazine/18archives-thumb/18archives-thumb-thumbStandard.png', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/08/18/magazine/18archives-thumb/18archives-thumb-thumbLarge.png', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Required U.S. Open Reading, Old and New', 'kicker': 'The 6th Floor', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Tennis', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2013-08-23T17:43:07+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Magazine', 'byline': {'original': 'By Samantha Henig', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Samantha', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Henig', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': No