'byline': {'original': 'By Nate Chinen', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Nate', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Chinen', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/b739f20d-9c2a-5b93-99a0-a78cc1ccf44d', 'word_count': 615, 'uri': 'nyt://article/b739f20d-9c2a-5b93-99a0-a78cc1ccf44d'}, {'abstract': 'The composer and lyricist William Finn, rather than seeking rest, looks for satisfaction in work and play.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2013/08/25/nyregion/making-the-rounds-at-boxing-and-brunch.html', 'snippet': 'The composer and lyricist William Finn, rather than seeking rest, looks for satisfaction in work and play.', 'lead_paragraph': 'The composer and lyricist William Finn lives on the Upper West Side of Manhattan and spends his summers at “Wasabi Gables.” That’s what he and his longtime partner, Arthur Salvadore, call their corner Victorian house in Pittsfield, Mass., for its exterior hue. Mr. Finn, 61, a two-time Tony Award winner, has been the artistic director since 2006 of the Musical Theater Lab at the Barrington Stage Company, up the block. There he was part of the team that developed and premiered “The 25th Annual Putnam County Spelling Bee” before its Off Broadway and Broadway success. After two years of revision, “Little Miss Sunshine,” by Mr. Finn and the librettist James Lapine, will open off Broadway this fall at Second Stage. On the last summer Sundays, Mr. Finn will socialize with Barrington Stage’s director, Julianne Boyd, and go to productions, when not serving as M.C. for “Mr. Finn’s Cabaret” at the theater. Rather than seeking rest, he looks for the joy in work and play.', 'print_section': 'MB', 'print_page': '2', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/08/25/nyregion/25ROUTINE2_SPAN/25ROUTINE2-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 