e}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/c717a3cb-ef7b-5dd8-9d6f-3e2a17e9635a', 'word_count': 1222, 'uri': 'nyt://article/c717a3cb-ef7b-5dd8-9d6f-3e2a17e9635a'}, {'abstract': 'Governors Island, whose cannons once protected New York Harbor, now has French carousels and a growing number of weekend visitors.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2013/08/25/nyregion/strolling-and-playing-but-no-more-saluting.html', 'snippet': 'Governors Island, whose cannons once protected New York Harbor, now has French carousels and a growing number of weekend visitors.', 'lead_paragraph': 'On a map, New York’s biggest summer playground looks just like an ice cream cone, shaped for the city’s enjoyment. Governors Island was an Army base until 1966, then used by the Coast Guard through the mid-1990s. It opened to the public in 2004, with guests first arriving in small numbers. But times change: on a recent weekend, 12,000 visitors took a free ferry from Brooklyn Bridge Park or the Beaux-Arts Battery Maritime Building in Lower Manhattan to enjoy the 172-acre island’s lawns, historic buildings, food carts and arts programs — all open Saturdays, Sundays and holiday Mondays through Sept. 29. ', 'print_section': 'MB', 'print_page': '5', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/08/25/nyregion/25STOPSUB2/25subSTOP2-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 400, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2013/08/25/nyregion/25STOPSUB2/25subSTOP2-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 400}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/08/25/nyregion/25STOPSUB2/25subSTOP2-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 683, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'imag