t', 'value': 'Hydraulic Fracturing', 'rank': 9, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Michaels, David (1954- )', 'rank': 10, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2013-08-24T00:17:21+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Business', 'section_name': 'Business Day', 'byline': {'original': 'By Steven Greenhouse', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Steven', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Greenhouse', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/cc8a17ae-20b9-5f25-a3a5-f71d8b375885', 'word_count': 887, 'uri': 'nyt://article/cc8a17ae-20b9-5f25-a3a5-f71d8b375885'}, {'abstract': 'The conviction that removing obstreperous foreign leaders facilitates Washington’s ability to steer events remains the most persistent — and dangerous — of illusions.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2013/08/24/opinion/the-ugly-american-telegram.html', 'snippet': 'The conviction that removing obstreperous foreign leaders facilitates Washington’s ability to steer events remains the most persistent — and dangerous — of illusions.', 'lead_paragraph': 'BOSTON — ON Aug. 24, 1963, the American ambassador to South Vietnam, Henry Cabot Lodge, received a top-secret message with the bureaucratically anodyne title Deptel 243. But the content of the message was anything but routine. Hastily drafted and cleared over the course of a single day, with most of official Washington on vacation, Deptel 243, also known as the Hilsman telegram, signaled a major shift in American policy. A few days later Mr. Lodge remarked, “We are launched on a course from which there is no respectable turning back.” ', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'The Ugly American Telegram', 'kicker': 'Op-Ed Contributor', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'The Ugly American Telegram', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Vietnam', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'United States International Relations', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Vietnam War', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Diem, Ngo Dinh', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2013-08-24T00:20:37+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'OpEd', 'section_name': 'Opinion', 'byline': {'original': 'By Andrew J. Bacevich', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Andrew', 'middlename': 'J.', 'lastname': 'Bacevich', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Op-Ed', '_id': 'nyt://article/eb9f2e7a-a126-5e9f-8a32-df038cff1ed3', 'word_count': 893, 'uri': 'nyt://article/eb9f2e7a-a126-5e9f-8a32-df038cff1ed3'}, {'abstract': 'Irina Falconi, a 23-year-old American player, explains wild cards, seedings in qualifying and the costs of the tour.', 'web_url': 'https://straightsets.blogs.nytimes.com/2013/08/23/qualifying-chronicles-answering-reader-questions/', 'snippet': 'Irina Falconi, a 23-year-old American player, explains wild cards, seedings in qualifying and the costs of the tour.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Irina Falconi, a 23-year-old American, who lost in the first round of qualifying, is writing posts for Straight Sets from the United States Open this year. You can also follow her on tumblr and Twitter.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Qualifying Chronicles: Answering Reader Questions', 'kicker': 'Straight Sets', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'United States Open (Tennis)', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'King, Vania', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Riske, Alison', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'vickery, Sachia', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'United States Tennis 