', 'byline': {'original': 'By Carlos Puig', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Carlos', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Puig', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/1b843194-adb1-538c-afdf-f24782b8f66a', 'word_count': 442, 'uri': 'nyt://article/1b843194-adb1-538c-afdf-f24782b8f66a'}, {'abstract': 'The Treasury secretary spoke to John Harwood about extending government funding past Sept. 30, dealing with the so-called budget sequester and raising the federal debt limit.', 'web_url': 'https://thecaucus.blogs.nytimes.com/2013/08/27/10-questions-for-jacob-lew/', 'snippet': 'The Treasury secretary spoke to John Harwood about extending government funding past Sept. 30, dealing with the so-called budget sequester and raising the federal debt limit.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Treasury Secretary Jacob J. Lew has been part of Washington’s fiscal battles for three decades, since he served as an aide to then-House Speaker Thomas P. O’Neill. But soon he will be the Obama administration’s point man on an unusual convergence of fronts: extending government funding past the end of the current fiscal year on Sept. 30, dealing with the so-called budget sequester and raising the federal debt limit.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': '10 Questions for Jacob Lew', 'kicker': 'The Caucus', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Federal Budget (US)', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'National Debt (US)', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'United States Economy', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Lew, Jacob J', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'House of Representatives', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2013-08-27T18:10:23+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'U.S.', 'subsection_name': 'Politics', 'byline': {'original': 'By John Harwood', 'person': [{'firstname': 'John', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Harwood', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/28c2d624-ee7a-53d4-8eda-35660df9b083', 'word_count': 1446, 'uri': 'nyt://article/28c2d624-ee7a-53d4-8eda-35660df9b083'}, {'abstract': 'Jurgen Melzer and Angelique Kerber are also among the top tennis players who are naturally right-handed but swing a racket with their left hands.', 'web_url': 'https://straightsets.blogs.nytimes.com/2013/08/27/in-choice-of-hands-nadal-is-not-alone/', 'snippet': 'Jurgen Melzer and Angelique Kerber are also among the top tennis players who are naturally right-handed but swing a racket with their left hands.', 'lead_paragraph': 'A European player belting tennis balls on Court 7 drew onlookers to his midday practice Sunday. Sprinting to his left, he hit a topspin forehand like he had done for as long as anyone could remember. He scampered to his right and smacked a two-handed backhand that jumped off the court. He is naturally right-handed but makes his living hitting the ball primarily with his non-dominant hand.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'In Choice of Hands, Nadal Is Not Alone', 'kicker': 'Straight Sets', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'United States Open (Tennis)', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Kerber, Angelique', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Melzer, Jurgen', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Nadal, Rafael', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2013-08-27T18:18:58+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Sports', 'subsection_name': 'Tennis', 'byli