e': 'Tennis', 'byline': {'original': 'By Ben Rothenberg', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Ben', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Rothenberg', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/88557072-2150-595a-b3b4-b21d8d841723', 'word_count': 369, 'uri': 'nyt://article/88557072-2150-595a-b3b4-b21d8d841723'}, {'abstract': '“The Tender Soldier,” by the journalist Vanessa M. Gezari, examines the military’s “Human Terrain” teams, which operate as quasi-social scientists in Iraq and Afghanistan.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2013/08/28/books/the-tender-soldier-examines-the-us-counterinsurgency-strategy.html', 'snippet': '“The Tender Soldier,” by the journalist Vanessa M. Gezari, examines the military’s “Human Terrain” teams, which operate as quasi-social scientists in Iraq and Afghanistan.', 'lead_paragraph': 'The wars in Iraq and Afghanistan, among other things, were stark reminders of how little Americans know about the rest of the world. A vast majority of new recruits had never left the United States before deploying for combat, and even many field commanders were clueless about the people they were expected to live among, protect and kill.', 'print_section': 'C', 'print_page': '2', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/08/28/arts/jpbook/jpbook-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 767, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2013/08/28/arts/jpbook/jpbook-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 767}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/08/28/arts/jpbook/jpbook-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 1024, 'width': 802, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': N