: 'Isabel', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Kershner', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/9b3b8d94-08e0-5946-a350-1abfada7eb58', 'word_count': 309, 'uri': 'nyt://article/9b3b8d94-08e0-5946-a350-1abfada7eb58'}, {'abstract': 'Some think the United States Tennis Association has been lazy and complacent in developing young players.', 'web_url': 'https://6thfloor.blogs.nytimes.com/2013/08/28/an-american-tennis-extravaganza-with-few-americans/', 'snippet': 'Some think the United States Tennis Association has been lazy and complacent in developing young players.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Arthur Ashe Kids’ Day at the U.S. Open last Saturday, a perfect August afternoon, was also a practice day for the players in the tournament, and through no particular acumen or foresight, I lucked into the best seat in the house to view the Billie Jean King National Tennis Center’s five practice courts: the top of the grandstand overlooking Court 4. As the children’s event went on down below — something with giant tennis balls and plastic cones — I leaned on the railing and gazed in the other direction, at the astounding scene on Courts P1 through P3: Roger Federer hitting with Tommy Haas, Rafael Nadal with Feliciano Lopez, Milos Raonic with Ernests Gulbis. Off in the distance, on P4 and P5, I spotted Victoria Azarenka and Agnieszka Radwanska. After a little while Andy Murray strolled onto P1 to replace Federer. The tennis was as glorious as the weather.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/08/27/magazine/27-andy-murray-practice/27-andy-murray-practice-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 393, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2013/08/27/magazine/27-andy-murray-practice/27-andy-murray-practice-articleLarge.j