sic', 'byline': {'original': 'By Sam Tanenhaus', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Sam', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Tanenhaus', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/5c3c3078-885b-5ed3-a8ae-cc1fb38b6b9a', 'word_count': 2032, 'uri': 'nyt://article/5c3c3078-885b-5ed3-a8ae-cc1fb38b6b9a'}, {'abstract': 'For a conversation about the sordid world of Washington, check back here to watch a live-streaming Times Talk at 7:30 p.m. E.D.T.', 'web_url': 'https://6thfloor.blogs.nytimes.com/2013/08/28/mark-leibovich-talks-with-the-stars-from-house-of-cards/', 'snippet': 'For a conversation about the sordid world of Washington, check back here to watch a live-streaming Times Talk at 7:30 p.m. E.D.T.', 'lead_paragraph': 'If the sordid world of Washington excites you; if Congressman Frank Underwood’s salacious maneuvering on “House of Cards” shook you up; if you enjoyed the rollicking narrative Mark Leibovich delivered in his book, “This Town: Two Parties and a Funeral — Plus, Plenty of Valet Parking! — in America’s Gilded Capital,” which was excerpted in the magazine earlier this summer; then you should watch this Times Talk. The conversation, with the “House of Cards” star and executive producer Kevin Spacey, his co-star Robin Wright, the producer-writer Beau Willimon and Mark Leibovich, chief national correspondent for the magazine, was the first Times Talk to take place not in front of a live audience, but on the set of a show. Watch below:', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/08/27/magazine/27-house-of-cards/27-house-of-cards-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 400, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2013/08/27/magazine/27-house-of-cards/27-house-of-cards-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlar