ce': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/08/29/garden/29OPEN1_SPAN/29CURR1-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 400, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2013/08/29/garden/29OPEN1_SPAN/29CURR1-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 400}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/08/29/garden/29OPEN1_SPAN/29CURR1-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 683, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/08/29/garden/29OPEN1_SPAN/29CURR1-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 1366, 'width': 2048, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/08/29/garden/29OPEN1_SPAN/29OPEN1_SPAN-thumbStandard-v2.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2013/08/29/garden/29OPEN1_SPAN/29OPEN1_SPAN-thumbStandard-v2.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/08/29/garden/29OPEN1_SPAN/29OPEN1_SPAN-thumbLarge-v2.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Knock Twice for Offbeat Design', 'kicker': 'Currents | Open', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Knock Twice for Offbeat Design', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Shopping and Retail', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Interior Design and Furnishings', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Studio O+A', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2013-08-28T22:21:46+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Home', 'section_name': 'Home & Garden', 'byline': {'original': 'By Julie Lasky', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Julie', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Lasky', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/0afcf241-def6-5eb8-af23-19ee1eeb731f', 'word_count': 164, 'uri': 'nyt://article/0afcf241-def6-5eb8-af23-19ee1eeb731f'}, {'abstract': 'The jewelry designer shares her impressions of the island of Patmos, where she savored the the beaches, the colors and the fried cheese.', 'web_url': 'https://tmagazine.blogs.nytimes.com/2013/08/28/travel-diary-irene-neuwirths-eight-great-days-in-greece/', 'snippet': 'The jewelry designer shares her impressions of the island of Patmos, where she savored the the beaches, the colors and the fried cheese.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Irene Neuwirth, the fine jewelry designer whose colorful statement pieces are red carpet regulars and top sellers at Barneys New York, calls herself a seasoned traveler. Yet after her long flight from Los Angeles to London to Athens, and then her eight-hour overnight boat ride from Athens to Patmos, she said, “I was proud of myself for making the trip. The cabin was disgusting, and people were drunk and sleeping on the stairs. I got there and was like, ‘Maybe I made a mistake coming here.’ And then you drag your bags down, and you’re waiting in the bottom of the boat where they let the cars out, and it opens out into paradise. It’s so gorgeous.”', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Irene Neuwirth’s Eight Great Days in Greece', 'kicker': 'T Magazine', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Travel and Vacations', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Neuwirth, Irene', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Patmos (Greece)', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2013-08-28T22:23:27+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'TStyle', 'section_name': 'T Magazine', 'byline': {'original': 'By T Magazine', 'person': [{'firstname': '', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': '', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/b5687ba8-caa0-5511-a499-36701f1ab3aa', 'word_count': 203, 'uri': 'nyt://article/b5687ba8-caa0-5511-a499-36701f1ab3aa'}, {'abstract': 'Rita Motta designs bubble-inspired carpets for Tai Ping.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2013/08/29/garden/its-enough-to-give-you-hiccups.html', 'snippet': 'Rita Motta designs bubble-inspired carpets for Tai Ping.', 'lead_paragraph': '“When you say something is bubbly, you’re talking about something that is light and free,” said Rita Motta, an Italian-born interior and product designer who has made spherical forms a signature. Ms. Motta — a founder, with her husband, Michael Perez, of the Miami design studio Mr. O — first put bubbles on cabinets and side tables. Now they have been hand-sheared into rugs for Tai Ping. The wool and silk rugs come in effervescent colors like magenta, vermilion (shown), citron and violet. Following their introduction last winter at Design Miami, Ms. Motta said the jewel tones stood out among the neutrals favored in showrooms.', 'print_section': 'D', 'print_page': '3', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/08/29/garden/29RUGS1/29CURR3-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 400, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2013/08/29/garden/29RUGS1/29CURR3-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 400}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': 