e, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/08/29/garden/20130829-WATERMELON-slide-S1NW/20130829-WATERMELON-slide-S1NW-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Paws Off My Watermelon!', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Gardens and Gardening', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Long Island City (Queens, NY)', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2013-08-28T22:26:33+0000', 'document_type': 'multimedia', 'news_desk': 'Home & Garden', 'section_name': 'Home & Garden', 'byline': {'original': '', 'person': [], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Slideshow', '_id': 'nyt://slideshow/55384a23-8e3c-5296-8da2-8e95e9acc6ca', 'word_count': 0, 'uri': 'nyt://slideshow/55384a23-8e3c-5296-8da2-8e95e9acc6ca'}, {'abstract': 'A Norwegian trio designs their first product: a chair called Oslo.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2013/08/29/garden/the-scandinavian-way.html', 'snippet': 'A Norwegian trio designs their first product: a chair called Oslo.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Jerry Helling, president of Bernhardt Design, makes a habit of boosting the careers of young designers. His latest protégés are Christoffer Angell, Oyvind Wyller and Simen Aarseth, founders of AWAA, a Norwegian studio, whom he met at a cocktail party and asked to create a side chair with arms. They returned with Oslo, a chair that will be introduced at the London Design Festival on Sept. 18. The trio, who had never designed a commercial product, focused first on the chair’s skeletal structure, creating a sturdy frame of solid walnut. They added a sling seat and a back that is attached to the frame with buttons. The look reminds Mr. Helling of a messenger bag; others see it as a faithful homage to Scandinavian modern.', 'print_section': 'D', 'print_page': '3', 'source':