ths (Obituaries)', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'creative_works', 'value': 'The English Patient (Movie)', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2013-08-29T02:44:01+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Culture', 'section_name': 'Arts', 'subsection_name': 'Art & Design', 'byline': {'original': 'By Daniel E. Slotnik', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Daniel', 'middlename': 'E.', 'lastname': 'Slotnik', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Obituary (Obit)', '_id': 'nyt://article/2bc08346-30d2-51f8-8794-1f920f8e5ecf', 'word_count': 404, 'uri': 'nyt://article/2bc08346-30d2-51f8-8794-1f920f8e5ecf'}, {'abstract': 'The police said a jealous livery-cab driver rammed a car his ex-girlfriend was in, then stabbed her.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2013/08/29/nyregion/woman-dies-in-stabbing-after-a-bronx-car-chase-onto-i-95.html', 'snippet': 'The police said a jealous livery-cab driver rammed a car his ex-girlfriend was in, then stabbed her.', 'lead_paragraph': 'The two cars came barreling down the on-ramp to the New England Thruway: one, an S.U.V. with a man and woman inside; the other, a livery cab painted green, part of New York City’s new program to allow street hails outside Manhattan.', 'print_section': 'A', 'print_page': '21', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Woman Dies in Stabbing After a Bronx Car Chase Onto I-95', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Bronx Car Chase Ends in a Fatal Stabbing', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Bronx (NYC)', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Murders and Attempted Murders', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Interstate 95', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Jimenez, Jose', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'New York City', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2013-08-29T02:47:00+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Metro', 'section_name': 'New York', 'byline': {'original': 'By J. David Goodman', 'person': [{'firstname': 'J.', 'middlename': 'David', 'lastname': 'Goodman', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/1646847e-a579-5ea8-93f7-4ae5f68de853', 'word_count': 655, 'uri': 'nyt://article/1646847e-a579-5ea8-93f7-4ae5f68de853'}, {'abstract': 'A Mumford & Sons show in Forest Hills was the first music performance since 1997 at the former site of the United States Open.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2013/08/29/nyregion/concert-anyone-music-returns-to-queens-tennis-stadium.html', 'snippet': 'A Mumford & Sons show in Forest Hills was the first music performance since 1997 at the former site of the United States Open.', 'lead_paragraph': 'People wore terry-cloth sweatbands and headbands, and underfoot, the familiar outlines of a tennis court could be seen inside the stadium in Forest Hills, Queens. But on the court, girls in cutoffs shimmied. And the wristbands and headbands did not sop up sweat from athletes, but rather served as V.I.P. passes of sorts — yellow was for coveted floor space; blue meant banishment to the stands.', 'print_section': 'A', 'print_page': '20', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/08/29/nyregion/FORESTHILLS1/FORESTHILLS1-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 400, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2013/08/29/nyregion/FORESTHILLS1/FORESTHILLS1-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 400}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/08/29/nyregion/FORESTHILLS1/FORESTHILLS1-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 683