byline': {'original': '', 'person': [], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Slideshow', '_id': 'nyt://slideshow/68755e26-ffe1-5787-8953-724f9b227906', 'word_count': 0, 'uri': 'nyt://slideshow/68755e26-ffe1-5787-8953-724f9b227906'}, {'abstract': 'Television highlights.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2013/08/29/arts/television/whats-on-thursday.html', 'snippet': 'Television highlights.', 'lead_paragraph': '4:30 P.M. (HBO) GLICKMAN (2013) In 1936, the Bronx-born Marty Glickman, above, a national sprinting champion, earned a spot on the United States Olympic track team as part of the 400-meter relay. But in Berlin, with Hitler in the stands, Mr. Glickman and Sam Stoller — the only Jews on the relay team — were replaced the day before the event by Jesse Owens and Ralph Metcalfe, ostensibly because “secret” German runners were expected to challenge for the gold. (Mr. Glickman, who died in 2001, maintained that anti-Semitism was at work.) The Americans won the race; the Germans finished a distant fourth. After returning to the United States, Mr. Glickman eventually found his niche in basketball and football broadcasting. Despite being rejected for a “more Midwestern” announcer when the N.B.A. finally signed a network contract, he became the voice of the Giants during the 1950s and ’60s and the Jets in 1972. This documentary from James L. Freedman, who produced Mr. Glickman’s late-night radio show as a high school senior, brings to light some of his pioneering techniques, phrases and programming innovations. Marv Albert, Bob Costas, Bill Bradley, Frank Gifford and Larry King weigh in.', 'print_section': 'C', 'print_page': '8', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'What’s on Thursday', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'What’s On Thursday', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [], 'pub_date': '2013-08-29T05:00:05+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Culture', 'section_name': 