st Edmund de Waal, who is preparing for his first exhibition in the United States, “pots turn into words, and words turn into pots.”', 'lead_paragraph': 'LONDON — In England, Edmund de Waal is a celebrated potter, known for installations of impeccably made vessels in soft shades of celadon or white, many of them permanently displayed in places like the Victoria and Albert Museum here. But in the United States Mr. de Waal is known primarily as a writer whose 2010 family memoir “The Hare With Amber Eyes” became a surprise best seller. Despite countless rejections from publishers, it ended up selling more than 1.5 million copies, has been translated into 26 languages and was recently optioned for a movie.', 'print_section': 'AR', 'print_page': '1', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/09/01/arts/01DEWAAL_SPAN/01DEWAAL_SPAN-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 400, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2013/09/01/arts/01DEWAAL_SPAN/01DEWAAL_SPAN-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 400}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/09/01/arts/01DEWAAL_SPAN/01DEWAAL_SPAN-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 683, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/09/01/arts/01DEWAAL_SPAN/01DEWAAL_SPAN-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 1367, 'width': 2048, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/09/01/arts/01DEWAAL_SPAN/01DEWAAL_SPAN-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2013/09/01/arts/01DEWAAL_SPAN/01DEWAAL_SPAN-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/09/01/arts/01DEWAAL_SPAN/01DEWAAL_SPAN-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Poetry Written in Porcelain', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Poetry Written in Porcelain', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Vogel, Carol', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Art', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'de Waal, Edmund', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Ceramics and Pottery', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Gagosian Gallery', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'England', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Books and Literature', 'rank': 7, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2013-08-29T12:00:07+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Arts&Leisure', 'section_name': 'Arts', 'subsection_name': 'Art & Design', 'byline': {'original': 'By Carol Vogel', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Carol', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Vogel', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/7c452c77-8529-5687-82c6-45035a4321c4', 'word_count': 1766, 'uri': 'nyt://article/7c452c77-8529-5687-82c6-45035a4321c4'}, {'abstract': 'Scientists are studying whether mothers pass microbes to their fetuses during gestation, research that could help fight some pregnancy complications.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2013/08/29/science/human-microbiome-may-be-seeded-before-birth.html', 'snippet': 'Scientists are studying whether mothers pass microbes to their fetuses during gestation, research that could help fight some pregnancy complications.', 'lead_paragraph': 'We are each home to about 100 trillion bacteria, which we carry with us from birth till death. But when Juliette C. Madan was trained as a neonatologist in the mid-2000s, her teachers told her in no uncertain terms that we only acquire those bacteria after we are born. “It was clear as day, we were told, that fetuses were sterile,” she said.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/08/29/science/29zimmer/29zimmer-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 790, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2013/08/29/science/29zimmer/29zimmer-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 790}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/08/29/science/29zimmer/29zimmer-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 1024, 'width': 778, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/08/29/science/29zimmer/29zimmer-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 2048, 'width': 1556, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/08/29/science/29zimmer/29zimmer-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2013/08/29/science/29zimmer/29zimmer-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/08/29/science/29zimmer/29zimmer-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Human Microbiome May Be Seeded Before Birth', 'kicker': 'Matter', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Babies and Infants', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'AMNIOCENTESIS', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Bacteria', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Science and Technology', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2013-08-29T12:00:07+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Science', 'section_name': 'Science', 'byline': {'original': 'By Carl Zimmer', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Carl', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Zimmer', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/ec41bfa6-7f78-51e0-b981-4980b2f5ba4b', 'word_count': 831, 'uri': 'nyt://article/ec41bfa6-7f78-51e0-b981-4980b2f5ba4b'}, {'abstract': 'The central bank acted at a hastily called meeting to protect the nation’s currency, which has slumped to its lowest level since April 2009.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2013/08/30/business/global/indonesia-raises-key-interest-rate.html', 'snippet': 'The central bank acted at a hastily called meeting to protect the nation’s currency, which has slumped to its lowest level since April 2009.', 'lead_paragraph': 'The Indonesian central bank raised its interest rates on Thursday, in a desperate effort to shore up a currency that has been badly hit by the recent sell-off in emerging markets worldwide.', 'print_section': 'B', 'print_page': '3', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Indonesia Increases Benchmark Interest Rate', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Indonesia Increases Benchmark Interest Rate', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Interest Rates', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Indonesia', 'rank': 2, 'major':