ause-button/', 'snippet': 'Detroit Electric, which was planning to produce electric cars in Michigan, said on Thursday that it would first make cars in the Netherlands.', 'lead_paragraph': 'The reborn Detroit Electric definitely has one thing in common with the original company, which went out of business in 1939 — it ran into problems trying to sell battery-powered vehicles. Detroit Electric is the latest in a long series of companies to have had difficulty realizing big dreams in the electric marketplace.', 'print_section': 'AU', 'print_page': '10', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/08/29/automobiles/wheels/Detroit-Electric-kaput/Detroit-Electric-kaput-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 338, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2013/08/29/automobiles/wheels/Detroit-Electric-kaput/Detroit-Electric-kaput-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 338}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/08/29/automobiles/wheels/Detroit-Electric-kaput/Detroit-Electric-kaput-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 347, 'width': 616, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/08/29/automobiles/wheels/Detroit-Electric-kaput/Detroit-Electric-kaput-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 347, 'width': 616, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/08/29/automobiles/wheels/Detroit-Electric-kaput/Detroit-Electric-kaput-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2013/08/29/automobiles/wheels/Detroit-Electric-kaput/Detroit-Electric-kaput-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/08/29/automobiles/wheels/Detroit-Electric-kaput/Detroit-Electric-kaput-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Detroit Electric Pushes the Pause Button', 'kicker': 'Wheels', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Detroit Electric Reboots', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Automobiles', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Start-ups', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Detroit Electric Holdings Ltd', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2013-08-29T19:40:45+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Automobiles', 'byline': {'original': 'By Jim Motavalli', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Jim', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Motavalli', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/f5c9bf4d-90c0-59fc-9976-32de4629f43f', 'word_count': 574, 'uri': 'nyt://article/f5c9bf4d-90c0-59fc-9976-32de4629f43f'}, {'abstract': 'Today’s U.S. Open food review: the pulled chicken torta at Fresca Mexicana in the main food court.', 'web_url': 'https://straightsets.blogs.nytimes.com/2013/08/29/u-s-open-food-review-the-sandwich-revolution/', 'snippet': 'Today’s U.S. Open food review: the pulled chicken torta at Fresca Mexicana in the main food court.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Because eating at the United States Open is a high-stakes game, we will feature a daily review of items available at the food courts at the National Tennis Center. ', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'U.S. Open Food Review: The Sandwich Revolution', 'kicker': 'Straight Sets', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Tennis', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'United States Open (Tennis)', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'National Tennis Center', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2013-08-29T19:44:36+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Sports', 'subsection_name': 'Tennis', 'byline': {'original': 'By Lynn Zinser', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Lynn', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Zinser', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/987d8529-8d6f-5b3a-9b24-9b600bcaf04b', 'word_count': 391, 'uri': 'nyt://article/987d8529-8d6f-5b3a-9b24-9b600bcaf04b'}, {'abstract': 'The forgotten French advantage.', 'web_url': 'https://krugman.blogs.nytimes.com/2013/08/29/the-gloire-to-come/', 'snippet': 'The forgotten French advantage.', 'lead_paragraph': 'A very belated reaction to Steven Erlanger’s piece on French decline, on fears that the proud nation is slipping into second-tier status. One point one should always note here is Roger Cohen’s: the French have been morose for decades now, yet the country remains a pretty good place to live. Maybe some discounting is called for?', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'The Gloire To Come', 'kicker': 'Paul Krugman', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [], 'pub_date': '2013-08-29T19:48:04+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Opinion', 'byline': {'original': 'By Paul Krugman', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Paul', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Krugman', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/a21de4e1-cbcf-5867-992e-e6b0f22dd086', 'word_count': 184, 'uri': 'nyt://article/a21de4e1-cbcf-5867-992e-e6b0f22dd086'}, {'abstract': 'A number of contemporary Jewish writers are engaging with religious belief in their works.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2013/09/01/books/review/articles-of-faith.html', 'snippet': 'A number of contemporary Jewish writers are engaging with religious belief in their works.', 'lead_paragraph': 'In January, The New Yorker’s Shouts & Murmurs blog serialized a lighthearted novella called “Sell Out,” by Simon Rich, about a religious Jewish immigrant in 1912 Brooklyn who falls into a vat of brine at a pickle factory. Emerging intact a century later, he meets his hipster great-great-grandson and ultimately draws him back to some version of faith. ', 'print_section': 'BR', 'print_page': '31', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/09/01/books/review/0901Horn/0901Horn-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 754, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2013/09/01/books/review/0901Horn/0901Horn-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 754}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/09/01/books/review/0901Horn/0901Horn-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 1024, 'width': 816, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/09/01/books/review/0901Horn/0901Horn-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 2048, 'width': 1631, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/09/01/books/review/0901Horn/0901Horn-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'th