igurations, a duo set with John Zorn, and a rock trio.', 'lead_paragraph': 'When John Zorn first opened the Stone, his humble, no-frills East Village performance space, in 2005, the booking policy indicated his credo: artists first. Mr. Zorn, a celebrated composer, improviser and bandleader, is among New York’s foremost community builders, having stitched together an impressive range of friends and peers into an estimable aesthetic bloc.', 'print_section': 'C', 'print_page': '10', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/08/30/arts/0830FRITH_SPAN/0830FRITH-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 400, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2013/08/30/arts/0830FRITH_SPAN/0830FRITH-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 400}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/08/30/arts/0830FRITH_SPAN/0830FRITH-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 683, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/08/30/arts/0830FRITH_SPAN/0830FRITH-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 1365, 'width': 2048, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/08/30/arts/0830FRITH_SPAN/0830FRITH_SPAN-thumbStandard-v2.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2013/08/30/arts/0830FRITH_SPAN/0830FRITH_SPAN-thumbStandard-v2.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/08/30/arts/0830FRITH_SPAN/0830FRITH_SPAN-thumbLarge-v2.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Guitarist and His Friends Make for a Diverse Week', 'kicker': 'Music Review', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Guitarist and His Friends Make for a Diverse Week', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Frith, Fred', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Music', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Stone, The (Manhattan, NY, Performance Space)', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2013-08-29T20:38:14+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Weekend', 'section_name': 'Arts', 'subsection_name': 'Music', 'byline': {'original': 'By Steve Smith', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Steve', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Smith', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Review', '_id': 'nyt://article/66ddb34c-450c-5107-9cba-ca044683b5f1', 'word_count': 491, 'uri': 'nyt://article/66ddb34c-450c-5107-9cba-ca044683b5f1'}, {'abstract': 'The exhibition “Leonid Sokov: Ironic Objects,” which explores how the artist responded to politics and consumer culture, is under way at the Zimmerli Art Museum at Rutgers.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2013/09/01/nyregion/a-review-of-leonid-sokov-ironic-objects-at-the-zimmerli-art-museum.html', 'snippet': 'The exhibition “Leonid Sokov: Ironic Objects,” which explores how the artist responded to politics and consumer culture, is under way at the Zimmerli Art Museum at Rutgers.', 'lead_paragraph': 'There is an entire body of scholarly writing around the word “irony” as it relates to literature. There is not as much for art objects. But irony seems to flourish in periods when political repression is high and artists resort to registering their protest through humor or subversive imitation. Such is the case in “Leonid Sokov: Ironic Objects” at the Zimmerli Art Museum in New Brunswick. ', 'print_section': 'NJ', 'print_page': '8', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/09/01/nyregion/01ARTSNJ1/01ARTSNJ1-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 514, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2013/09/01/nyregion/01ARTSNJ1/01ARTSNJ1-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 514}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/09/01/nyregion/01ARTSNJ1/01ARTSNJ1-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 878, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/09/01/nyregion/01ARTSNJ1/01ARTSNJ1-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 1756, 'width': 2048, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/09/01/nyregion/01ARTSNJ1/01ARTSNJ1-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2013/09/01/nyregion/01ARTSNJ1/01ARTSNJ1-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/09/01/nyregion/01ARTSNJ1/01ARTSNJ1-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'The Art of Propaganda, Repurposed', 'kicker': 'Arts Review | New Jersey', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'The Art of Propaganda, Repurposed', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Art', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'New Brunswick (NJ)', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 