pinion', 'byline': {'original': 'By Ginger McCall', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Ginger', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'McCall', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Op-Ed', '_id': 'nyt://article/087ba6bf-a4cb-5b13-b13f-59dd7f728d5a', 'word_count': 696, 'uri': 'nyt://article/087ba6bf-a4cb-5b13-b13f-59dd7f728d5a'}, {'abstract': 'Those who oppose the East Midtown rezoning plan may love New York, but they don’t understand that they are compromising the city’s future.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2013/08/30/opinion/gothams-towering-ambitions.html', 'snippet': 'Those who oppose the East Midtown rezoning plan may love New York, but they don’t understand that they are compromising the city’s future.', 'lead_paragraph': 'FOR much of the last century, New York City has been the financial, cultural, media, retailing and fashion capital of not just the country but the world. Yet the great Hudson River metropolis is in danger of losing that status because of a growing local attitude that favors the old over the new, stability over growth, the status quo over change and short buildings over tall ones. ', 'print_section': 'A', 'print_page': '19', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Gotham’s Towering Ambitions', 'kicker': 'Op-Ed Contributor', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Gotham’s Towering Ambitions', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Midtown Area (Manhattan, NY)', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Jackson, Kenneth T', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Historic Buildings and Sites', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Buildings (Structures)', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Zoning', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'New York City', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Area Planning and Renewal', 'rank': 7, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2013-08-30T01:18:47+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'OpEd', 'section_name': 'Opinion', 'byline': {'original': 'By Kenneth T. Jackson', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Kenneth', 'middlename': 'T.', 'lastname': 'Jackson', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Op-Ed', '_id': 'nyt://article/4fa54ac6-e0b9-549f-bc13-d85e6bbd6fa1', 'word_count': 979, 'uri': 'nyt://article/4fa54ac6-e0b9-549f-bc13-d85e6bbd6fa1'}, {'abstract': 'The author of a post on a Facebook page that appears to belong to the son of Syria’s president, Bashar al-Assad, dared the United States to attack Syria, predicting that “victory is ours in the end.”', 'web_url': 'https://thelede.blogs.nytimes.com/2013/08/29/facebook-post-said-to-be-by-assads-son-dares-americans-to-attack/', 'snippet': 'The author of a post on a Facebook page that appears to belong to the son of Syria’s president, Bashar al-Assad, dared the United States to attack Syria, predicting that “victory is ours in the end.”', 'lead_paragraph': 'A Facebook post said to be written by the 11-year-old son of the Syrian president, Bashar al-Assad, and “liked” or commented on by several people who appear to be the children and grandchildren of other senior members of Mr. Assad’s government, may offer a glimpse into the mindset of Syria’s ruling elite as the country braces for a potential Western strike in response to a chemical weapons attack on Aug. 21. ', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Facebook Post Said to Be by Assad’s Son Dares Americans to Attack', 'kicker': 'The Lede', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Middle East and North Africa Unrest (2010- )', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Assad, Bashar al-'