abortion bill, is considering a campaign for governor even though she is thought to be a long shot.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2013/08/30/us/politics/democrats-see-value-in-texas-in-candidacy-for-governor.html', 'snippet': 'State Senator Wendy Davis of Texas, who rocketed to fame with her 11-hour filibuster to block an anti-abortion bill, is considering a campaign for governor even though she is thought to be a long shot.', 'lead_paragraph': 'FORT WORTH — Early this month, Wendy Davis, the unknown Texas state senator who rocketed to fame this summer with her 11-hour filibuster to block an anti-abortion bill, met with strategists of the Democratic Governors Association at their K Street offices in Washington. They were urging her to run for governor next year, even though Texas is not among their realistic targets for flipping a Republican-held executive mansion.', 'print_section': 'A', 'print_page': '11', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/08/30/us/SUB-JP-DAVIS/SUB-JP-DAVIS-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 368, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2013/08/30/us/SUB-JP-DAVIS/SUB-JP-DAVIS-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 368}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/08/30/us/SUB-JP-DAVIS/SUB-JP-DAVIS-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 629, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/08/30/us/SUB-JP-DAVIS/SUB-JP-DAVIS-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 1257, 'width': 2048, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/08/30/us/SUB-JP-DAVIS/SUB-JP-DAVIS-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2013/08/30/us/SUB-JP-DAVIS/SUB-JP-DAVIS-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/08/30/us/SUB-JP-DAVIS/SUB-JP-DAVIS-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Democrats See Value in Texas in Candidacy for Governor', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Democrats See Value in Texas In Candidacy For Governor', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Davis, Wendy', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Gabriel, Trip', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Elections, Governors', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Texas', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Democratic Party', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Republican Party', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2013-08-30T02:02:00+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'National', 'section_name': 'U.S.', 'subsection_name': 'Politics', 'byline': {'original': 'By Trip Gabriel', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Trip', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Gabriel', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/1a47ff19-6df4-5974-8ce4-25fd6995b923', 'word_count': 1268, 'uri': 'nyt://article/1a47ff19-6df4-5974-8ce4-25fd6995b923'}, {'abstract': 'A judge ruled Thursday that the Department of Veterans Affairs violated federal law in leasing for commercial use parts of its campus in West Los Angeles.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2013/08/30/us/california-veterans-center-lease-ruled-illegal.html', 'snippet': 'A judge ruled Thursday that the Department of Veterans Affairs violated federal law in leasing for commercial use parts of its campus in West Los Angeles.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Judge S. James Otero of Federal District Court ruled Thursday that the Department of Veterans Affairs violated federal law in leasing for commercial use parts of its campus in West Los Angeles. The ruling was in response to a class-action lawsuit on behalf of four homeless veterans, who objected to the lease when homelessness among veterans was high. Judge Otero ruled that Congress permitted such lease agreements only if the property was going to be used to provide health or other services for veterans. The campus has been leased for, among other things, a laundry for a luxury hotel. ', 'print_section': 'A', 'print_page': '12', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'California: Veterans Center Lease Ruled Illegal', 'kicker': 'National Briefing | West', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'California: Veterans Center Lease Ruled Illegal', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Veterans', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Homeless Persons', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Los Angeles (Calif)', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Veterans Affairs Department', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2013-08-30T02:15:47+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'National', 'section_name': 'U.S.', 'byline': {'original': 'By Adam Nagourney', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Adam', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Nagourney', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Brief', '_id': 'nyt://article/daf210ff-4a18-536f-8723-9ecec1b68ee8', 'word_count': 99, 'uri': 'nyt://article/daf210ff-4a18-536f-8723-9ecec1b68ee8'}, {'abstract': 'Former Lt. Gov. Timothy P. Murray on Thursday agreed to refund $50,000 and pay a $30,000 fine to settle accusations that he accepted illegal campaign contributions.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2013/08/30/us/massachusetts-ex-official-refunds-contributions.html', 'snippet': 'Former Lt. Gov. Timothy P. Murray on Thursday agreed to refund $50,000 and pay a $30,000 fine to settle accusations that he accepted illegal campaign contributions.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Former Lt. Gov. Timothy P. Murray on Thursday agreed to refund $50,000 and pay a $30,000 fine to settle accusations that he accepted illegal campaign contributions. Attorney General Martha Coakley said Mr. Murray received $50,000 in contributions illegally solicited by two public employees. One of them, Michael McLaughlin, an ex-housing official serving a three-year sentence for lying about his income to federal authorities, was indicted Thursday on charges of unlawfully soliciting campaign contributions from state employees. ', 'print_section': 'A', 'print_page': '12', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Massachusetts: Ex-Official Refunds Contributions', 'kicker': 'National Briefing | New England', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Massachusetts: Ex-Official Refunds Contributions', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Bidgood, Jess', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Campaign Finance', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Fines (Penalties)', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Murray, Timothy P', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Massachusetts', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2013-08-30T02:17:00+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'National', 'section_name': 'U.S.', 'byline': {'original': 'By Jess Bidgood', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Jess', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Bidgood', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Brief', '_id': 'nyt://article/9c68ce75-5e54-5cee-ae2b-979e9d4c2f27', 'word_count': 76, 'uri': 'nyt://article/9c68ce75-5e54-5cee-ae2b-979e9d4c2f27'}, {'abstract': 'Robel Phillipos, a classmate of Dzhokhar Tsarnaev, the Boston Marathon bombing suspect, was indicted on two counts of making false statements during the investigation.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2013/08/30/us/massachusetts-bomb-suspects-classmate-charged.html', 'snippet': 'Robel Phillipos, a classmate of Dzhokhar Tsarnaev, the Boston Marathon bombing suspect, was indicted on two counts of making false statements during the investigation.', 'lead_paragraph': 'A classmate of Dzhokhar Tsarnaev, the Boston Marathon bombing suspect, was indicted Thursday on two counts of making false statements during the investigation. The man, Robel Phillipos, 19, is accused of concealing from investigators the fact that he accompanied two of Mr. Tsarnaev’s other friends, Dias Kadyrbayev and Azamat Tazhayakov, to Mr. Tsarnaev’s dormitory at the University of Massachusetts Dartmouth days after the bombing. Investigators say that the men removed items including a laptop and a backpack, and that Mr. Kadyrbayev disposed of the backpack. Mr. Phillipos could face up to eight years in prison on each count and a $250,000 fine. Mr. Kadyrbayev and Mr. Tazhayakov have been charged with obstruction of justice. ', 'print_section': 'A', 'print_page': '12', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Massachusetts: Bomb Suspect’s Classmate Charged', 'kicker': 'National Briefing | New England', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Massachusetts: Bomb Suspect’s Classmate Charged', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Boston Marathon Bombings (2013)', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Bidgood, Jess', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Phillipos, Robel', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Tsarnaev, Dzhokhar A', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Massachusetts', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2013-08-30T02:18:30+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'National', 'section_name': 'U.S.', 'byline': {'original': 'By Jess Bidgood', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Jess', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Bidgood', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Brief', '_id': 'nyt://article/12c529e3-18d0-5e23-9a13-64217d1d0e28', 'word_count': 114, 'uri': 'nyt://article/12c529e3-18d0-5e23-9a13-64217d1d0e28'}, {'abstract': 'The two men were the first detainees to leave the prison since President Obama renewed his pledge to close it in May.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2013/08/30/us/2-guantanamo-detainees-transferred-to-algeria.html', 'snippet': 'The two men were the first detainees to leave the prison since President Obama renewed his pledge to close it in May.', 'lead_paragraph': 'The Pentagon announced\xa0on Thursday\xa0that it had transferred two Algerian detainees from the military prison at Guantánamo Bay, Cuba, to their native country. The two men, Nabil Said Hadjarab and Mutia Sadiq Ahmad Sayyab, who have spent more than a decade incarcerated without charges, were the first detainees to leave the prison since President Obama renewed his pledge to close it in May. ', 'print_section': 'A', 'print_page': '12', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': '2 Guantánamo Detainees Transferred to Algeria', 'kicker': 'National Briefing | Washington', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '2 Guantánamo Detainees Transferred To Algeria', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Detainees', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'United States Defense and Military Forces', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Obama, Barack', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Guantanamo Bay Naval Base (Cuba)', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2013-08-30T02:19:40+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'National', 'section_name': 'U.S.', 'byline': {'original': 'By Scott Shane', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Scott', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Shane', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Brief', '_id': 'nyt://article/c89b75f3-02cb-52ec-897d-298a4a112d83', 'word_count': 62, 'uri': 'nyt://article/c89b75f3-02cb-52ec-897d-298a4a112d83'}, {'abstract': 'Graham DeLaet had lower back surgery one year after his debut on the PGA Tour, requiring him to miss virtually all of the 2011 season, but he has overcome the physical and mental pain.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2013/08/30/sports/golf/delaet-survivor-of-back-woes-soldiers-on.html', 'snippet': 'Graham DeLaet had lower back surgery one year after his debut on the PGA Tour, requiring him to miss virtually all of the 2011 season, but he has overcome the physical and mental pain.', 'lead_paragraph': 'NORTON, Mass. — Tiger Woods’s back woes dominated the news on the eve of the $8 million Deutsche Bank Championship. Fifteen of the 24 questions he fielded on Thursday — and 12 of the first 14 — concerned his back, which bothered him last weekend at the Barclays in Jersey City. ', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/08/30/sports/Y-GOLF/Y-GOLF-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 400, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2013/08/30/sports/Y-GOLF/Y-GOLF-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 400}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 