+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'New York', 'byline': {'original': 'By Nicholas Wells', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Nicholas', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Wells', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/aecd0bb5-4a53-5179-b461-f148db64e610', 'word_count': 3008, 'uri': 'nyt://article/aecd0bb5-4a53-5179-b461-f148db64e610'}, {'abstract': 'A bride who chose not to make her twin sister a bridesmaid (because of her many tattoos) asks how she can honor her during the ceremony, and other etiquette questions answered.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2013/09/01/fashion/weddings/wedding-q-and-a.html', 'snippet': 'A bride who chose not to make her twin sister a bridesmaid (because of her many tattoos) asks how she can honor her during the ceremony, and other etiquette questions answered.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Tattoos Cause a Bridal Party Problem', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/02/11/fashion/weddings/peggypost/peggypost-jumbo-v2.jpg', 'height': 288, 'width': 288, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/02/11/fashion/weddings/peggypost/peggypost-thumbStandard-v2.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2011/02/11/fashion/weddings/peggypost/peggypost-thumbStandard-v2.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/02/11/fashion/weddings/peggypost/peggypost-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Wedding Q. and A.', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Wedding Q+A', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Weddings and Engagements', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Customs, Etiquette and Manners', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2013-08-30T14:14:51+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Society', 'section_name': 'Fashion & Style', 'subsection_name': 'Weddings', 'byline': {'original': 'By Peggy Post', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Peggy', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Post', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/761401d0-9f24-5394-ab56-7307f9c8cb65', 'word_count': 1052, 'uri': 'nyt://article/761401d0-9f24-5394-ab56-7307f9c8cb65'}, {'abstract': 'Jonathan Franzen once wrote about Thomas Pynchon in a letter: “The better he is the more I want to hate him but the less I can.”', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2013/09/01/books/review/pynchons-gravitational-pull.html', 'snippet': 'Jonathan Franzen once wrote about Thomas Pynchon in a letter: “The better he is the more I want to hate him but the less I can.”', 'lead_paragraph': 'In an essay in the September issue of Harper’s, Jonathan Franzen quotes — at what he concedes is “embarrassing length” — from letters he sent to his fiancée when he was 22. “I recognize Pynchon as my major precursor,” the young, besotted Franzen wrote. “The better he is the more I want to hate him but the less I can.” In an interview with The Paris Review in 2010, Franzen described discovering “Gravity’s Rainbow”: “It was like getting the flu to read that book. It was like I was fighting off some very aggressive infection.” He wrote to his then-fiancée: “I’ll get over Pynchon somehow. Maybe through criticism, some discontinuity, some dialectic, that will enable me to forget him.” His future wife proved a stronger motivation than any dialectic. About his “Pynchonian” love letters, Franzen told The Paris Review, “I think it’s significant that she hated those letters and made her hatred of them known, and that I steered away from that voice.”', 'print_section': 'BR', 'print_page': '6', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/09/01/books/review/0901-Openbook/0901-Openbook-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 205, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2013/09/01/books/review/0901-Openbook/0901-Openbook-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 205}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/09/01/books/review/0901-Openbook/0901-Openbook-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 350, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/09/01/books/review/0901-Openbook/0901-Openbook-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 359, 'width': 1049, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/09/01/books/review/0901-Openbook/0901-Openbook-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2013/09/01/books/review/0901-Openbook/0901-Openbook-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/09/01/books/review/0901-Openbook/0901-Openbook-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Pynchon’s Gravitational Pull', 'kicker': 'Open Book', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Pynchon’s Gravitational Pull', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Books and Literature', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2013-08-30T14:22:42+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'BookReview', 'section_name': 'Books', 'subsection_name': 'Book Review', 'byline': {'original': 'By John Williams', 'person': [{'firstname': 'John', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Williams', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/330fe54f-0c95-5a7f-92a7-2d792672f35a', 'word_count': 390, 'uri': 'nyt://article/330fe54f-0c95-5a7f-92a7-2d792672f35a'}, {'abstract': 'A landmark decision reignited the death penalty debate.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2013/09/01/books/review/a-wild-justice-by-evan-j-mandery.html', 'snippet': 'A landmark decision reignited the death penalty debate.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Furman v. Georgia is among the oddest Supreme Court cases in American history. Decided in 1972, it struck down every death penalty statute in the nation as then practiced without outlawing the death penalty itself. The ruling, based on the constitutional protection against “cruel and unusual punishment,” stunned even the closest court watchers. The death penalty seemed impregnable. It was part of the bedrock of America’s legal system, steeped in the intent of the founders, the will of most state legislatures and the forceful — if occasional — rulings of the courts.', 'print_section': 'BR', 'print_page': '12', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/09/01/books/review/0901-Oshinsky-SUB/0901-Oshinsky-SUB-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 656, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2013/09/01/books/review/0901-Oshinsky-SUB/0901-Oshinsky-S