le': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Review', '_id': 'nyt://article/f2668e19-6e97-55c6-be05-baac03871cfa', 'word_count': 1275, 'uri': 'nyt://article/f2668e19-6e97-55c6-be05-baac03871cfa'}, {'abstract': 'Paperback books of particular interest.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2013/09/01/books/review/paperback-row.html', 'snippet': 'Paperback books of particular interest.', 'lead_paragraph': 'INSIDE SCIENTOLOGY: The Story of America’s Most Secretive Religion,  by Janet Reitman. (Mariner/Houghton Mifflin Harcourt, $15.95.) This is a meticulous history of the Church of Scientology, from its birth as a 1950s self-help system in the science fiction imagination of L. Ron Hubbard (1911-86) to its current status as a worldwide movement. Reitman, who spent five years piercing the walls Scientologists put up against outsiders, has rendered what our reviewer, Garry Wills, called “the most complete picture of Scientology so far.”', 'print_section': 'BR', 'print_page': '28', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Paperback Row', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Paperback Row', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Books and Literature', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2013-08-30T14:23:28+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'BookReview', 'section_name': 'Books', 'subsection_name': 'Book Review', 'byline': {'original': 'By Ihsan Taylor', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Ihsan', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Taylor', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'List', '_id': 'nyt://article/618868c1-42ee-5ded-8c46-838fe4141b50', 'word_count': 503, 'uri': 'nyt://article/618868c1-42ee-5ded-8c46-838fe4141b50'}, {'abstract': 'Recently reviewed books of particular interest.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2013/09/01/books/review/editors-choice.html', 'snippet': 'Recently reviewed books of particular interest.', 'lead_paragraph': 'THE SMARTEST KIDS IN THE WORLD: And How They Got That Way,  by Amanda Ripley. (Simon & Schuster, $28.) A look at countries that are outeducating us (Finland, South Korea, Poland) through the eyes of American high school students abroad.', 'print_section': 'BR', 'print_page': '26', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Editors’ Choice', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Editors’ Choice | Recent books of particular interest', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Books and Literature', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2013-08-30T14:23:37+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'BookReview', 'section_name': 'Books', 'subsection_name': 'Book Review', 'byline': {'original': '', 'person': [], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'List', '_id': 'nyt://article/cdc37644-76af-5aba-bd03-0563eb0f41f3', 'word_count': 283, 'uri': 'nyt://article/cdc37644-76af-5aba-bd03-0563eb0f41f3'}, {'abstract': 'Readers respond to a recent essay about Joyce Kilmer’s “Trees.”', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2013/09/01/books/review/arbor-day.html', 'snippet': 'Readers respond to a recent essay about Joyce Kilmer’s “Trees.”', 'lead_paragraph': 'To the Editor:', 'print_section': 'BR', 'print_page': '7', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Arbor Day', 'kicker': 'Letters', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Arbor Day', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Poetry and Poets', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Books and Literature', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2013-08-30T14:23:56+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'BookReview', 'section_name': 'Books', 'subsection_name': 'Book Review', 'byline': {'original': '', 'person': [], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Letter', '_id': 'nyt://article/baa01ee7-04a3-5abd-80b1-85076d4b1c3a', 'word_count': 878, 'uri': 'nyt://article/baa01ee7-04a3-5abd-80b1-85076d4b1c3a'}, {'abstract': 'Wilton Barnhardt’s humorous novel concerns a family and a region coming apart.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2013/09/01/books/review/wilton-barnhardts-lookaway-lookaway.html', 'snippet': 'Wilton Barnhardt’s humorous novel concerns a family and a region coming apart.', 'lead_paragraph': '“Tell about the South,” his Harvard classmates beseech Quentin Compson in “Absalom, Absalom!” You’ll notice he doesn’t have to be asked twice. Sensitive Southerners — and Southern writers most of all — seem to love nothing better than explaining themselves and their region to outsiders (or to themselves, if no outsider is handy). There are exceptions, though, and Wilton Barnhardt would stand at the head of any list: though born and reared in the South, he has, over the last two decades, produced three well-regarded novels that almost never stray south of the Mason-Dixon line. There ought to be a prize for such forbearance. Or maybe he was just biding time, stockpiling ammunition. Because at long last he has tackled the South head on. Lacerating but affectionate, as exuberant as it is shrewd, “Lookaway, Lookaway” is a Southern novel so sure-footed the only real question for Barnhardt is, “What took you so long?”', 'print_section': 'BR', 'print_page': '9', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/09/01/books/review/0901-bks-JOnes/0901-bks-JOnes-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 382, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2013/09/01/books/review/0901-bks-JOnes/0901-bks-JOnes-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 382}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/09/01/books/review/0901-bks-JOnes/0901-bks-JOnes-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 651, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/09/01/books/review/0901-bks-JOnes/0901-bks-JOnes-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 1303, 'width': 2048, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/09/01/books/review/0901-bks-JOnes/0901-bks-JOnes-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2013/09/01/books/review/0901-bks-JOnes/0901-bks-JOnes-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/09/01/books/review/0901-bks-JOnes/0901-bks-JOnes-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Southern Discomfort', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Southern Discomfort', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Books and Literature', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Barnhardt, Wilton', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2013-08-30T14:24:47+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'BookReview', 'section_name': 'Books', 'subsection_name': 'Book Review', 'byline': {'original': 'By Malcolm Jones', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Malcolm', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Jones', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Review', '_id': 'nyt://article/04c0dcbc-4303-5500-8ebf-52e60dafb878', 'word_count': 950, 'uri': 'nyt://article/04c0dcbc-4303-5500-8ebf-52e60dafb878'}, {'abstract': 'A memoir looks back on the life of the fifth and final Mrs. Getty.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2013/09/01/books/review/alone-together-by-teddy-getty-gaston.html', 'snippet': 'A memoir looks back on the life of the fifth and final Mrs. Getty.', 'lead_paragraph': 'I’ve often wondered what it takes to be the wife of a captain of industry. What combination of boundless optimism and self-delusion is necessary to live with someone whose real lover is his work, who cannot turn off his competitiveness when he walks through the door, whose wealth means his opportunities and temptations with women are somewhere between “a lot” and “infinite,” and whose children and wife exist as a beautiful backdrop to what he considers his real accomplishments?', 'print_section': 'BR', 'print_page': '10', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/09/01/books/review/01NEWMAN/01NEWMAN-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 792, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2013/09/01/books/review/01NEWMAN/01NEWMAN-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 792}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/09/01/books/review/01NEWMAN/01NEWMAN-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 1024, 'width': 777, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/09/01/books/review/01NEWMAN/01NEWMAN-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 2048, 'width': 1553, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/09/01/books/review/01NEWMAN/01NEWMAN-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2013/09/01/books/review/01NEWMAN/01NEWMAN-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/09/01/books/review/01NEWMAN/01NEWMAN-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'His Favorite Wife', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'His Favorite Wife', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Newman, Judith', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Getty, J Paul', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Diehl, Digby', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Books and Literature', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Gaston, Teddy Getty', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2013-08-30T14:25:07+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'BookReview', 'section_name': 'Books', 'subsection_name': 'Book Review', 'byline': {'original': 'By Judith Newman', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Judith', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Newman', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Review', '_id': 'nyt://article/11f39977-8ec3-54ab-bfe2-016ae2a9ed15', 'word_count': 1137, 'uri': 'nyt://article/11f39977-8ec3-54ab-bfe2-016ae2a9ed15'}, {'abstract': 'A Haitian girl vanishes in Edwidge Danticat’s novel.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2013/09/01/books/review/claire-of-the-sea-light-by-edwidge-danticat.html', 'snippet': 'A Haitian girl vanishes in Edwidge Danticat’s novel.', 'lead_paragraph': 'At first, I resisted what appeared to be the fablelike delicacy of \xadEdwidge Danticat’s new novel, “Claire of the Sea Light.” Was it going to be too precious? Would her lyricism camouflage or ennoble Haiti’s life-or-death struggles? But it quickly became apparent that her hypnotic prose was perfectly suited to its setting, the tragic and yet magical seaside town of Ville Rose.', 'print_section': 'BR', 'print_page': '11', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/09/01/books/review/01SONTAG/01SONTAG-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 907, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2013/09/01/books/review/01SONTAG/01SONTAG-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 907}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/09/01/books/review/01SONTAG/01SONTAG-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 1024, 'width': 678, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/09/01/books/review/01SONTAG/01SONTAG-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 2048, 'width': 1355, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/09/01/books/review/01SONTAG/01SONTAG-thumbStandard-v2.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2013/09/01/books/review/01SONTAG/01SONTAG-thumbStandard-v2.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/09/01/books/review/01SONTAG/01SONTAG-thumbLarge-v2.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Island Magic', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Island Magic', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Sontag, Deborah', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Danticat, Edwidge', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Books and Literature', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2013-08-30T14:25:21+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'BookReview', 'section_name': 'Books', 'subsection_name': 'Book Review', 'byline': {'original': 'By Deborah Sontag', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Deborah', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Sontag', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Review', '_id': 'nyt://article/41275fcd-9de6-5df4-ad13-1b0fe09198ca', 'word_count': 636, 'uri': 'nyt://article/41275fcd-9de6-5df4-ad13-1b0fe09198ca'}, {'abstract': 'The first college Meh List; Mario Batali’s Labor Day drink recipe; how Eric LeGrand stayed positive after paralysis.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/interactive/2013/09/01/magazine/01-one-page-magazine.html', 'snippet': 'The first college Meh List; Mario Batali’s Labor Day drink recipe; how Eric LeGrand stayed positive after paralysis.', 'lead_paragraph': 'The first college Meh List; Mario Batali’s Labor Day drink recipe; how Eric LeGrand stayed positive after paralysis.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/09/01/magazine/01OPM-thumb/01OPM-thumb-jumbo.png', 'height': 225, 'width': 368, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/09/01/magazine/01OPM-thumb/01OPM-thumb-superJumbo.png', 'height': 225, 'width': 368, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/09/01/magazine/01OPM-thumb/01OPM-thumb-thumbStandard.png', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2013/09/01/magazine/01OPM-thumb/01OPM-thumb-thumbStandard.png', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Will the Mile-High Club Shut Down?', 'kicker': 'The One-Page Magazine', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [], 'pub_date': '2013-08-30T14:25:24+0000', 'document_type': 'multimedia', 'news_desk': 'Magazine', 'section_name': 'Magazine', 'byline': {'original': '', 'person': [], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Interactive Feature', '_id': 'nyt://interactive/7141960f-62ea-5e5b-a0fe-c21b47e959c5', 'word_count': 0, 'uri': 'nyt://interactive/7141960f-62ea-5e5b-a0fe-c21b47e959c5'}, {'abstract': 'There is, however, an ethical argument.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2013/09/01/magazine/there-are-no-sound-moral-arguments-against-performance-enhancing-drugs.html', 'snippet': 'There is, however, an ethical argument.', 'lead_paragraph': ' The argument against performance-enhancing drugs in sports is that the drugs give players an unfair advantage. But how do P.E.D.’s differ from Tommy John surgery? Or pre-emptive Tommy John surgery? What about rich kids? Is their access to superior coaching, facilities and equipment a similarly unfair advantage? In a society that embraces plastic surgery, Botox injections, Viagra and all kinds of enhancements, what moral line do P.E.D.’s cross? LYNN MOFFAT, SLEEPY HOLLOW, N.Y.', 'print_section': 'MM', 'print_page': '14', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/02/02/magazine/ethicist/ethicist-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 1316, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2014/02/02/magazine/ethicist/ethicist-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 1316}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/02/02/magazine/ethicist/ethicist-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 1024, 'width': 467, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/02/02/magazine/ethicist/ethicist-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 2048, 'width': 934, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/02/02/magazine/ethicist/ethicist-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2014/02/02/magazine/ethicist/ethicist-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/02/02/magazine/ethicist/ethicist-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'There Are No Sound Moral Arguments Against Performance-Enhancing Drugs', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Rules of Enhancement', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Drugs (Pharmaceuticals)', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Klosterman, Chuck', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Doping (Sports)', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Ethicist, The (Times Column)', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2013-08-30T14:25:27+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Magazine', 'section_name': 'Magazine', 'byline': {'original': 'By Chuck Klosterman', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Chuck', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Klosterman', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Question', '_id': 'nyt://article/13f2c640-4151-5f65-8734-40c5234edc3c', 'word_count': 512, 'uri': 'nyt://article/13f2c640-4151-5f65-8734-40c5234edc3c'}, {'abstract': 'For the self-identified cineaste, new parenthood doesn’t mean just seeing a lot fewer movies; it means no longer trusting your judgment about the rare movies you do get to see.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2013/09/01/magazine/two-sucked-thumbs-up.html', 'snippet': 'For the self-identified cineaste, new parenthood doesn’t mean just seeing a lot fewer movies; it means no longer trusting your judgment about the rare movies you do get to see.', 'lead_paragraph': 'A year ago, on the day my wife, Liz, would give birth to our first child, we sat down to watch “The Avengers.” She’d already seen it, and it wasn’t something I was particularly dying to see — superhero movies aren’t usually my thing. My cinematic taste generally tends to the somewhat obscure and the challenging — I’d much rather rewatch anything by Rainer Werner Fassbinder than sit through any of the “Fast and Furious” sequels. But Liz had started to feel the fluttering of early labor, and our birth-class instructor had advised us that this particular stage could last for several hours. The best thing to do, she said, was find a distraction: go for a walk, play cards or watch a dumb movie. As it turned out, there wasn’t going to be a need for any distractions. Within minutes, that fluttering became what Liz described, with some understatement, as cramps. What followed was such an incredibly compressed labor that there’s actually a special name for it: precipitous labor, which happens less than 3 percent of the time. Our son, Jack, was born an hour and a half later, at home, in our bed. I still haven’t seen “The Avengers.”', 'print_section': 'MM', 'print_page': '44', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/09/01/magazine/01riff/mag-01Riff-t_CA0-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 89, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2013/09/01/magazine/01riff/mag-01Riff-t_CA0-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 89}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/09/01/magazine/01riff/mag-01Riff-t_CA0-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 151, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/09/01/magazine/01riff/mag-01Riff-t_CA0-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 303, 'width': 2048, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/09/01/magazine/01riff/mag-01Riff-t_CA0-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2013/09/01/magazine/01riff/mag-01Riff-t_CA0-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/09/01/magazine/01riff/mag-01Riff-t_CA0-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Two (Sucked) Thumbs Up', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Two (Sucked) Thumbs Up', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Movies', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'McBride, Jason', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Parenting', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2013-08-30T14:25:27+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Magazine', 'section_name': 'Magazine', 'byline': {'original': 'By Jason McBride', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Jason', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'McBride', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/fad658c3-456b-591b-a041-deec2cac9f4c', 'word_count': 1830, 'uri': 'nyt://article/fad658c3-456b-591b-a041-deec2cac9f4c'}, {'abstract': 'The latest in high-tech eyewear.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2013/09/01/magazine/googling-yourself-takes-on-a-whole-new-meaning.html', 'snippet': 'The latest in high-tech eyewear.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Here’s what you see if you look at my face: a skinny titanium headband stretched across my forehead. It looks like a futuristic pair of sunglasses, minus the lenses. On my right-hand side there’s a computer, a metal frame with a small, clear cube of plastic perched just over my eye. When I tilt my head upward a bit, or run my finger along the side of the frame, the cube lights up. What I see, floating six inches in front of me, is a pinkish, translucent computer screen. It gives me access to a few simple apps: Google search, text messaging, Twitter, a to-do list, some hourly news headlines from CNN (“See a Truck Go Airborne, Fly Over Median,” “Dolphin Deaths Alarm Biologists”). Beside the screen is a teensy camera built into the frame of the glasses, ready to record anything I’m looking at.', 'print_section': 'MM', 'print_page': '32', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/09/01/magazine/01computers1/mag-01Computers-t_CA0-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 401, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2013/09/01/magazine/01computers1/mag-01Computers-t_CA0-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 401}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/09/01/magazine/01computers1/mag-01Computers-t_CA0-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 684, 'width': 1024, 'subType':