for the U.S. military. John L. Barker Sr. — then an engineer at Automatic Signal Company — had been working on traffic lights in the 1930s, but during World War II, he dedicated himself to military research. “He pointed the machine out a window, and he tested it on cars that drove through an intersection.” Barker was about 8 years old at the time and admits that his memories are hazy. “I have no idea why I was even involved,” he says.', 'print_section': 'MM', 'print_page': '17', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/09/01/magazine/01wmt/01wmt-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 600, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2013/09/01/magazine/01wmt/01wmt-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 600}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/09/01/magazine/01wmt/01wmt-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 1024, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/09/01/magazine/01wmt/01wmt-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 2047, 'width': 2048, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/09/01/magazine/01wmt/01wmt-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2013/09/01/magazine/01wmt/01wmt-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/09/01/magazine/01wmt/01wmt-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Who Made That Traffic Radar?', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Who Made That? (Traffic Radar)', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Radar', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Kennedy, Pagan', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Roads and Traffic', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Inventions and Patents', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Barker, John L', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2013-08-30T14:25:31+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Magazine', 'section_name': 'Magazine', 'byline': {'original': 'By Pagan Kennedy', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Pagan', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Kennedy', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/9427ce00-972a-5995-8d32-f5755cb748d6', 'word_count': 720, 'uri': 'nyt://article/9427ce00-972a-5995-8d32-f5755cb748d6'}, {'abstract': 'The mayor says that Boston has changed — but he hasn’t had to.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2013/09/01/magazine/thomas-menino-is-a-cranberry-juice-drinking-workhorse.html', 'snippet': 'The mayor says that Boston has changed — but he hasn’t had to.', 'lead_paragraph': ' There’s an old maxim that politicians are either show horses or workhorses. After 20 years as mayor of Boston, you’ve won a reputation as a workhorse. What do you think the show horses can learn from the workhorses?  Well, a mayor has to be a workhorse. You can’t be a show horse, because people depend on you. We see our constituents every day. You go buy a newspaper, go to a coffee shop, buy gas for our car — you see them.', 'print_section': 'MM', 'print_page': '12', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/09/01/magazine/01talk/ma