{}}], 'headline': {'main': 'A Brief Vacation From Myself', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'A Brief Vacation From Myself', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'AMNESIA', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Fields-Meyer, Tom', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Hospitals', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Memory', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2013-08-30T14:25:32+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Magazine', 'section_name': 'Magazine', 'byline': {'original': 'By Tom Fields-Meyer', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Tom', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Fields-Meyer', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/ce18c8a3-37a0-5fe8-8622-7903a78af2dd', 'word_count': 767, 'uri': 'nyt://article/ce18c8a3-37a0-5fe8-8622-7903a78af2dd'}, {'abstract': 'A story collection encompasses the mythic and the everyday.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2013/09/01/books/review/the-color-master-by-aimee-bender.html', 'snippet': 'A story collection encompasses the mythic and the everyday.', 'lead_paragraph': 'In Aimee Bender’s short stories, the value of life is measured in terms of goodness, succulence and simplicity, all qualities that can be tasted, chewed and ultimately swallowed by the mouth or the mind. In “Appleless” (one of this new book’s many parable-like fictions), the nameless narrator distrusts a girl who doesn’t appreciate the bright-hued, hand-clutched density and watery meat of apples — and the narrator goes on, with the help of others, to devour every last apple in sight. The overachieving seamstress of “Tiger Mending” isn’t satisfied with simply stitching up cut lips and handkerchiefs; she eventually takes a job in a faraway country sewing the stripes back on ruptured tigers (who apparently yawn too widely for their own good). And in one of Bender’s best stories, “The Red Ribbon,” a bored wife can’t enjoy sex with her husband unless he pays her. She likes to keep track of their accumulating ecstasies in a ledger: “ ‘I need a specific amount, each time,’ she said, ‘or,’ clearing her throat, ‘I feel I will melt into nothingness.’ ” ', 'print_section': 'BR', 'print_page': '11', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/09/01/books/review/01BRADFEILD/01BRADFEILD-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 1062, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2013/09/01/books/review/01BRADFEILD/01BRADFEILD-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 1062}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/09/01/books/review/01BRADFEILD/01BRADFEILD-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 1024, 'width': 579, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/09/01/books/review/01BRADFEILD/01BRADFEILD-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 1062, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/09/01/books/review/01BRADFEILD/01BRADFEILD-thumbStandard-v2.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2013/09/01/books/review/01BRADFEILD/01BRADFEILD-thumbStandard-v2.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/09/01/books/review/01BRADFEILD/01BRADFEILD-thumbLarge-v2.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline'