 New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/09/01/books/review/01LAWRENCE/01LAWRENCE-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 373, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2013/09/01/books/review/01LAWRENCE/01LAWRENCE-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 373}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/09/01/books/review/01LAWRENCE/01LAWRENCE-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 636, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/09/01/books/review/01LAWRENCE/01LAWRENCE-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 1272, 'width': 2048, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/09/01/books/review/01LAWRENCE/01LAWRENCE-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2013/09/01/books/review/01LAWRENCE/01LAWRENCE-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/09/01/books/review/01LAWRENCE/01LAWRENCE-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Land of Mystery', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Land of Mystery', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Books and Literature', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'North Korea', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Lankov, Andrei', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Jager, Sheila Miyoshi', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2013-08-30T14:28:08+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'BookReview', 'section_name': 'Books', 'subsection_name': 'Book Review', 'byline': {'original': 'By Mark Atwood Lawrence', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Mark', 'middlename': 'Atwood', 'lastname': 'Lawrence', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Review', '_id': 'nyt://article/bd1c5093-a972-51db-a2cc-ddc1c8750cc3', 'word_count': 1132, 'uri': 'nyt://article/bd1c5093-a972-51db-a2cc-ddc1c8750cc3'}, {'abstract': '“How the Light Gets In,” Louise Penny’s latest mystery featuring the honorable detective Armand Gamache, takes place in a secluded Canadian hamlet.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2013/09/01/books/review/louise-pennys-how-the-light-gets-in-and-more.html', 'snippet': '“How the Light Gets In,” Louise Penny’s latest mystery featuring the honorable detective Armand Gamache, takes place in a secluded Canadian hamlet.', 'lead_paragraph': 'A four-day blizzard has left the village of Three Pines looking like a toy town sheltered under a snow globe. But this secluded Canadian hamlet, tucked away in a valley deep in the mountains, contributes more than its picturesque setting to Louise Penny’s latest mystery, HOW THE LIGHT GETS IN (Minotaur, $25.99). You might even say Three Pines is the hero of the novel.', 'print_section': 'BR', 'print_page': '21', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/09/01/books/review/0901-Crime/0901-Crime-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 698, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2013/09/01/books/review/0901-Crime/0901-Crime-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 698}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'u