 {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Kraniak, Scott', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Long Island (NY)', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2013-08-30T19:03:25+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Metro', 'section_name': 'New York', 'byline': {'original': 'By Corey Kilgannon', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Corey', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Kilgannon', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/3395fe4a-1429-5378-a870-329d1a9e4251', 'word_count': 731, 'uri': 'nyt://article/3395fe4a-1429-5378-a870-329d1a9e4251'}, {'abstract': 'Sydney Llewellyn, who coached a young Arthur Ashe, may have taught more African-American tennis champions than any other coach.', 'web_url': 'https://6thfloor.blogs.nytimes.com/2013/08/30/tennis-in-harlem/', 'snippet': 'Sydney Llewellyn, who coached a young Arthur Ashe, may have taught more African-American tennis champions than any other coach.', 'lead_paragraph': 'At the U.S. Open this week and next, thousands of tennis fans have been and will be visiting Arthur Ashe Stadium at the tournament’s site in Queens. It’s not all that far from where Ashe spent some time in Harlem honing his skills with a tennis coach named Sydney Llewellyn. According to another former tennis student, Bob Davis, Llewellyn\xa0taught more African-American national champions than any other coach — among them Althea Gibson, to whom he was married for five years in the mid-’80s.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Tennis in Harlem', 'kicker': 'The 6th Floor', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Tennis', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'United States Open (Tennis)', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Ashe, Arthur', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Ashe, Arthur, Stadium', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Harlem (Manhattan, NY)', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2013-08-30T19:07:20+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Magazine', 'byline': {'original': 'By Stacey Baker', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Stacey', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Baker', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/aba3d370-e486-5561-aaaa-769930fc386c', 'word_count': 187, 'uri': 'nyt://article/aba3d370-e486-5561-aaaa-769930fc386c'}, {'abstract': 'The actor has new roles in “Sesame Street,” “The Good Wife,” and the new Regina Taylor play, “stop. reset.”', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2013/09/01/arts/television/a-chameleon-onstage-and-on-tv.html', 'snippet': 'The actor has new roles in “Sesame Street,” “The Good Wife,” and the new Regina Taylor play, “stop. reset.”', 'lead_paragraph': '“Sesame Street” was looking for a Latino guy; “stop. reset.” was looking for an African-American guy; “The Good Wife” was looking for a white guy. They all ended up with Ismael Cruz Córdova, who was born and raised in Aguas Buenas, P.R. ', 'print_section': 'AR', 'print_page': '3', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/09/01/arts/01SNAPSHOT/01SNAPSHOT-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 1212, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2013/09/01/arts/01SNAPSHOT/01SNAPSHOT-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 1212}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/09/01/arts/01SNAPSHOT/01SNAPSHOT-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 1024, 'width': 507, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJ