ts', 'subsection_name': 'Television', 'byline': {'original': 'By Neil Genzlinger', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Neil', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Genzlinger', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/bcd13385-e1d6-5172-8f07-05affe5a9429', 'word_count': 221, 'uri': 'nyt://article/bcd13385-e1d6-5172-8f07-05affe5a9429'}, {'abstract': 'More than ever, tension among forces of cosmopolitan democracy, religious traditionalism and militaristic order roil the Middle East.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2013/09/01/arts/design/identity-puzzles-from-us-and-iran.html', 'snippet': 'More than ever, tension among forces of cosmopolitan democracy, religious traditionalism and militaristic order roil the Middle East.', 'lead_paragraph': 'More than ever, tension among forces of cosmopolitan democracy, religious traditionalism and militaristic order roil the Middle East. So Asia Society’s “Iran Modern,” an exhibition of more than 100 artworks made in Iran in the two decades before the revolution of 1979, should be something to see. Modern art was starting to thrive then, as artists explored new possibilities of identity. It opens Friday. (Through Jan. 5, 725 Park Avenue, at 70th Street, asiasociety.org.)', 'print_section': 'AR', 'print_page': '2', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/09/01/arts/01GUIDE2/01GUIDE2-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 453, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2013/09/01/arts/01GUIDE2/01GUIDE2-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 453}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/09/01/arts/01GUIDE2/01GUIDE2-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 772, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/09/01/arts/01GUIDE2/01GUIDE2-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 1545, 'width': 2048, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/09/01/arts/01GUIDE2/01GUIDE2-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2013/09/01/arts/01GUIDE2/01GUIDE2-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/09/01/arts/01GUIDE2/01GUIDE2-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Identity Puzzles From U.S. and Iran', 'kicker': 'The Week Ahead | Art', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Identity Puzzles From U.S. and Iran', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Art', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Asia Society', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'ANTIN, ELEANOR', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Wallach, Miriam and Ira D, Art Gallery', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Morningside Heights (Manhattan, NY)', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2013-08-30T21:29:13+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Arts&Leisure', 'section_name': 'Arts', 'subsection_name': 'Art & Design', 'byline': {'original': 'By Ken Johnson', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Ken', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Johnson', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/92daaafe-7478-576a-a186-19b3d01e874b', 'word_count': 169, 'uri': 'nyt://article/92daaafe-7478-576a-a186-19b3d01e874b'}, {'abstract': 'Opening in time for Fashion Week, the curated boutique gathers artisanal, handmade products from the Brooklyn creative community and beyond.', 'web_url': 'https://tmagazine.blogs.nytimes.com/2013/08/30/in-store-the-wythe-hotels-new-concept-shop-is-a-family-affair/', 'snippet': 'Opening in time for Fashion Week, the curated boutique gathers artisanal, handmade products from the Brooklyn creative community and beyond.', 'lead_paragraph': 'To some, the Wythe Hotel in Williamsburg may represent the way Brooklyn is changing, but it’s sticking with the borough’s tradition of keeping it in the family. Opening next Tuesday is a new boutique known as the Shop, with a theme and offerings chosen by one local mastermind, which will change every four to six months. The first curator is the fashion designer Caron Callahan, whose label is based in the neighborhood. (Her husband, Sean Rembold, is the executive chef at the restaurant Marlow & Sons, owned by Andrew Tarlow, who is also the man behind the Wythe.) “They were looking for something that gave a little energy to that nook,” Callahan said. “There’s so much happening here, and the taste level is super-high.”', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'In Store | The Wythe Hotel’s New Concept Shop Is a Family Affair', 'kicker': 'T Magazine', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Fashion and Apparel', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Hotels and Travel Lodgings', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Wythe Hotel (Brooklyn, NY)', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Williamsburg (Brooklyn, NY)', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2013-08-30T21:30:56+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'T Magazine', 'byline': {'original': 'By Julie Baumgardner', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Julie', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Baumgardner', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/4b31e11b-3f08-539d-9610-ac170e2da378', 'word_count': 294, 'uri': 'nyt://article/4b31e11b-3f08-539d-9610-ac170e2da378'}, {'abstract': 'This week three ambitious artists come to the stylish Bleecker Street nightclub Le Poisson Rouge.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2013/09/01/arts/music/its-pre-season-at-poisson-rouge.html', 'snippet': 'This week three ambitious artists come to the stylish Bleecker Street nightclub Le Poisson Rouge.', 'lead_paragraph': 'The fall season is looming, but plenty of ambitious artists are not content to wait for any official starter’s pistol to sound, and this week three of them come to the stylish Bleecker Street nightclub Le Poisson Rouge. Two are coming together. On Tuesday, the excellent young pianists Ran Dank and Soyeon Kate Lee present an evening of works for piano duo, including the premiere of Frederic Rzewski’s “Four Hands,” as well as Brahms’s 16 Waltzes, Op. 39 and an arrangement of Stravinsky’s “Rite of Spring.”', 'print_section': 'AR', 'print_page': '2', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/09/01/arts/01GUIDE3/01GUIDE3-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 399, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2013/09/01/arts/01GUIDE3/01GUIDE3-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 399}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/09/01/arts/01GUIDE3/01GUIDE3-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 680, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 