: 'Bronx (NYC)', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Firearms', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Assaults', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2013-08-31T04:00:49+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Metro', 'section_name': 'New York', 'byline': {'original': 'By The New York Times', 'person': [], 'organization': 'The New York Times'}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/036ec9e4-0706-53c8-8cee-697ab5abc359', 'word_count': 160, 'uri': 'nyt://article/036ec9e4-0706-53c8-8cee-697ab5abc359'}, {'abstract': 'Instead of attacking a crucial second serve by Marcel Granollers in the fifth set, Rajeev Ram allowed Granollers to recover with a forehand approach, turning the match, ultimately, in Granollers’s favor.', 'web_url': 'https://straightsets.blogs.nytimes.com/2013/08/31/ram-swats-away-a-chance-to-reach-the-third-round/', 'snippet': 'Instead of attacking a crucial second serve by Marcel Granollers in the fifth set, Rajeev Ram allowed Granollers to recover with a forehand approach, turning the match, ultimately, in Granollers’s favor.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Rajeev Ram was two points away from his biggest Grand Slam victory, and it was time to make something happen.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Ram Swats Away a Chance to Reach the Third Round', 'kicker': 'Straight Sets', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Tennis', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Granollers, Marcel', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Ram, Rajeev', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2013-08-31T04:11:06+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Sports', 'subsection_name': 'Tennis', 'byline': {'original': "By Craig O'Shannessy", 'person': [{'firstname': 'Craig', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': "O'Shannessy", 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/81955c07-9e86-59e9-984d-42fe680a3b9a', 'word_count': 754, 'uri': 'nyt://article/81955c07-9e86-59e9-984d-42fe680a3b9a'}, {'abstract': 'Television highlights.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2013/08/31/arts/television/whats-on-saturday.html', 'snippet': 'Television highlights.', 'lead_paragraph': '8 P.M. (HBO) LES MISÉRABLES (2012) Hugh Jackman (above, who earned an Oscar nomination for his role in the film), Anne Hathaway (who won for best supporting actress), Russell Crowe, Amanda Seyfried and Eddie Redmayne sing their hearts out in this adaptation of the Victor Hugo novel about an inspector’s pursuit of a convict and the love between a young woman and a firebrand of the French Revolution. “The artful grunge layered onto the cast can be a distraction, as you imagine assistant dirt wranglers anxiously hovering off camera,” Manohla Dargis wrote in The New York Times. Ms. Hathaway, though, as Fantine, “the factory worker turned prostitute turned martyr, and singing the showstopping ‘I Dreamed a Dream,’ her gaunt face splotched red and brown,” she wrote, “holds you rapt with raw, trembling emotion.” But while the director, Tom Hooper, demonstrated in “The King’s Speech” that he can be very good with actors, his “inability to leave any lily ungilded — to direct a scene without tilting or hurtling or throwing the camera around — is bludgeoning and deadly,” she added. “By the grand finale, when tout le monde is waving the French tricolor in victory, you may instead be raising the white flag in exhausted defeat.”', 'print_section': 'C', 'print_page': '6', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'What’s on Saturday', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'What’s On Saturday', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [], 'pub_date': '2013-08-31T05:00:08+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Culture', 'section_name': 'Arts', 'subsection_name': 'Television', 'byline': {'original': 'By Kathryn Shattuck', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Kathryn', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Shattuck', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Schedule', '_id': 'nyt://article/91546387-fe90-5670-9f3d-92420efd9780', 'word_count': 743, 'uri': 'nyt://article/91546387-fe90-5670-9f3d-92420efd9780'}, {'abstract': 'In an unusual step, federal officials also warned agencies and companies of possible cyberattacks if the American military takes action against Syria.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2013/09/01/us/fbi-sharpens-scrutiny-of-syrians-in-us-for-signs-of-retaliation.html', 'snippet': 'In an unusual step, federal officials also warned agencies and companies of possible cyberattacks if the American military takes action against Syria.', 'lead_paragraph': 'The F.B.I. has increased its surveillance of Syrians inside the United States in response to concerns that a military strike against the government of President Bashar al-Assad could lead to terrorist attacks here or against American allies and interests abroad, according to current and former senior United States officials.', 'print_section': 'A', 'print_page': '10', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'F.B.I. Sharpens Scrutiny of Syrians in U.S.', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'F.B.I. Sharpens Scrutiny of Syrians in U.S.', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Terrorism', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Schmidt, Michael S', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Federal Bureau of Investigation', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Cyberwarfare', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Syria', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Surveillance of Citizens by Government', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Middle East and North Africa Unrest (2010- )', 'rank': 7, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Iran', 'rank': 8, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'United States Defense and Military Forces', 'rank': 9, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2013-08-31T05:18:53+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Foreign', 'section_name': 'U.S.', 'byline': {'original': 'By Michael S. Schmidt', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Michael', 'middlename': 'S.', 'lastname': 'Schmidt', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/a19f482e-bea2-544c-a133-7fa68fd5dbcc', 'word_count': 758, 'uri': 'nyt://article/a19f482e-bea2-544c-a133-7fa68fd5dbcc'}, {'abstract': 'President Obama abruptly changed course by asking lawmakers, who are not due to return to town for more than a week, to give him authorization before he launches a limited military strike against Syria.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2013/09/01/world/middleeast/syria.html', 'snippet': 'President Obama abruptly changed course by asking lawmakers, who are not due to return to town for more than a week, to give him authorization before he launches a limited military strike against Syria.', 'lead_paragraph': 'WASHINGTON — President Obama abruptly changed course on Saturday and postponed a military strike against the Syrian government in retaliation for a chemical weapons attack so he could seek authorization first from a deeply skeptical Congress.', 'print_section': 'A', 'print_page': '1', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/09/01/us/sub-prexy/sub-prexy-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 526, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legac