llowed.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2013/08/31/opinion/what-happened-to-the-rule-of-law.html', 'snippet': 'The United Nations Charter has been violated more than times than it has been followed.', 'lead_paragraph': 'SINCE the United Nations was created in 1945, its Charter has been more honored in the breach than the observance. So maybe it should not surprise us that President Obama seems poised to authorize American military action against Syria, in clear violation of international law. ', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'What Happened to the Rule of Law?', 'kicker': 'Op-Ed Contributor', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'What Happened to the Rule of Law?', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'United Nations', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Security Council (UN)', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Syria', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Defense and Military Forces', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'International Relations', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2013-08-31T15:15:25+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'OpEd', 'section_name': 'Opinion', 'byline': {'original': 'By Jack L. Goldsmith', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Jack', 'middlename': 'L.', 'lastname': 'Goldsmith', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Op-Ed', '_id': 'nyt://article/7587ab8c-4dfe-5617-8ec3-9540b84c4fe5', 'word_count': 961, 'uri': 'nyt://article/7587ab8c-4dfe-5617-8ec3-9540b84c4fe5'}, {'abstract': 'Economic policy in 2009-2010: Success or failure?', 'web_url': 'https://krugman.blogs.nytimes.com/2013/08/31/bankers-workers-obama-and-summers/', 'snippet': 'Economic policy in 2009-2010: Success or failure?', 'lead_paragraph': 'Brad DeLong has an excellent piece distinguishing between two views of central banking. There’s the “banking camp,” which sees the central bank’s job as being to secure the stability of the financial system – full stop. OK, maybe also price stability. And then there’s the “macroeconomics camp,” which sees the central bank’s job as being to achieve full employment; banking stability and even price stability are basically means towards that end.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Bankers, Workers, Obama and Summers', 'kicker': 'Paul Krugman', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [], 'pub_date': '2013-08-31T15:50:39+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Opinion', 'byline': {'original': 'By Paul Krugman', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Paul', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Krugman', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/1c50343b-7a2e-53e5-b5f7-82987da9be47', 'word_count': 513, 'uri': 'nyt://article/1c50343b-7a2e-53e5-b5f7-82987da9be47'}, {'abstract': 'Last October, Max Shatto was adopted from an orphanage in northwest Russia. Three months later, he was dead.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/video/us/100000002326294/death-of-a-russian-orphan.html', 'snippet': 'Last October, Max Shatto was adopted from an orphanage in northwest Russia. Three months later, he was dead.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Last October, Max Shatto was adopted from an orphanage in northwest Russia. Three months later, he was dead.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/07/13/us/video-russia-shatto-adopt2/video-russia-shatto-adopt2-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 338, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2013/07/13/us/video-russia-shatto-adopt2/vid